2.2.5.1. GAS POROSITY
Gas porosity is categorized as a spherical porosity type and is caused by trapped vapor in the melt pool that is unable to escape before the molten melt pool solidifies. Trace amounts of gas or water may be left in the feedstock powder. When the powder is melted, the trace amounts of gas or water are also heated and expand as a result. The expanding vapor puts pressure on the surrounding metal, leaving a telltale mirror shine on the void’s internal surface. Figure 2.23 captures gas porosity in AlSi10Mg that occurred when trace H2O reacted with Al to form H2 bubbles that then became trapped in the melt pool [52]. In some alloys, gas porosity can be attributed to the vaporization of lowtemperature melt elements [70, 71].
