2.2.6. LASER FOCUS DIAMETER (SPOT SIZE) AND VARIOUS MACHINES
The L-PBF laser focus diameter, or spot size, varies based on the laser scanner used but typically ranges from 40 to 80 mm depending on the machine. Some variation in spot size occurs with changes in laser power (i.e., an increase in laser power corresponds to a minor increase in spot size). However, unlike in DED, laser focus diameter in most L-PBF platforms is a fixed variable, although there are development efforts to allow for variable focus shape and size. Some systems are programed to control spot size on the build plane by raising or lowering the build platform itself, whereas others actively control the spot size in the optics train. Spot size control using these mechanisms is somewhat debated. It is generally recommended that spot size be kept constant, as adjustments can introduce significant process instability that is not sufficiently offset by any conferred benefits. Machines with finer spot sizes are able to print parts with finer feature resolution. This is particularly advantageous when it comes to printing lattice structures or other fine details. It is worth bearing in mind that the benefits conferred by smaller laser spot sizes come at the cost of increased build times. This cost–benefit trade-off is evident in the general correlation between the build
