2.2.7. RESIDUAL STRESSES AND DISTORTION
AM processes that melt the metal feedstock to produce a part, such as L-PBF, involve residual thermal stresses that can distort the part. The contractive forces that result from thermal gradients (and resulting thermal stresses) can become very strong depending on the part geometry; therefore, it is beneficial to develop an understanding of how these stresses manifest and how to mitigate them. Figures 2.26–2.28 illustrate an unsupported, overhanging part in a hypothetical build scenario and are intended to demonstrate the fundamental factors that lead to part curling. As the laser travels into the unsupported overhang, heat buildup becomes more significant (Fig. 2.26). Convective cooling capacity across the part remains constant. However, the conductive capability is diminished as the unmelted powder under the overhang acts as a thermal insulator; this effect is illustrated in Fig. 2.27 [58]. This uneven cooling results in increased contractive forces closer to the overhang, visible in Fig.2.28, which can, in turn, result in part curling and distortion. The part build orientation should be properly evaluated to avoid potential curling and recoater blade collisions. Support structures are used to counteract the forces that result from thermal contraction. Generally speaking, geometries with overhangs greater than 458 are not considered printable without supports, although some metals are more
