2.2.8. BUILD FAILURES
A majority of build failures in L-PBF occur as a result of thermally induced residual stresses; this is important to keep in mind when debugging a faulty build. Additionally, the majority of critical, build-terminating failures are rarely single isolated faults; rather, they are typically the end results of several cascading failures, culminating in a failed or “crashed” build. Specific types of build failures and defects are demonstrated in this section. Understanding material failure modes and being able to identify the signs of a failure cascade are critical knowledge sets for the AM practitioner to develop. They will both help to fix the part before it is too far gone and avoid similar crashes in the future. Additionally, this section provides insight into the identification of potential failures before they evolve into a full (and costly) scrapped build.
2.2.8.1. RESIDUAL STRESS AND DISTORTION
In L-PBF, it is generally recommended that part volume and surface area of the part contacting the build plate be minimized whenever possible. This not only results in lighter, cheaper parts but also reduces the risk of residual stress induced failures such as distortion or cracks. The crack seen in Fig. 2.31 likely occurred as a result of building thermal gradients across the large part cross section. Once removed from the build chamber, the part was allowed to sit
