Later in the build, the powder in the feedstock reservoir becomes more compacted as a result of regular platform movement, and it thus has fewer voids. For this reason, it is highly recommended that the feedstock be densified after the feedstock reservoir is filled by removing residual trapped air (i.e., plunging a metal spatula into the powder or vibrate) to encourage sufficient powder compaction. Likewise, it is recommended that the first several layers of a build be monitored for the emergence of the characteristics of powder short-feeding. If a short feed extends over a part and correction occurs after more than approximately three to five layers have been deposited, the subsequent layer of powder will be excessively thick, and the melt pool will not connect to the part below. This will cause the most recent layer (and the next several) to curl. The effects of this distortion can be seen at the base of the part in Figure 2.33, where short-feeding began to occur. The error was corrected by the machine operator, and the part began to recover, leaving behind a “burned” downward-facing surface that did not fully connect to the plate below.
2.2.8.3. RECOATER BLADE DAMAGE
Recoater blade damage can occur when the part being built distorts as a result of thermal stresses, powder short-feeding, or some other debris crossing paths with the recoater blade. Recoater damage is not always severe enough to halt a build. If left unchecked, however, it can result in a cascade of failures that can end in a crashed build. Figure 2.34 shows the result of an uncorrected short feed that grew until it met the part, eventually causing part curling that intersected the recoater blade [52]. The part then cut into the recoater blade (which, in this case, was rubber), permanently damaging it. This resulted in the deposition of excessively thick powder layers, causing further distortion and ultimately build failure.
