Build restarts are a common occurrence in L-PBF and have several causes, from argon outage to mid-build powder refills or short-feeding that requires manual correction. These build restarts often leave restart lines in the parts being built. “Witness marks” or “restart lines” are visible horizontal lines across the surface of AM parts that result from a modification in deposition and scanning time, layer thickness, or other variations in the build process [74]. Witness marks are most commonly a result of build pauses due to the planned addition of powder, particularly for tall components. Unexpected reasons for build pauses can be attributed to machine interrupts or facility power outages. During the build process, the part is at elevated temperature but cools during the build pause, causing the part to experience minor shrinkage. After restart, the machine continues the scan strategy with the assumption that the part is at the heated geometric size (meaning that the first few layers appear slightly oversized until the part reaches thermal equilibrium and the part and scan geometries again match). The resulting geometric mismatch produces a witness mark across the entire part at the pause build height [75]. Examples of witness marks, or witness lines, are shown in Fig. 2.35.
