2.2.8.4. STRAY VECTORS
Stray vectors in a part occur when a build file has been corrupted. The level of severity of build-file corruption varies, but the result is the same: The laser traces out and melts areas that were not identified in the original model. Figure 2.36 demonstrates two instances of stray vectors occurring as a result of build-file corruption. Often, the only solution is to scrap the build, reprocess the build files, and start over. Build files can become corrupted when a machine without an alternative power supply for the control computer experiences an unscheduled power interruption.
2.2.9. RECYCLING POWDER
After each build, the powder surrounding the parts must be removed and sieved to separate jagged, oblong, and otherwise oversized powder from the remaining spherical particles. If these irregular particles are left in the powder bed, LOF defects can appear in the resulting parts. This phenomenon occurs for a few reasons. The first is that the larger, irregularly shaped particles reduce powder packing efficiency, as illustrated in Fig. 2.37, so that less material overall is melted by each laser pass. The second is that larger particles require more energy to melt. If this threshold is not met by the laser, these particles will remain solid and retain their original shapes. A third, slightly different reason to consider is that oblong particles may not be fully contained within the melt pool. Heat from the laser will conduct away from area in contact with laser, preventing full melting.
