For L-PBF processes, recycled powder is typically classified using a 63 mm (mesh 230) sieve, which is larger than the typical upper powder size limit of 44 mm (mesh 325). Use of the larger sieve improves process flow. Use of a finer sieve is possible but can be detrimentally slow and does not typically result in appreciable benefit to powder quality. The demonstrated impact of reuse varies with specific metal alloys. The flowability of powder is improved with each consecutive reuse (as long as the powder is sieved before reuse) because of improved sphericity. Cordova et al. found that the particle size distribution in recycled Inconel 718 increased with each consecutive reuse, but that this effect was easily mitigated by sieving the powder [54], whereas Ti6Al4V showed little variation in size distribution as a result of powder recycling. In the case of easily oxidized metals, such as GRCop-42, powder recycling may not inherently have a negative impact on the powder; however, the process of removing parts from the build chamber and sieving the powder will likely result in additional exposure to oxygen. It is recommended that powder be tested periodically for oxygen content by inert-gas fusion analysis, mass spectrometry, or other suitable analytical methods [76].
