2.3. LASER POWDER DIRECTED ENERGY DEPOSITION (LP-DED)
2.3.1. INTRODUCTION
The LP-DED process uses concentrated thermal energy to fuse metals by melting them locally as they are deposited [78]. This process allows for both the freeform fabrication of components and the addition of material to components for the purposes of coating, repair, or modification using local deposition. Freeform components are formed by the precise control of various parameters to create individual (weld) beads that are deposited integrally to take a final geometric shape. Additionally, multiple metals can be used within this process, thereby enabling the creation of locally optimized properties, coatings on dissimilar alloys, and the addition of functionally graded materials (FGMs). Various forms of DED that offer large-scale AM exist. The most common of these is LP-DED. Although laser beam laser powder DED is recognized as the terminology by ASTM International, LP-DED is also recognized by several other industry names [79], including direct metal deposition (DMD), direct laser metal deposition (DLMD), laser metal deposition (LMD), laser engineered net shape (LENS), additive layer deposition (ALD), laser metal deposition shaping, laser material deposition, laser powder deposition, and laser cladding [80–82].
