LP-DED uses powder feedstock as the filler metal and a laser as the energy source. The powder and the laser optics are mounted on an integrated deposition head, which allows for the convergence of the laser and the powder at a defined focus at the surface of the substrate (or part), where metal melting and deposition take place (Fig. 2.38). This substrate can be a sacrificial build plate, it can be used as part of the final component, or an existing component can be used. The deposition head is mounted to a robotic arm or gantry system to control motion through programmed or logic toolpaths to deposit material or create features. LP-DED was advanced in the mid-1980s and was originally used in industrial applications for depositing clad (cladding) metal to increase part thickness, repair parts, and allow for multimaterial compositions. Starting in the early 1990s, the process began advancing toward the freeform fabrication of components [83]. The LP-DED process can form near-net-shape blanks, final-shape components, and integral features within components, providing the ability to significantly reduce part counts. The advantages of this process are numerous, as discussed previously, with the main application being significantly larger scale and higher deposition rates than L-PBF and the ability to use multiple materials and FGM deposition strategies [84–86]. Adapting the deposition heads to multiaxis robots and gantry systems allows features to be added locally on parts, creating near-net or near-final shapes and significantly reducing the need for support structures or added stock. The metal is deposited and locally purged within an enclosure that is a fully inert environment to minimize oxidation. These aspects of the LP-DED process allow large component shapes with complex curvature to be created with multiple ports or asymmetric features. These include internal
