2.3.3. BUILD PROCESS OVERVIEW
The LP-DED process is initiated by starting the flow of powder and energizing the laser source to deposit metal locally onto a build plate or a part, forming a bead. The head must have a defined motion. If stalled too long in one area, the system can continue to build excess material. The proper fabrication of part geometry, grain structure and density, and distortion control for successful builds requires the control and monitoring of various parameters. These parameters control the systems that are integral to the deposition head, including the laser, powder feeder, and motion control [92, 104]. Many of these parameters include physical components to control the laser spot size or flow of powder, and many are softwaredriven. They can be categorized according to the subsystems (see Fig. 2.43),
