also be adjusted to maintain proper power and density. Depending on the build strategy, geometry, and other factors such as distortion or dilution, the laser power can stay the same for the duration of the build or change frequently. The laser power is a software-controlled parameter, although some physical hardware might require changes, such as fiber optics to accommodate increased laser power.
2.3.3.2. TRAVEL SPEED
Travel speed is the rate at which the head moves in a linear direction. It is also referred to as the feed rate, scan speed, or traverse speed. The travel speed can be measured along a certain axis or combination of axes, such as around corners or along radii. The travel speed also controls the amount of local heat flux into the part because it has a time component. The travel speed is generally measured in velocity units such as mm/s (in./min) and is controlled by the motion system. A gantry-type system can provide a more constant travel speed than a robotic arm. The speed may be adjusted with geometry variations such as corners or radii to provide a constant bead height throughout the part build.
2.3.3.3. POWDER FEED RATE
Powder feed rate is the mass or volumetric flow rate of the powder introduced onto the focal plane that converges with the laser beam. The powder feed rate is influenced by the inert carrier gas and the powder. It is generally measured as a volumetric flow rate (cm3/min) or as mass flow rate (g/min). Integral to the powder flow rate is the jet shape of the powder injection. This jet shape is controlled by the flow rate, powder delivery nozzle design, and gas flow rates, and thus, the focal plane may vary based on variations in the powder flow rate. The secondary shielding gas flow also has an impact on the jet shape of the powder.
2.3.3.4. SPOT SIZE
Spot size is the diameter of the laser at its focal point, where it meets the build substrate or part. The spot size is the major contributor to the melt-pool size and, subsequently, the width of the bead. It is controlled physically with the optics in the head and the fiber-optic cable, although the latter is usually kept constant. Because the spot size controls the melt pool, it determines, in conjunction with laser power and powder feed rate, the size of features that can be built. A smaller spot size can build a much thinner wall, yielding higher feature resolution. However, a smaller spot size will require a reduction of laser power to maintain power density. This results in a reduction in deposition rate, but a finer grain size because of the decreased heat input and reduced columnar grains. There is a trade-off between the spot size and the deposition rate, based on part requirements.
