2.3.3.5. HATCH SPACING OR STEP-OVER
Hatch spacing or step-over controls the spacing between the deposited beads (see Fig. 2.45). Track overlap is a term that is also used, but in the opposite context referencing the mixing between deposited adjacent beads. The spacing of the beads must be controlled such that there is adequate joining between beads; otherwise, LOF defects will be present. Increased spacing, or high step-over, between beads leads to higher porosity. An ideal step-over leads to a more uniform microstructure but can also result in variations in bead height. Although the step-over varies, it is generally between 30% and 50%.
2.3.3.6. LAYER HEIGHT
Layer height, also called layer thickness, is the step increase with subsequent build height. It differs from the bead height because dilution into the previously deposited layer occurs and must be taken into account to achieve proper joining in a part. Once determined during initial development, it is controlled by software. The layer height is highly influenced by the powder feed rate, but laser power and travel speed are also factors. It is important to be able to monitor actual layer height relative to the programmed layer height. Some deposition systems include a closed-loop monitoring system for resulting layer height.
