oxidation, pinholes, or distortion. Several common failures occur during the LP-DED process [138–140]. Many of these failures can be mitigated through parameter optimization, including toolpath optimization, design or material changes, machine maintenance, or evaluations of the process supply chain (e.g., powder). Different metals will react much differently in the process and may be more prone to build issues than others. Although many failures in LP-DED mimic those observed in traditional fusion welding processes, some unique failure modes can arise based on extended-time welding (deposition) compiling or drifting over the length of freeform AM builds. Some typical LP-DED failures are as follows (also see Fig. 2.61):
1. Residual stresses are caused by rapid heating and cooling during the build process. As subsequent layers are built on the part, the metal is deposited at the melt pool and then solidifies. This causes rapid expansions and contractions of the metal. These residual stresses are common across all of the metal AM processes in which material is melted. Residual stresses can also be generated as a result of solidification shrinkage during processing and phase transformations during solidification. Alloys that can undergo martensitic transformation, such as steels and titanium, accumulate residual stresses following phase transformation during solidification. The strategy for the toolpath and the approach to building the part can help reduce residual stresses but cannot eliminate them. Post-build heat treatments are also used to help reduce residual stresses.
