2. Distortion is often a result of residual stresses. Distortion occurs where a local area of material has residual stresses higher than the yield strength of the metal [117]. This most often occurs in an area where the metal has been deposited and is in the process of cooling. Cracking could also be present if the ultimate strength of the metal is exceeded. Distortion is the visual indication of residual stresses and can be seen as deformation, bowing, misalignment, or potential offsets or shifts in a build toolpath. In the LP-DED process, long thin walls may tend to bow and may require interim intersecting supports. It can also be common to see distortions in the base build plate if not sized properly.
3. Melt-pool instabilities can cause variations in the melt pool that may penetrate too deep into the prior layers or not enough dilution, causing a lack of bonding. Instabilities can also cause irregular bead shapes or excessive spatter, which can lead to significant roughness and coarsening issues.
4. Voids can be caused by several issues from the LP-DED process, including those arising from the material feedstock and trapped voids from deposition. Voids in the deposited material can also be caused by trapped vapor or moisture in the powder, variations in the powder shape and size, porosity connected to the surface, and shielding gas that can become trapped in each metal layer.
5. Oxidation and surface contamination can be present on regions that are not properly shielded with an inert gas locally within the global purge environment. There also may be regions of contamination from the feedstock powder or the build plate. Surface contamination can also cause additional issues, with subsequent layers trapping the contamination within the deposited metal.
6. Lack of fusion (LOF) describes voids of fine-scale inclusions that can be present as a result of inadequate penetration of the deposited region into the substrate, layer below, or between the adjacent beams (overlap). LOF defects can also be caused by improper bead geometry or bead sizing in parts with more than one track width per layer. Melt-pool instability can also cause LOF defects. This form of defect can often be longer than just a single void and depends on the length of the track.
7. Deposition overrun or underrun is a condition in which excessively built material or lack of material is present where the axis of travel and the laser powder deposition are not in sync. When the laser is powered on and the powder is being fed into the melt pool, material will be deposited, and the head should be in motion. This is most commonly observed at the starts, stops, and intersections or overlaps of the component. Programming strategies can help mitigate or prevent this defect.
8. Layer delamination is a defect in which significant oxidation occurs from an insufficient purge or excessive oxygen within the environment. The high content of oxygen within the deposit can cause a layer to delaminate as a
