result of incomplete bonding. This can also be related to residual stress and a lack of dilution into the previous bead. Unfused powder can also cause layer delamination if the powder flow rate is too high.
9. Partially melted powder is often observed on the surface where it adheres to outer walls, causing a significant increase in surface roughness. Because the LP-DED process is not fully efficient with respect to powder usage, excess powder that is not melted can adhere to regions in heat-affected zones that are adjacent to the melt pool. This can often occur immediately behind the melt pool, as well as on the adjacent walls. Partially melted powder can also be trapped in the melt pool between layers because of Marangoni-driven melt flow, which causes higher surface tensions and foldover of deposited material [141].
10. Elemental segregation or solute segregation is a result of the rapid heating and cooling of the elements and dendritic structure formed during solidification. This is due to rapid and directional solidification, which does not allow for redistribution of the alloying elements into the parent metal matrix. It causes an inhomogeneous microstructure and can lead to cracking and anisotropic properties at the microstructural level. Although microsegregation is always present in dendritic solidification, elemental segregation in DED is less than that in a typical casting because of the smaller melt-pool size. However, elemental loss can occur for elements with high vapor pressures if the melt pool is superheated. In other words, an increase in laser power can cause a high melt-pool temperature and result in elemental losses.
11. Cracking is often caused by a nonoptimized build process window and unfavorable metal selection for the LP-DED process. Cracking can be observed at the interfaces of passes, internal to the deposition beads, and in the metal substrate. Cracks can be caused by the reheating of previous layers, excessive segregation of solute, residual stresses, or the solidification behavior of the metal being deposited. For crack-sensitive metals, cracking can also initiate at start and stop points of layers, particularly if the start and stop points overlay one another. Repeated heating and cooling at these points can initiate microcracks that can grow with subsequent layers.
2.4. ELECTRON BEAM POWDER BED FUSION (EB-PBF)
2.4.1. INTRODUCTION
EB-PBF is an AM process in which metal powders can be selectively fused by an electron beam to fabricate three-dimensional structures. Compared to conventional manufacturing processes, EB-PBF is capable of building low-volume, highvalue components at reduced lead times. For example, EB-PBF has been widely demonstrated for the production of more than 150,000 acetabular cups for medical devices, but it has also been used for satellite and propulsion components
