For weld parameters and environmental control, empirical data based on previous successes are used for a new build and are often based on welding techniques [157]. They depend on the type of metal alloy, wire size, part geometry, and desired deposition bead width. Proper setting of these parameters results in the desired wire feed rate and robot travel speed, which are essential for obtaining a superior-quality microstructure through weld beads that are free of porosity and cracks. Part distortion usually worsens with increasing build volume. Careful process considerations result in desired dimensions and quality. Distortions have the greatest effects on the substrate plate geometry. To date, several millimeters of substrate plate distortions have been recorded for even up to 19 mm (0.75 in.)-thick plates. Such distortion is due to thermal compression stresses resulting from solidification shrinkage of the molten metal and the melt pool. These effects can be minimized using thicker substrate plates and/or depositing metal on both sides of the substrate plate to cancel out deformations in opposite directions. For the latter case, it is necessary for the part dimension measurement systems to consider the amount of substrate plate distortion to enable metal deposition in the correct location and to sustain proper wire “stick-out” height during deposition. Certain random circumstances can negatively impact weld quality, even for an optimized and controlled process with real-time integrated sensing. Weld defects can include porosity (surface and subsurface), LOF defects, hot cracking, and microcracking. If any of these defects are present in the welded layer, they need to be addressed by determining the root cause and developing mitigation strategies. Defective layers should not be buried under a fresh layer and will need to be ground or machined down to a defect-free layer. This in situ subtractive process is followed by a corrective deposition; following corrective action, the next layer can be applied. However, these in-situ repairs must be carefully tracked and not typically part of a qualified process. Preliminary studies indicate that in situ surface visual and thermal imaging may be effective nondestructive evaluation (NDE) methods for detecting and identifying such defects (Chapter 5). The AW-DED process has a high deposition rate compared to powder-based processes and can result in a high-density microstructure. Applications of this process include manufacturing large near-net-shape structural components, such as composite layup tooling, nozzle liners, fuel tanks, and hypersonic aircraft components, and is also being evaluated for in-space manufacturing use.
