2.6.1. INTRODUCTION
Traditionally, wire-based DED processes have received less attention than PBF processes and the LP-DED process. However, wire DED processes have started to gain traction since 2005 because of the increasing market demand for fabricating large-scale metal components with AM [158]. These processes offer a costcompetitive alternative to the milling of large components, especially for expensive or difficult-to-machine alloys, such as titanium alloys and nickel, which have readily available wire feedstock. Wire-based DED systems have previously been used for simple geometry and welding purposes, but with software developments, they can now be used to fabricate freeform structures. The expanding market interest in serial component production has recently advanced wire-based DED processes, as repeatability, material properties, material usage, component size, and fabrication speed have advanced and overcome issues. Using wire instead of powder feedstock can provide higher deposition rates and deposition efficiency and simplify feedstock handling and storage. Furthermore, wire, in general, has a higher purity than powder and is less susceptible to contamination during the process because of its reduced surface area [159]. Other advantages of wire over powder include better shielding since optimization of the shielding gas can interfere with the powder supply and must be well tuned. Finally, the powder must be completely dried to ensure proper flow and prevent humidity from entering the process zone, which is not required for wire [160]. Generally, arc-based DED systems are inexpensive compared to LW-DED and EB-DED systems. Thus, less research has been conducted on LW-DED and EB-DED. However, compared to arc-based DED, LW-DED can control the thermal heat input with a higher accuracy, which reduces the unnecessary heat input and keeps oxidation and distortion to a minimum. For this reason, surface finish is also enhanced in the case of LW-DED. Traditionally, LW-DED is applied in an open environment for nonreactive alloys such as stainless steel 316L and in an argon environment for reactive alloys such as Ti6Al4V. This process has also been applied in a vacuum, but so far, little research on vacuum operations has been conducted [161]. In contrast, EB-DED requires a vacuum environment to allow for the beam of electrons to flow to a workpiece and create a melt pool. Figure 2.72 presents a schematic diagram of a typical LWDED process.
