2.7. LASER HOT-WIRE DIRECTED ENERGY DEPOSITION (LHW-DED)
2.7.1. INTRODUCTION
An alternative to the laser wire DED process using cold wire is LHW-DED. This process was developed for welding and cladding operations at least as early as the 1980s as an improvement on standard cold-wire laser processes [166]. Cold-wire laser processes use a laser as the heat source to melt a wire feedstock fed into the laser on a translating head to create freeform structures. LHW augments coldwire laser processes through the introduction of preheating to the wire feedstock, typically by electrical resistance from an electric current passed through the wire between the contact tip and the workpiece, as shown in Fig. 2.77 [167]. Compared with a cold-wire process, the independent control of laser and wire power in this method allows for the replacement of some of the laser power with more efficient and cheaper resistive heating. This reduces the average temperature of the melt pool, as the preheated wire reduces the quenching effect of a cold-wire addition. Development of this process for AM began in about 2012 [168], and it has a number of unique features compared to LP-DED and EB-DED processes.
2.7.2. DIFFERENCES FROM OTHER PROCESSES
Compared to traditional arc welding and laser arc hybrid processes, a wellcontrolled laser hot-wire process suppresses the formation of an arc between the wire and workpiece. The goal of hot-wire process control is to maximize the resistive heating power in the wire while minimizing arcing events, which reduce control over the melt-pool temperature and geometry. With the increased
