2.8.3. PARAMETERS AND HOW THEY CAN AFFECT BUILDS
For EBW DED processes, there are many variables that affect the builds, some of which are fixed by the system and others that can be controlled to fine tune the resulting metal microstructure and component geometry. The geometry of the EBW-DED hardware, such as the size of the vacuum chamber, wire entry angle into the molten pool, and gun-to-work distance (distance between the electron beam gun and the molten pool) are generally limited by the design of the system. These fixed limitations dictate the maximum size and angles of complexity that can be achieved with that specific EBW-DED hardware. Since EBW-DED systems are currently not as common as those for other AM processes, many systems are customized to the sizes and shapes intended to be built [176]. The EBW-DED process involves physical motion of either the electron beam gun assembly, the parts, or both, including translation, tilt, and rotation. Because EBW-DED is well-suited for high deposition rates and large parts, physical distances and heavy weights limit the achievable travel speeds, typically less than 2.54 m/min (100 in./min). The build is free-standing, allowing tilting of both the substrate and the electron beam gun to achieve unsupported overhangs that are limited only by the physical collision of the hardware. Multi-axis operations also enable the EBW-DED process to add details onto simplified preforms, existing structures, or repairs. Careful toolpath planning, leveraging standard multiaxis programming used in CNC machines, allows for flexibility in the geometry of builds using the EBW-DED process [176]. The finest controllable variables in EBW-DED processes are the beam parameters, which include the accelerating voltage, beam current, focus, deflection,
