2.9. ADDITIVE FRICTION STIR-DEPOSITION (AFS-D)
2.9.1. INTRODUCTION
AFS-D is a solid-state AM process that provides unique capabilities for printing and repairing components [183–185]. The AFS-D process was originally introduced by van der Stelt et al. and Liu and Zhang [186, 187] and builds off the friction stir cladding (FSC) technique. The processes uses a rod that is pushed through a hollow rotating tool so that frictional heat generation and severe plastic deformation from the tool shoulders are able to aid in more robust deposited metal than what was achieved through FSC. Specifically, during AFS-D, an actuator applies a downward force onto a solid rod feedstock that is fed through a hollow rotating tool that provides confining forces. As the rod is pressed onto the substrate build plate, frictional heat is generated, and the metal undergoes shear-induced severe plastic deformation, dynamic recrystallization, and deposition onto a moving substrate and subsequent layers, as shown in Fig. 2.86a. Stacked strips can also be used as the feedstock instead of a solid rod. AFS-D produces a refined and equiaxed microstructure while avoiding the liquidsolid phase transformation that can be problematic for metals such as hard steels, aluminum alloys, and magnesium alloys in which hot cracking may occur [188, 189]. Figure 2.86b shows a feed system for waste machine chips or powder fed by an auger through the hollow rotating tool. Powder feedstock has been demonstrated to a certain extent, but the tooling is immature and requires significant development. Process parameters include the tool geometry, tool or spindle rotational speed, substrate traversing velocity, feed or deposition rate, actuator force, and layer height. Torque is monitored, because it provides an indication of when the metal is plasticizing, which then indicates when to initiate traverse and deposition.
