AFS-D requires a low power input ( 5 kW); is scalable; deposits most metals in ambient atmosphere; is capable of printing a variety of metals such as nonweldable alloys; and because of low residual stress, creates builds with little to no geometric distortion. The lack of residual stress can be attributed to the solid-state nature of the AFS-D process, where the maximum deposition temperature (Td) remains below the liquidus temperature (Td , Tm) of the deposited metal, which prevents phase transformation during the deposition. The relatively low deposition temperature results in low residual stress and low geometric distortion for the part, unlike for fusion-based AM processes. AFS-D also has the potential to join or repair dissimilar metals to produce functionally graded components. Since the feedstock is generally in the form of rods and not powder, AFS-D has demonstrated the ability to print highly reactive metals such as magnesium under inert argon atmosphere, which significantly decreases pyrophoric hazards. Metal in the plasticized zone is heated to the deposition temperature, which ranges from 60% to 80% of the melting temperature, so reactive metals must be printed in an argon-backfilled enclosure. AFS-D has successfully been used to print a variety of Al, Ti, and Mg alloys in an inert argon environment [191]. As-deposited materials exhibit nearly full density (.99.99% RD) and submicrometer equiaxed grain sizes and distributions (Fig. 2.87), resulting in higher yield strengths, ultimate tensile strengths, and strains at failure compared to wrought metals [192].
