2.10.2. DIFFERENCES FROM OTHER PROCESSES
The fundamental distinguishing characteristic of UAM, in comparison to many other metallic AM processes, is that it is a solid-state welding process, meaning that melting of the metal does not occur. Thus, the impact of high temperatures on microstructure is eliminated. Ultrasonic metal welding has existed since about the 1950s, with modern applications in the everyday welding of battery tabs, thin foil packaging, and even electronic wires. An ultrasonic weld operation begins by pressing a thin metal foil onto another metal component. While it is under a constant force, ultrasonic vibrations are applied to cause scrubbing of the mating faces. This shearing motion cleans off surface oxides through friction to then allow direct contact of pure metal on pure metal. The result is a solid-state atomic bond with minimal heating. Some example parts made with this process are shown in Fig. 2.94. The heat and plastic deformation promote diffusion and recrystallization at the interface, resulting in a true metallurgical bond. Ultrasonic welding can be accomplished at very low temperature and without any special environments. For all metals, the bonding temperature is significantly below the respective melting temperature. In aluminums for example, this peak temperature is always below 1208C. The solid-state nature is a key advantage of ultrasonic welds for the following reasons:
