2. The process protects the material properties of the incoming feedstock. Because the metals are only slightly heated, they do not experience changes in grain size, precipitation reactions, or phase changes. The properties of the incoming feedstock are the same as the properties of the final part.
3. UAM creates bonds between dissimilar metals without creating an undesirable metallurgy. This capability differentiates UAM from fusion-based (i.e. melting) processes and allows for printed engineered metals with custom material properties or properties to match an existing component. For instance, layers of molybdenum and Invar can be printed into an aluminum heat exchanger to match the coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE) of a mounted electronic circuit.
4. UAM allows for embedded temperature-sensitive components in solid metal parts. Many electronic components including microprocessors, sensors, and telemetry have been successfully embedded in solid metal parts using UAM. The low-temperature bond allows delicate components to be embedded in solid metal without the damage incurred in comparable fusionbased AM processes.
UAM relies on both vibrations and large downward forces to create bonds. These two factors limit the geometries available by UAM. Although AM is well known for fine-featured organic shapes, UAM systems can crush small delicate features. Similarly, tall, skinny features are not suitable for production by this method.
2.10.3. PARAMETERS AND HOW THEY CAN AFFECT BUILDS
2.10.3.1. ULTRASONIC AMPLITUDE
A normal scrubbing amplitude range for welding metal foils is between 20 and 50 mm peak-to-peak. In general, a higher amplitude will provide better bond strength, up to a point. If the amplitude is too high, the foil may undesirably weld to the sonotrode (forming a “nugget”), which is not sustainable under production conditions. Researchers are developing new sonotrode materials to allow welding at higher amplitudes without incurring this undesirable welding issue.
2.10.3.2. TRAVEL SPEED
A normal range of travel speeds is between 1 and 5 m/min (between 3.3 and 16.4 ft/min) for linear travel. A lower speed allows more time for the sonotrode to interact with any given point on the part, thereby increasing the amount of energy put into a given area. Thus, decreasing the travel speed can increase the
