bond quality, to a point. However, too low of a travel speed can produce welding issues similar to those that occur when using too high of a welding amplitude (wherein metal can undesirably stick to the welding device).
2.10.3.3. DOWNWARD FORCE
During printing, the UAM weld head applies force between the metal feedstock to be welded and the build plate beneath it. A normal downward force range is between 1000 and 5000 N. In experimentation, it has been found that there is a minimum downward force required to make good bonds. However, once this minimum has been reached, applying more force has minimal effect on the bonding dynamics. Thus, in practice, the minimum force is experimentally found, and the machines are run slightly higher than this minimum to ensure good bonding.
2.10.3.4. MATERIAL
UAM is useful for bonding all types of metals and alloys. The higher the yield strength of the metal, the higher the amount of energy needed to produce strong bonds. For example, welding aluminum has much lower parameters (lower amplitude, higher travel speed) than welding stainless steel.
2.10.3.5. MATERIAL THICKNESS
A normal thickness range of foil feedstock is between 150 and 250 mm. The thicker the material, the more energy is needed to make a strong bond, up to a point. When metal feedstock is too thick, ultrasonic energy is lost to internal shear within the thickness, and the scrubbing energy is not effectively directed to the bonding interface. In scenarios of overly thick feedstocks, bond strength can suffer. As such, thicker feedstocks are better replaced by multiple layers of a thinner feedstock.
2.10.3.6. PREHEATING
In some cases, the base plate anvil can be preheated to improve productivity. A normal preheat temperature is typically less than 1008C. However, the preheat cannot get so hot that the metal starts to oxidize. Thus, preheating is mainly used on softer alloys such as aluminum to enable a higher travel speed.
