2.10.5. BASIC LIMITS OF THE PROCESS
2.10.5.1. GEOMETRY
The first limitation is best described as a height-to-width ratio. Tall, skinny structures, such as thin walls, look like simple beams. At a height-to-width ratio of about 0.7, the theoretical simple beam has a resonant frequency of 20 kHz. As UAM is inputting energy at 20 kHz, structures made at this ratio can be incidentally put into resonance, and printing can be negatively impacted. Similarly, tall, skinny features are also prone to buckling under high loads. To avoid this limitation, tall structures are printed wider than needed and then machined afterward to get the true skinny final shape. Although UAM has the ability to produce fine features down to less than 250 mm, this is primarily accomplished through subtractive CNC machining. As mentioned previously, very small features with high aspect ratios are prone to buckling. As a result, UAM tends to succeed in larger parts with larger features.
