2.11. COLD SPRAY
2.11.1. INTRODUCTION
Cold spraying (also called kinetic spraying or cold gas dynamic spraying) has become an established method for near-net-shape component fabrication, coating, joining, and repair applications [196–198]. Currently, there is growing interest in optimizing cold spray deposition to AM structural components at the same level of thermomechanical properties as other manufacturing methods [199]. In the cold spray process, metallic powders are accelerated to supersonic velocity (up to 1200 m/s) by injection into a high-temperature and high-pressure gas jet and then impinged onto a substrate. The high-velocity gas jet is generated by use of a converging-diverging de Laval–type nozzle. The physical description of this process is based on aerodynamics and the dynamic plasticity behavior of metals [200]. The bonding of cold spray deposition is usually formed for ductile metallic particles with diameters of approximately 10–100 mm when impacted on substrates of metals, ceramics, or glasses. Due to the limited thermal exposure, the deposited metal is clean of oxides and molten features, so there is no need for a controlled atmosphere and the deposition is done in air, which dramatically simplifies the process when compared to environmentally controlled AM processes. The cold spray process is illustrated in Fig. 2.97, where a cold spray gun is mounted on a robotic arm that aims the supersonic particle stream onto a target. It uses existing thermal spray infrastructure, usually installed in a spray booth where one or multiple powder feeders are connected to the gun to inject the powder into the converging part of a de Laval nozzle, where the kinetic energy of the carrier gas is transfered to the powder particles. The gas, nitrogen or helium, is heated before it enters the nozzle to increase gas velocity. Usually, electrical heating elements are located in the body of the spray gun and increase the gas temperatures up to 11008C, under high pressure (typically 10–60 bar) and, depending on nozzle geometry, up to 250 m3/h. After the particles have been accelerated and leave the nozzle under highvelocity impact conditions, adhesion at the particle–substrate interface occurs
