2.11.4. COLD SPRAY ADVANTAGES FOR AEROSPACE PROPULSION
Due to its low-temperature and solid-state characteristics, cold spray has been considered for repair applications as an alternative to welding. The low heat input into the part allows for the repair of temperature-sensitive alloys where the weld heat-affected zone would have unacceptable properties. Solid-state deposition enables repair with alloys that are difficult to weld because of cracking. Repair for aerospace, especially engines, can be divided into two categories: geometrical restoration and structural repair. For geometrical restoration, the need is to restore the original shape of a component that was physically damaged by corrosion, wear, or any other process that produced the loss of the designed shape of the part. As its name suggests, the damaged area is not considered critical for structural or functional loading in the component. As such, the only concerns are sufficient adhesion between the cold spray deposition and the original component, and restoration of the original shape of the part. Conversely, structural repairs may require material properties to be equivalent to or close to those of the original part. A key engineering challenge in structural repair is that, to achieve this, some post-spray heat treatment of the deposit is usually required, as discussed further in Chapter 3. The heat-treatment operation can pose real challenges and constraints because of possible microstructural degradation of the part being repaired. Distortion may cause the component to deviate beyond the dimensional tolerances. These considerations can make the decision to repair the part using cold spray quite complicated, depending on the base metal and part engineering requirements.
