Successful application of the cold spray process to aerospace propulsion necessitates a thorough understanding of the entire process. Knowledge of process fundamentals is critical but not sufficient for predicting material outcomes. Metallurgical understanding of the metal or alloy in its powder form, during flight and after impact, as well as post-spray heat treatment is needed to determine the resulting mechanical and physical properties. Cold spray can demonstrate near-wrought properties when the process is optimized for both geometry and metallurgical characteristics, and it is well-suited for large-scale high-throughput AM of aerospace components
2.12. BINDER JET
2.12.1. INTRODUCTION
Binder jetting AM uses an inkjet printhead to loosely bind dry powder layer-by-layer to form a three-dimensional part that is consolidated later in a furnace. The process works by spreading a layer of powder in a powder bed, depositing the binder, lowering the powder bed, spreading more powder, adding more binder, and continuing this process until the build is complete. The binding solution usually consists of a polymer binder in a solvent carrier; however, monomers have also been used [217, 218]. After printing, the entire “job box” or “build volume” is placed in a curing oven and heated to set the binder, that is, to evaporate the solvent carrier fluid or to cure the monomer. Once the curing cycle is complete, the parts are removed from the powder bed, depowdered, and post-processed in a furnace to achieve near-full density. Traditionally, melt infiltration of the printed preform with a secondary metal was used to create fully dense composite parts (e.g., steel printed powder would be infiltrated with bronze). However, modern techniques focus on sintering metals to near-full density. During the furnace cycle, the polymer from the binding solution is pyrolyzed, usually leaving some residual carbon, and the part is
