2.12.2. DIFFERENCES FROM OTHER METAL AM PROCESSES
Binder jetting is one of four “indirect” AM technologies that creates a “green part” or bound-powder preform that is post-processed (cured and sintered) to achieve its final material properties. The other indirect AM methods, including extrusion, vat photopolymerization, and material jetting, also use binders to shape powder. Unlike material jetting, which uses inkjet to deposit the actual build material in slurry form, binder jetting uses inkjet to deposit a binder only. Furthermore, unlike extrusion or photopolymerization of a premixed slurry, binder jetting forms a binder–powder system during the printing process itself. Although the means of forming a preform differ among indirect AM processes, these processes face the same challenges with distortion during sintering. Dimensional shrinkage resulting from densification in binder jet parts is observed from the initial green preform to the sintered state [219, 220]. This shrinkage is due to the binder burn-off and sintering, which can be anisotropic. The shrinkage can vary (e.g., from 3% to 25%) depending on the metal alloy, geometry, direction, particle size distribution and morphology, and binder used [221, 222]. Binder jetting is the only indirect AM method that uses dry powder instead of a slurry, and binder jet preforms are typically lower in density (dry powders pack poorly compared with engineered slurries) and are made up of larger particles (required for safety reasons); both of these factors contribute to poor sintering kinetics. Although these differences are important to understand when evaluating AM processes, the main difference between binder jetting and other AM processes (and main disadvantage of the former) is the depowdering stage. Unlike PBF or even the other indirect AM methods, binder jetting produces low-strength parts that are buried within a bed of powder and must be removed in a process called depowdering, as shown in Fig. 2.103 [223]. This step in the binder jet process is unique and quite labor-intensive. In fact, it is the depowdering step that limits the design of binder parts, as certain features, although printable, cannot survive the handling required in the depowdering step [224].
