The crucial advantage of binder jetting is the unique combination of speed and resolution: Binder jetting is capable of depositing more than 100 L/h while maintaining a resolution down to the millimeter scale [225, 226]. Its ability to shape complex metal artifacts with high resolution and speed has attracted the automotive and metal injection molding industries, among others (Speed and scalability are known to be the main cost drivers in AM.) Thus, binder jetting has been found to be the most well-suited of all AM processes for mass-production scenarios.
2.12.3. PARAMETERS AND HOW THEY AFFECT BUILDS
As with any AM process, seemingly endless build parameters can be adjusted to optimize the outcome of the print. Binder jetting is no exception, with parameters governing printing, curing, and densification. The primary parameters deal with the binder deposition and the powder spreading and drying; these parameters are binder saturation, spread speed, and dry time, respectively. Binder saturation is the amount of binder that is deposited within a specific “voxel” of the build. It is driven by the powder properties and/or the ability of the powder to absorb the binder and is determined experimentally. A binder saturation that is too high will result in parts that appear swollen or poorly defined (from the binder wicking out of its designated voxel), and one that is too low will create parts that are too weak to be removed from the powder bed [227, 228]. Powder spread speed is adjusted independently of binder saturation and affects the build speed and powder bed density and uniformity. (These are competing traits: Lower spread speed yields a denser, more uniform powder bed but dramatically increases build time.) Finally, powder drying occurs after binder is deposited in the layer and serves an important role in ensuring a successful build. Without enough drying, the binder will stick to the roller during the spreading of the next layer and create clumps of bound powder on the roller, which ultimately mars the layer. Too much drying results in cracking and peeling of the layer. Other build parameters that affect the quality of the part include droplet characteristics such as drop volume and drop density, bed temperature (which affects binder permeation), and layer height (which affects resolution).
