2.12.4. BASIC LIMITS OF THE PROCESS
Consolidated, singlealloy binder jet parts with densities better than those obtained by casting are routinely achieved ( 97% density), which demonstrates the viability of the process in the single-alloy space [230, 231]. However, the nuance to this achievement is that these parts follow specific design guidelines as listed in “Manufacturing Guidelines for ExOne Metal Sintered Parts” [232]. Large parts can be created with binder jetting (larger than 10 in. in one dimension). However, the challenges include handling a heavy, fragile part during depowdering and then identifying the right sintering parameters to evenly densify the large preform; this process is often iterative and, thus, time-consuming and expensive. As previously mentioned, the design of binder jet parts is driven by the low strength of the printed preform. The material selection, however, is driven by two main factors: the effect of the binder on the alloy composition and the ability of the material to sinter. Since the binder usually consists of organic polymers, it leaves behind residual carbon when pyrolyzed in the furnace cycle. As a result, the current research has mostly focused on steels, in which carbon usually enhances the strength. Significant research has been focused on alternative, “clean” binders that leave no residual carbon. Until then, steels are a main focus of mass production, with Inconel alloys also being demonstrated.
2.12.5. BINDER JET SUMMARY
Binder jetting is unique among AM processes in many ways, but most significant is its binding of dry powder with inkjet and its unique combination of speed and resolution. As an indirect AM process, binder jetting produces metal powder parts that must be sintered in a furnace for usable material properties to be achieved. Unlike other indirect metal AM processes, binder jetting creates parts from dry powder, which has a low packing density compared with engineered slurries. In
