2.13. SUMMARY
Metal additive manufacturing encompasses multiple processes, and each has distinct advantages and disadvantages. This chapter provided an overview of the processes of powder bed fusion (PBF) processes, directed energy deposition (DED) processes, and solid-state processes which include cold spray, ultrasonic additive manufacturing (UAM), additive friction stir deposition (AFS-D), and binder jet. Various feedstocks for these processes include powder, wire, barstock and sheet. There are several considerations to be made when selecting an AM process. These include process maturity, part complexity, geometric feature resolution, scale of the part, required properties, deposition rate (e.g. process speed that cost is proportional to), process availability, and material options for monolithic or multi-metallic alloys. As the list of available AM processes grows, the trade space becomes exponentially more complex. Due to the stringent need for propulsion components to be safe, reliable, and repeatable, it is fundamental that the full AM process lifecycle must be considered. It must also be understood that the AM process selection is an integrated and iterative activity that begins early in the design phase of a part.
