3.1. INTRODUCTION
Proper selection of metal alloys is essential to component design for propulsion applications. Throughout this chapter, elemental metals and metal alloys are referred to as metals, alloys, metal alloys, or materials, and these terms are used interchangeably. Appropriate selection and use of these alloys and how they are processed throughout the entire life cycle will have a significant impact on the success of parts when in service. The environments in which metals must operate for propulsion applications are highly demanding and involve temperature extremes from cryogenic (22538C, [24238F]) through elevated temperatures (often .10008C [18328F]), high dynamic and static loadings, and long life cycles. The metal requirements are subjected to further demands, as weight is a primary consideration for any aerospace application. Aerospace components are often designed with minimal margins, and so a thorough understanding of metal behavior under the expected operating conditions is critical. Propulsion systems also tend to have a high number of components, each with unique environments that result in component-specific metal requirements. For example, a tank structure may contain a cryogenic propellant such as liquid oxygen, hydrocarbon fuel, or liquid hydrogen. The tank may operate at cryogenic temperatures and lower pressures but still require a high
