3.2. INTRODUCTION TO METALS FOR AM
This chapter provides fundamental concepts throughout the AM design life cycle that a design engineer can use for component and system design. One important aspect to note is the shift in the role of the design engineer. A classical design structure may propose that the design team coordinate with the manufacturing team to determine an optimal design that is realistically producible. Although this is still true with AM, the manufacturing role also shifts more to the design team because the part will be built directly from computer-aided design (CAD) models. The build process and post-processing must be taken into account early in the design phase. This is true of the desired metal quality as well. The properties of AM materials are more process-sensitive than those of forged metals. In addition, geometry-dependent properties may be more prevalent in AM metals than in forged metals. For example, some of this variation may be heavily dependent on the part geometry, such as variations in wall thickness and orientation of features, which have been shown to impact properties [3–5]. The design team needs to be fully aware of these AM-specific property variations, along with the proper post-processing to ensure that the required material
