3.3. ADDITIVE METALS FOR PROPULSION
Propulsion applications use many alloys that can be challenging to fabricate with conventional manufacturing methods. The conventional stock requirements are stringent to ensure that any starting stock material satisfies the metal cleanliness and imperfection criteria and will meet the intended property requirements. As detailed in Chapter 6, AM feedstock requirements must be well-controlled for aerospace applications as well. This is due to both the extreme environments encountered in these applications and the strict weight requirements that component designs must meet. For example, a part may be designed with a factor of safety 1.2 (providing a margin of safety of 0.2) on yield strength. Any offnominal processing conditions could impact the margin and design safety. The selected metal alloys tend to be metals with high strength-to-weight ratios (or high specific strengths) from various alloy families. These alloys tend to be more expensive than common industrial steels or aluminum alloys and require secondary operations including machining to produce the final part. As AM has become more developed, interest has grown across the aerospace and propulsion industry in using these novel processes with existing alloy families in addition to developing new AM-specific alloys. From 2000 through 2015, the metals available with AM processes were very limited. The most common alloys available for use were Ti6Al4V, AlSi10Mg, Inconel 625, Inconel 718, CoCr, and 316L stainless steel. Much of this development and maturity of metals was guided by applications for aerospace and medical devices. This led designers to use what was available, but not always the optimal design solution. In propulsion applications, which tend to be more risk-sensitive, a design may be driven to select the common and available AM alloy types over a new AM alloy that needs to be developed and matured. Now that the understanding of AM process has matured, adoption of new alloys for AM can occur more readily, and the material developmental cycle is greatly reduced, pending proper process parameters and thermomechanical treatments. Having the choice of new alloys that are only enabled by AM could provide many benefits, including expanded design options, improved product performance, and reduced post-processing costs. Propulsion applications make use of metal alloys from a variety of alloy families, including nickel-, iron-, and cobalt-based superalloys; aluminum-, copper-, and titanium-based alloys; refractory alloys; and platinum-based alloys. Many of the alloys within these categories are formulated specifically for hightemperature applications, particularly the superalloys, copper-based alloys, and refractory alloys. Propulsion applications may require these alloys to operate at
