temperatures above 10008C (18328F) for long-duration use while remaining stable in microstructure and properties. Although many wrought alloys fit these requirements, they are generally Ni-based and refractory alloys that are challenging to machine, as discussed further in Chapter 5. Traditional processing required these parts to be machined from a monolithic wrought product form, and machining was slow and expensive. Nickel-based and refractory alloys are two families of metals that are very difficult to machine and require specialty tooling, cutting fluids, and long machining times. AM is a solution to provide the design complexity that eliminates or significantly reduces the machining time, reduces process metal waste (e.g., machine chips), and builds near-net-shape or near-final-shape parts. As AM processing of materials evolves, new alloys are being developed faster than has historically occurred, thanks in large part to improvements in simulation and modeling methods (Chapter 10). However, statistical databases with reliable or complete design properties are limited. This is true not only for new alloys under development but also for legacy alloys being processed using AM. Limited sets of validated properties are being published. The existence of limited data presents its own set of challenges for mature alloys to meet designs used in flight applications, as further discussed in Chapter 9. Beyond new alloy development, many advances have been made using bimetallic and multimetallic methods to manufacture components in a single piece. Functionally graded materials (FGMs) are also used in AM to gradually transition from one metal to another to provide a further improvement in properties and avoid the issues that can be associated with discrete joints [14]. Application of FGMs and complex-topology-optimized and generatively designed components (Chapter 7) was extremely difficult with traditional manufacturing methods. It must be mentioned that, in one respect, AM can provide too many options. Bimetallic and multimetallic alloys and FGMs that allow for new opportunities in part and metal design options are in development. These solutions come with some risks, such as much more complex parameter development and greater potential for the formation of undesirable carbides and intermetallic secondary phases with associated brittleness and crack-prone behavior [15]. Although deleterious phase formation does occur during traditional manufacturing, such as through improper heat treatment or brazing, such phases can become more apparent in FGMs or bimetallic alloys because of the highly variable feedstock distribution, complex parameters, and associated melt pool characteristics.
