Once the property requirements for a part have been determined, a material candidate list that can potentially meet the intended application is generated, which also takes into consideration availability and economics. AM may not always be the best solution based on metallurgical characteristics, manufacturability, and economic trade criteria. It is recommended that AM not be selected based solely on the technology’s novelty. AM is simply another manufacturing process available to the design and manufacturing team that must be traded against other manufacturing methods [2]. The AM supply chain, although maturing, is not as well established as the traditional metal manufacturing supply chain. Supplychain limitations can inhibit the availability of some alloys or processes, particularly for near-term part fabrication. An AM process may also be selected based on investments that an institution has already made. This decision process could be a mistake. Depending on the specific physical and metallurgical characteristics of the part and the anticipated service conditions, not all metal AM processes will be a good choice for all parts. Metals commonly used in AM for aerospace are aluminum alloys, stainless steels, titanium alloys, nickel- and iron-based superalloys, copper alloys, refractory alloys, and platinum-based alloys. Most of these are used in pre-alloyed powder form, most often produced by gas atomization, or in wire form depending on the process [26, 27]. Although the number of alloys available is limited compared to wrought, many commonly used and well-known high-temperature and popular aerospace alloys are available. Supply-chain aspects and metal feedstock (powder or wire) are discussed in Chapter 6. Aluminum alloys have been used since the inception of the aerospace industry, and AM has brought new opportunities and challenges to its use. Aluminum alloys exhibit good strength-to-weight ratios and are used in many component applications [28]. Examples of aluminum alloys for use in AM processing include AlSi10Mg, F357, A205, 7A77, Al-6061-RAM2, Al-1000-RAM10, Scalmalloy, and many others that offer a range of strengths with successful AM builds [29–32]. However, aluminum alloys suffer from poor elevated-temperature capabilities and issues with weld repair, and in some high-strength aluminum alloys, poor SCC resistance is common. In addition, some of the AM aluminum alloys do not have the same forming advantages as wrought forms and are often more expensive. Stainless steels are used in aircraft and spacecraft components because of their good strength-to-weight ratios, ability to withstand elevated temperatures, and low costs. Various classes of stainless steels and specialty steels are often used with AM, including austenitic (i.e., 316L) and precipitation-hardened (PH) [33]. Stainless steels are used across components for engine and exhaust systems, hydraulic components, heat exchangers, landing gear systems, and structural joints. Stainless steels also exhibit high corrosion, oxidation, and wear resistances depending on the environment [34]. Industrial steels, as opposed to stainless steels, are the most produced metals in the world, at almost 30 times more than the next group of metals, aluminum
