3.5. DEVELOPMENT AND CHARACTERIZATION OF METALS FOR AM
Development and characterization of AM metals is required to fully understand a metal’s adaptation to AM, as understanding the metal after the build process alone does not provide the full story. The examination and optimization of a metal microstructure before, during, and after each stage of thermal processing and post-processing are essential in producing quality and repeatable parts. Optimization of the heat treatment and characterization of material properties are important in maturing metals for selection for various AM processes, as described in detail in Chapter 4. Design aspects are discussed in Chapter 7, and certification is addressed in Chapter 9, all of which tie together the proper development and characterization of AM metals. One obvious difference that may be apparent to designers is the visual difference of the surface finish compared to that of machined parts. The surface finish of AM-built metals is much rougher. Surface texture characteristics (Chapters 7 and 8), such as roughness and waviness, may look different for AM metals than for those produced with traditional manufacturing processes. The effects of surface texture, for example, in as-built rough surfaces, can be significant enough to impact material performance (Chapters 4 and 8). As with all metals, the performance of AM metals is influenced by multiple factors, such as surface texture, and microstructure differences due to variations in wall thicknesses should be taken into account for successful part design. The roughness texture is driven by localized melting of the metal, build parameters, and feedstock. The load-bearing area may be different from the measured area, as shown in Fig. 3.11. A consideration during design is the compatibility of the selected metal with the materials with which it may come into contact during manufacturing or operation. Some metals can experience embrittlement or corrosion, as discussed later in this chapter. Metal compatibility is of particular importan