3.6.2. HYDROGEN ENVIRONMENTAL EMBRITTLEMENT (HEE)
Hydrogen is a common propellant used in LREs and propulsion tank structures; therefore, it is important to understand its impacts on AM metals. Hydrogen embrittlement (HE) is a mechanism resulting in a decrease in the fracture toughness or ductility of a metal due to the presence of atomic hydrogen. HE through hydrogen–metal interactions can be classified into three broad categories: HEE, internal hydrogen embrittlement (IHE), and hydrogen reaction embrittlement (HRE) [64]. HEE occurs when metals are being exposed to a high-pressure gaseous hydrogen environment. IHE implies the degradation of certain mechanical properties that occurs as a result of the introduction of hydrogen into susceptible metals during forming or finishing operations of the manufacturing process. HRE is an irreversible degradation of certain mechanical properties due to a chemical reaction with hydrogen to form metallic compounds such as metal hydrides, and such damage can occur without an external applied stress. In contrast, the effects of HEE and IHE are similar in many instances, and they both require an external applied tensile stress for the HE effects to occur [64]. The severity of HE effects is influenced by material factors such as chemical composition, product forms, heat treatment, surface finish, and grain orientation. The severity of HE effects is also influenced by environmental factors such as pressure, temperature, and hydrogen gas purity. HEE is often seen in components when the metal is under the influence of an applied tensile stress and exposed to a gaseous hydrogen environment. High-pressure, high-temperature LRE components, in particular the regeneratively cooled chambers and nozzles that require very thin walls for cooling channels, provide an extreme and challenging high-pressure hydrogen environment for any metal [65].
