3.6.2.3. DEVELOPMENT OF HEE-RESISTANT AM ALLOYS
Metals Selection for HEE
The best way of preventing or mitigating HE is through metal selection. Selecting a composition that has inherent high resistance to HEE will reduce the need for sophisticated post-processing and constant monitoring for potential HEEinduced damage. The choice of iron–nickel-based alloys is justified, as alloys in this class generally exhibit a good balance between strength and resistance to HEE. NASA has been developing HEE-resistant AM Fe–Ni-based superalloys for LRE applications [65]. NASA HR-1 and JBK-75, both A-286 derivatives, are high-strength HEE-resistant wrought Fe–Ni superalloys, and have been selected for AM LRE components that require high HEE resistance [66]. NASA HR-1, originally developed at NASA in the 1990s, was derived from JBK-75, a HEE-resistant alloy, to increase strength and maintain high ductility in highpressure hydrogen environments [67].
Major Challenges
In addition to alloy compositions, localized chemical compositions (or chemical segregation), and microstructural inhomogeneities in AM metals are significant challenges that remain for the development of HEE-resistant AM alloys. Because of the rapid solidification nature of AM process, the as-built metals often exhibit a quenched dendritic microstructure (see Chapter 4) with varying degrees of chemical composition segregation and microstructural variation across the thickness [69]. Primary working at the mill for traditional metals converts the cast microstructure of the ingot into wrought microstructure through multiple thermomechanical processes. The microstructures of conventional wrought alloys are optimized during the primary working, which reduces any compositional segregation and microstructural inhomogeneities to very low levels. In contrast, the microstructures of AM metals can be optimized only by heat treatment, which can cause significant grain coarsening and nucleation of secondary phases, resulting in the possible elevation of HEE susceptibility in localized regions. Therefore, the development of an appropriate heat treatment that can minimize inhomogeneities in chemical composition and microstructure for AM metals is critical.
