3.6.2.4. FINAL REMARKS ON THE DEVELOPMENT OF HEE-RESISTANT AM ALLOYS
The cost and schedule savings resulting from AM are extremely attractive, and therefore, AM metals will replace some metals produced by conventional fabrication methods in the near future. However, changes in AM process parameters can significantly alter the material’s microstructure and affect its mechanical properties and HEE susceptibility. There are many unknowns in the metals produced by AM, and the existing knowledge on conventional bulk alloys may not be directly applicable to the assessment of the HEE characteristics of AM metals. Many issues need to be addressed for the development of HEE-resistant AM alloys, especially those related to chemical and microstructural inhomogeneities. Further research in terms of AM process optimization, post-AM heat treatment, microstructure characterization, and HEE testing is required to expedite the development of HEE-resistant AM alloys.
3.6.3. OXYGEN OR OXIDIZER COMPATIBILITY
At elevated concentrations and pressures, oxygen systems have a significant risk of fire and catastrophic failure. Many materials that are not flammable in air are readily ignited and sustain combustion in enriched oxygen environments. Material flammability is not a material property and can change dramatically depending on the oxygen concentration, pressure, temperature, and material thickness or configuration. There are ignition and flammability concerns with both metals and soft goods. This section focuses on the flammability and ignitability of metals only, but it must be noted that both metals and soft goods must be considered when evaluating system safety. In oxygen- or oxidizer-based systems,
