Flammability
Flammability is the characteristic of a material to sustain burning once it has become ignited. Flammability in metals is dependent on many aspects including temperature, oxygen concentration, pressure, flow rate, and metal thickness and configuration. Sources of data for metals flammability include ASTM MNL36, NFPA53, ASTM STP1561, and the NASA Materials and Processes Technical Information System (MAPTIS) [70–72]. Because AM metals are relatively new compared to other metal processes, there have only been a few studies of the flammability of metals produced by different AM methods. Much of the new alloy development, particularly for oxygen service, has been competitive and company proprietary. There has been limited testing in general and even less that is publicly available. In initial testing at NASA White Sands Test Facility (WSTF) of AM L-PBF-processed Inconel 718, there seemed to be possible effects on the flammability due to lot-to-lot variability in the powder stock and post-processing, particularly with regard to HIP. The interactions of these factors are not yet well understood or fully characterized. Going forward, it is important to understand the flammability characteristics of historical and future alloy systems. For better flammability data quality for specific applications, all post-processing relevant to the specific material application must be performed on flammability test specimens.
Ignitability
There are many heat sources to consider as ignition mechanisms in the operation of systems. The most common ignition mechanisms for metals, especially in propulsion systems, are particle impact, frictional ignition, and mechanical breakage.
Particle Impact
Particle impact is described in detail within ASTM MNL36. The primary characteristic elements are 1) particulates that can be entrained in flowing oxygen (flammable particulates are generally most severe), 2) gas velocities typically greater than 30.5 m/s (100 ft/s), 3) impact points ranging from 458 to perpendicular to the path of the particle, and 4) flammable target materials. This ignition mechanism is primarily active in the gaseous regions of a system. However, all regions of high-velocity oxidizer that impinge on flammable materials, gas or liquid, should be strongly considered. In propulsion systems, this mechanism is typically seen in high-velocity regions, such as oxygen-rich preburners, turbines, injectors, and tank ullage pressurization systems that rely on high-pressure oxidizer from the engines. Specifically for AM, one of the largest concerns with particle impact is loose surface-adherent particles that are not removed after the AM process. Most
