Frictional Ignition
Frictional ignition is heat that is generated when two more surfaces rub in the presence of an oxidizer until one or both of the metals ignite and sustain combustion. Metal pairs have been evaluated in frictional ignition for decades [75], but very little research has been performed on the friction ignition of AM-processed metal systems. It is unknown whether the risk of ignition from mechanical breakage is higher or lower for AM parts, and this issue must be better understood through testing.
Mechanical Breakage
It is unknown whether the risk of ignition from mechanical breakage is higher or lower for AM parts. It is best practice to avoid mechanical breakage of flammable materials in the nominal operation of oxidizer systems.
