3.6.5. CORROSION BEHAVIOR OF AM COMPONENTS
Corrosion is a consideration for various AM components during the manufacturing process and end use propulsion applications. With the unique fabrication of AM, corrosion must be better understood so that parts meet the intended design requirements. The focus of this section is on the effects of metallurgical characteristics and microstructural features, including grain size, surface roughness, inclusions, phases, porosity, chemical composition segregation, and residual stress, on the corrosion performance of AM components produced by the L-PBF and LP-DED processes [85–87]. As mentioned in the preceding text, build parameters have a significant effect on the microstructure features [88], and an optimum set of parameters is expected to improve the overall corrosion resistance of the AM parts. For this reason, broad studies are necessary on every aspect of each AM process and on the properties of AM products. An overview of the relationships among feedstock, microstructure, and environment for PBF-based processes is shown in Fig. 3.21. Typically, the corrosion performance of all metals is remarkably sensitive to AM processing parameters because of the influences of these parameters on the microstructural features, as shown in Fig. 3.21.
3.6.5.1. SURFACE ROUGHNESS
It is common for a rough surface to increase the surface electrochemical reactions between AM parts and the surrounding environment. To some extent, this is due to the increased surface roughness and surface area. These reactions can be interpreted as both localized and uniform corrosion attacks on the surface. Compared to the wrought products, AM parts have a rougher surface finish, which can be attributed to the staircase effect, balling phenomena, evaporation, and facing orientation. However, all of the mentioned impacts can be mitigated by choosing a proper set of AM processing parameters. Post-processing can also help mitigate these effects to reduce the roughness (see Chapters 5 and 8).
