3.6.5.2. GRAIN SHAPE AND SIZE
The formation of substructure and the impact of grain size on the corrosion resistance of AM parts are still controversial. The more accepted mechanism is based on a significantly higher density of subgrain boundaries that act as active surface sites for the nucleation and growth of a protective passive layer on as-fabricated stainless steel components, leading to better corrosion performance. Conversely, the elongated shape of grains in AM structures causes a harmful anisotropic behavior that should be addressed in SCC studies and crack growth rate calculations. To overcome this issue, the traditional combination of postprocessing heat treatment with severe plastic deformation may improve the corrosion performance through a grain refinement mechanism. However, such an approach may not be feasible for components produced using AM. The use of homogenization thermal treatments to provide a more uniform grain structure in various alloys is an effective method for helping to reduce this corrosion impact.
3.6.5.3. POROSITY
The porosity levels of AM parts depend highly on the process parameters and can be kept less than 1% by choosing the optimum set of parameters. Reports show that AM L-PBF stainless steel parts with low levels of porosity (,1%) can exhibit corrosion performance that is comparable to or even better than that of their traditional counterparts. Many other common AM alloys, such as Inconel 625, Inconel 718, CoCr, AlSi10Mg, and Ti6Al4V, can also be built with low
