3.6.5.4. RESIDUAL STRESS
Several variables, such as alloy strength, build direction, process parameters, and component geometry, affect the residual stress of AM components. Nonetheless, many complexities around the impact of structural residual stress on the corrosion behavior of AM metals are still not well understood. It is well known that the residual stress can induce distortion in the AM part and lead to regions with tensile or compressive stresses. These regions can act as microgalvanic couples and expedite the pit initiation process. On the other hand, a denser passive film is reported to be formed on the surface under compressive residual stress as a consequence of lowering of the point-defect concentration [90].
3.6.5.5. ALLOYING ELEMENTS AND CHEMICAL COMPOSITION SEGREGATION
AM components present challenges with chemical composition segregation because heating and cooling rates are very high for most AM processes. Higher-melting-point elements solidify first and concentrate in the center of dendrites or grains, whereas elements with lower melting points are enriched toward the interdendritic regions or grain boundaries. As a result, AM alloys are highly susceptible to microsegregation, which often results in poorer corrosion resistance because of the variations in composition from one region to the next [91]. It is well known that the outstanding corrosion performance of stainless steels can be attributed to the addition of at least 12% Cr to form a protective layer of chromium oxide, along with a high Ni content to stabilize the austenite phase. The presence of Mo in stainless steels is also critical for passive film formation, as it improves both general and localized corrosion performance. Carbon can lead to intergranular corrosion by precipitating carbides at grain boundaries during prolonged exposure to elevated temperatures. Oxygen may worsen localized corrosion attacks by decreasing the pitting potential of AM stainless steel parts. Some research has been conducted on the corrosion resistance of AM aluminum alloys. In L-PBF AlSi10Mg alloy [89], the microstructure displays a fine cellular–dendritic pattern with silicon located at the cellular–dendritic boundaries. Galvanic corrosion occurred in chloride solution, as the corrosion potential of the aluminum phase was lower than that of the cellular boundaries where silicon was located. Post-process heat treatments conducted on L-PBF
