3.6.5.6. PHASES
Because of the high cooling rate in L-PBF, the microstructure of 316L stainless steel parts fabricated by this method can be mainly austenitic with a proper set of processing parameters. In contrast, the cooling rate of the DED method is lower than that of the L-PBF method. Therefore, the DED process may promote the formation of some unfavorable phases such as intracellular d-ferrite, which has a higher corrosion rate than the austenite matrix in 316L stainless steel. Hence, a post-processing heat treatment should be performed to ensure a full austenite matrix and higher corrosion resistance. Formation of chromium and molybdenum carbides in L-PBF Inconel 718 can reduce corrosion resistance as these carbides deplete the chromium and molybdenum contents in the g-matrix [92]. Additionally, the presence of the grainboundary Laves phase also affects corrosion resistance [93]. As-built L-PBF Inconel 718 was characterized by a well-defined Laves phase network surrounding the g-matrix [92]. The as-built microstructure has been shown to have a worse corrosion resistance than the post-processed metals. The key factor contributing to the inferior corrosion resistance is anodic dissolution of the Laves phase. Dissolution of the Laves phase network through heat treatment can improve corrosion resistance. The presence of a grain-boundary h-phase (Ni3Ti) in fully heat-treated LP-DED NASA HR-1 superalloy is a serious concern, as the h-phase is brittle and has negative impacts on tensile ductility and HEE resistance [69]. The presence of the grain-boundary h-phase in the A-286 superalloy caused the passive layer to become unstable and, consequently, increased the susceptibility to localized corrosion [94]. More systematic research is needed to understand the effects of microstructural heterogeneity on corrosion behavior for AM alloys. Methods to mitigate precipitation of h-phase in LP-DED NASA HR-1, including
