3.6.5.7. INCLUSIONS
Because of the lower cooling rates employed in traditional manufacturing methods, the sizes of the Cr depletion zones and MnS inclusions are much larger in traditional stainless steel parts than in their AM-processed counterparts even without application of the optimum parameter set. This leads to a higher pitting resistance in AM-processed stainless steel components. On the other hand, the formation of nanoscale oxide inclusions enriched with Si, Al, and Mn has been reported. The nanoscale oxide inclusions in AM stainless steel components are much finer than those in conventional wrought or cast stainless steel parts. For this reason, their presence is not considered deleterious for stainless steel AM parts that need high pitting corrosion resistance in most industrial environments. However, the preferential dissolution of these inclusions along grain boundaries in some media such as high-temperature water in nuclear industries (.2808C) reportedly accelerated oxidation and caused extensive crack branching. The risk of the deterioration of corrosion properties should be considered in designing the post-processing heat-treatment process because the elevated-temperature environment facilitates the diffusion of alloying elements and inclusion formation.
3.6.5.8. SUMMARY OF CORROSION FOR AM
Variations in microstructure due to AM processes will significantly impact the mechanical properties, surface roughness, grain size, porosity, inclusions, residual stresses, chemical composition segregation, and phase formation, all of which will impact the corrosion behavior of the AM metal. Overall, more conclusive information is still needed to address this topic. The main limitations include the lack of a systematic method to reach a proper set of AM parameters for each alloy, the theories regarding the sources and effects of residual stress, the lack of understanding of the role and design of post-AM-build heat treatments, and the lack of data about the long-term exposure of AM parts to corrosive environments. Furthermore, because of the different alloys, environments, and measuring methods used in those studies, it is not possible to reach generalized conclusions, and this fact shows the necessity of designating a general standard for testing the corrosion performance of AM parts. Methods exist to reduce corrosion such as coatings, including AM processes to provide cladding or coating of surfaces with a noncorrosive metal or metal alloy [95].
3.7. SUMMARY
Many considerations are relevant to alloy selection and use in metal AM. Some of these considerations are the same as in conventional processing technologies, but
