4.1. INTRODUCTION
The basic metal additive manufacturing (AM) processes are summarized in Chapter 2, with a general overview and discussions of build volume, part geometry, feature size, and deposition rate. Guidelines are provided in Chapter 3 regarding the selection of AM metal alloys, microstructure differences between AM processes, and introduction to mechanical properties. This chapter discusses their interrelationship, as illustrated in Fig. 4.1. AM expands the interrelationship between material processing, microstructure, properties, and resultant performance to include that of materials engineering, further allowing users to design the metal alloy to meet end-use requirements. Throughout this chapter, metal alloys are referred to as metals, alloys, or metal alloys, and these terms are used interchangeably. Metals are selected to meet performance requirements on the basis of their physical, mechanical, and thermophysical properties. The mechanical strength of a metal is dependent on its crystal lattice
