structure and the ease with which dislocations form and propagate in response to applied loads. Metals are alloyed to form barriers within the microstructure to resist dislocation motion, which increases strength, at the expense of decreased ductility. These barriers are referred to as strengthening mechanisms. Two of the most prominent strengthening mechanisms in metal alloys in the aerospace industry are solid solution strengthening and age hardening. Solid-solutionstrengthened alloys rely on a homogeneous distribution of the alloying elements within the crystal structure of the matrix. In contrast, age hardening involves the precipitation of the alloying element(s) as intermetallic or secondary phases within the crystal structure of the matrix. Microstructures that form in AM processing are dominated by the localized rapid cooling of repeatedly deposited micrometer-sized melt pools. This rapid cooling results in elemental segregation in the form of dendrites, which depletes the matrix of its strengthening components [1]. This segregation can be alleviated through a homogenization heat treatment. Depending on the alloy and its strengthening mechanisms, other heat treatments may be required for optimization of the resulting microstructure and, hence, the properties.
4.2. DIFFERENCES IN AM METAL MICROSTRUCTURE
4.2.1. CONVENTIONAL WROUGHT OR CAST PRODUCT
Although AM microstructures are different than either cast or wrought metal microstructures of the same metal alloy, they share some similarities. In cast alloys, the component is formed inside a mold as molten metal solidifies. Dendritic structures, such as those illustrated in Fig. 4.2, result from chemical segregation with their spacing being inversely proportional to the cooling rate. As a metal alloy solidifies, the higher-melting-temperature constituents solidify first, forming the core of the dendrite. Because of the reduction in volume from a disordered liquid to an ordered crystalline solid, shrinkage voids can be trapped inside the component. Thus, casting microstructures are usually characterized by dendritic structures and voids. To alleviate this inhomogeneity, cast components typically undergo a hot isostatic pressing (HIP) process to both homogenize the metal alloy and reduce the void size. In castings, an inhomogeneous distribution of grain sizes results from heterogeneous nucleation of the solid along the colder mold surfaces. This promotes the formation of large, elongated columnar grains as the metal solidifies from the outer surfaces toward the center of the casting. Homogeneous nucleation of solid grains then occur in the last remaining liquid in the center, forming an equiaxed fine-grained microstructure. Depending on the operational requirements for mechanical performance, some microstructure modifications can be made by control of the cooling rate and direction. Figure 4.3 illustrates microstructural variations that can be achieved through process control in cast
