4.2.4. TYPICAL FLAWS OR DEFECTS IN AM METALS
Defects, reflecting an imperfection or abnormality that impairs quality, function, or utility, occur commonly in AM parts. These defects are related to the layerwise deposition of AM, with its repeated melting, solidification, and reheating. Common AM process defects have been studied, along with proposed mitigation strategies, for L-PBF [27 – 30], LP-DED [31 – 34], and AW-DED [35 – 40]. Most volumetric defects are either internal (voids) or on the surface (pores). Of the mechanical and physical properties, fatigue properties are most affected by defects. For example, surface roughness can become crack nucleation sites. Different types of internal defects are usually identified by their size and morphology and are presented in this section. Chapter 2 provides some information on defect mitigation techniques during builds for many AM processes. Common volumetric flaws across all AM platforms include lack of re-melt or lack of fusion (LOF) defects, excess internal voids due to trapped gases, keyhole voids, surface porosity (pores), and cracks. Volumetric defects typically result from nonoptimized process parameters, which are highly alloy-dependent. These flaws are common in several types of processes, including L-PBF AM processing, as illustrated in Fig. 4.14 for the XZ plane or Z direction [41]. Low melt-pool depth, depicted in Fig. 4.14a, can result in incomplete bonding between consecutive adjacent layers and potentially form LOF defects. Typically, this is an indication that the energy density is too low for the selected material and layer thickness. Melt-pool depth can be increased by changing the L-PBF parameters to increase energy density. However, an energy density that is too high can lead to excessive melt-pool depth and keyhole porosity. A higher melt-pool depth is shown in Fig. 4.14b. Optimized parameters balance a desired melt-pool depth that minimizes defects with a practical deposition rate and burn-out prevention, particularly for downward-facing surfaces.
