4.4. METAL ALLOY PROPERTY OVERVIEW
As a caveat to this section, note that the nature of AM results in varying microstructures, even when identical process parameters are employed for AM metals. A simple change in design parameters (e.g., build orientation, layer time interval, part size and geometry) can influence the thermal history and can, consequently, change the microstructure, defect formation, and defect distribution. As microstructure controls the resulting mechanical behavior, methods to address this variability are still an active area of research. Chapter 2 discusses some of the defects seen with metal AM processes, and Chapter 3 highlights how these defects can manifest in the final material properties. This known variability in microstructure and, hence, properties highlights an important challenge in the design and qualification of AM parts, as discussed in more detail in Chapter 9. This challenge is also mentioned in the Additive Manufacturing Standardization Collaborative (AMSC) roadmap under gap NDE8 regarding the fabrication of laboratory specimens with defect distribution similar to that of a critical location of an actual part and is given a medium priority, which requires research and development to address the knowledge gap [68].
