4.4.1.1. QUASI-STATIC PROPERTIES AND TESTING STANDARDS
ASTM International is one of the largest voluntary standards-developing organizations in the world. This technical organization oversees the development of standards for testing procedures and specimen geometries. As the need for test methods evolve, requests are made from trade associations, government agencies, and other professional societies that do not create their own standards. These requests can also come from manufacturers, consumer groups, and even individuals. As requests are made, they are forwarded to the appropriate ASTM technical committee, which may assign a subtask group to begin the development process. The various technical committees also periodically review existing test standards and provide updates to ensure compliance with advances in technology and requirements. The standardization of testing procedures and test specimens ensures that data produced by various laboratories are reliable, consistent, and repeatable. Quasi-static mechanical properties are obtained in accordance with applicable testing standards such as ASTM E111 [72] for the determination of the elastic modulus and ASTM E8/E8M [73] for the determination of strength and ductility. The elastic modulus is the linear response of a crystalline metal to an applied load and is governed by the atomic bond distance and strength within the matrix material. Therefore, the modulus is unaffected by the processing unless the processing severely modifies either the texture or void content. Because both are modified in AM metals, some consideration should be made relative to expected variations. ASTM standard E8/8M provides guidelines for specimen geometry and testing conditions used in the tension testing of metals [73]. Numerous studies have investigated size reduction in AM tensile specimens, with some sizes shown in Fig. 4.25 in comparison to the E8/8M standard. Because of the variations in microstructure incurred with AM, typical test-specimen geometries are being re-evaluated. As the part geometry has a significant impact on the heat profile during a build, this affects the microstructure and, hence, the properties; therefore, obtaining a representative test or witness specimen for an AM part becomes challenging. Because the thermal gradients during a build influence the resulting properties of an AM metal, the use of miniaturized specimens allows test specimens to be extracted from the actual build geometry. If a complete build cannot be sacrificed for a first article cut up to obtain specimens for mechanical testing, witness coupons are incorporated onto the build plate. To ensure that the reduced size of the specimen does not influence the properties, well-established correlations can be used. Provisions in ASTM standard E8 specify the gauge length to be 4 times the specimen diameter according to E8 and 5 times the specimen diameter according to E8M. Subsized specimens are scaled by either 4
