 horizontally built specimens. In vertically built specimens, the orientation of the columnar grains is parallel to the loading direction, which makes it easier for the grains to glide along the grain boundaries upon loading and can result in higher elongation to failure [9, 18, 48]. The formation and characteristics of both surface and volumetric defects are strongly influenced by the build orientation. For instance, parts fabricated in the diagonal direction (i.e., 458 with respect to the build plate) typically exhibit a greater surface roughness on the “downskin” surface compared to the “upskin” surface. Although the higher value of surface roughness is not as detrimental to tensile strength, it typically has a detrimental effect on ductility as well as fatigue resistance, as the cracks can preferentially initiate from the downskin surfaces [85, 86].
