4.4.1.4. HOW THE AM PROCESS AFFECTS FATIGUE PROPERTIES
As discussed in Sec. 4.2.4, a major characteristic of metal alloys fabricated using AM is the heterogeneity of structural features arising from voids, LOF, surface roughness, and microstructural variations [34, 85]. These factors create more uncertainty in the fatigue behavior of AM parts, thereby increasing the challenge of designing robust and structurally sound products. Therefore, characterizing and integrating the specific effects of defects, surface topography, and microstructural features on the fatigue behavior of AM parts are crucial to expediting the wider industrial adoption of AM, specifically in fatigue-critical applications. In general, AM metal alloys differ in performance from wrought metal alloys under cyclic loading because of their 1) rougher surface finish, 2) volumetric defects, and 3) microstructure [90]. Surface roughness is one of the main factors detrimentally influencing the fatigue strength of AM parts [86]. Both the surface roughness and the volumetric defects act as stress risers, significantly lowering the fatigue performance by substantially reducing the initiation time for fatigue cracks to form [91]. In the presence of surface roughness, cracks typically initiate from the surface, resulting in considerable knockdowns in fatigue performance [15]. When LOF defects are present in the material, the crack initiation site may shift to these internal defects, also resulting in reduced fatigue performance [66, 92]. Finally, the microstructure surrounding these stress risers can also influence the stress field, thereby affecting the fatigue behavior [93]. Generally, AM parts experience higher cooling or solidification rates than their conventional counterparts, producing microscale segregation and finer grain formation. The repeated deposition, solidification, and reheating can result in columnar grains in AM components. The resulting microstructure is sometimes described as “uneven” [94 – 100]. Although post-AM manufacture heat treatments are used to alter the as-deposited microstructure [65, 66, 101, 102], the variations from deposition sometimes persist. This section provides an overview of the influencing factors and the degree to which they affect the fatigue behavior of AM metal alloys. This includes the effects of surface roughness, internal defects, and microstructure and their variations caused by feedstock characteristics, geometry, build orientation, post-processing, and loading type.
