4.4.1.4.5. AM process parameters
In terms of fatigue properties, the grain boundary orientation relative to the loading direction, especially with the presence of brittle phases at the boundaries, can affect the growth behavior of fatigue cracks by causing them to propagate in an intergranular fashion. In such cases, higher FCG rates in the Paris regime, or steady-state region, can be observed, as illustrated in Fig. 4.33 [65, 85, 132]. Build-orientation-induced anisotropy in the fatigue resistance of AM parts can also arise in the presence of LOF defects, which are typically formed between layers or between adjacent melt pools, perpendicularly to the build direction [16, 84, 85]. The stress concentration resulting from the edges of these LOF defects may be higher when the layer orientation is perpendicular to the loading direction (i.e., vertically built specimens) compared to when it is parallel (i.e., horizontally built specimens) as illustrated in Fig. 4.35c [84]. On the other hand, because of the more spherical morphology of keyholes and entrapped-gas voids, the anisotropy in the fatigue resistance of AM metal alloys may be diminished in the absence of LOF defects when keyholes and entrapped-gas voids remain [84, 111]. Figure 4.35a illustrates the orientation of LOF defects and the resulting stress concentration with respect to the loading direction for vertically and horizontally built specimens. Figs. 4.35b and c show the effects of build orientation on the fatigue resistance of AM parts of different metal alloys in the as-built and machined surface conditions.
