4.4.1.4.8. Summary
In summary, the combination of the layer orientation of AM parts and the type of loading should be considered in the overall evaluation of fatigue performance because internal and surface defects can align with or be perpendicular to the maximum shear or tensile loading planes. Proper understanding of the damage mechanisms of the metal alloy is essential for a robust and successful fatigue-life estimation. Finally, and perhaps most importantly, if a multiaxial, cumulative, or mean stress damage model provides a good correlation for the part design with wrought metal alloy, its applicability to the part design with AM is not certain. AM metal alloys often have different failure mechanisms, but they may also exhibit more anisotropic behavior and less ductility than their wrought counterparts.
