4.4.2.6. HOW THE AM PROCESS AFFECTS THERMOPHYSICAL PROPERTIES
Because small volumetric voids are commonly encountered in the AM process, it is expected, and has been shown, that the density of AM alloys can be lower than that of wrought metal alloys. Density measurements can be used to determine how much void volume is within an AM part. Although this does not provide spatially resolved information regarding the location of the voids, it is helpful in establishing optimized process parameters for producing components with 98 –99% theoretical density (%TD). The simple rule of mixtures can be used to determine the effect of density on the physical properties of randomly oriented, crystalline metals. Because the density, r, of an AM part is used in the calculation of thermal conductivity, k, this thermal property can also be affected. An additional factor in AM parts is the resulting residual stress from the temperature gradients. As residual stress and strain are known to affect thermal conductivity, variations in this term are also expected in AM components. Resulting variations in k also affect a. Because of the extremely high cooling rate of the L-PBF AM process (106 107 K/s [162]), nonequilibrium states and supersaturated solid solutions [163] can exist. Those unique microstructures lead to either enhanced or reduced thermal conductivity values for the as-fabricated L-PBF components. Thermal diffusivity and conductivity values are less sensitive to the AM build direction.
4.4.2.7. CASE STUDY WITH REDUCED THERMAL CONDUCTIVITY FOR AS-FABRICATED L-PBF PARTS
Copper alloy C-18150 (Cu – 1.5 wt% Cr– 0.2 wt% Zr) is a precipitation-hardened CuCrZr alloy with dilute Cr and Zr elements. As heat is mainly carried by electrons in metals, a small addition of solute atoms can dramatically increase electrical resistivity and markedly reduce k. According to calculations using the calculation of phase diagrams (CALPHAD software) methodology, under equilibrium conditions, the dominant phase of C-18150 alloy at room temperature is the Cu FCC phase, together with Cu5Zr, and Cr2Zr precipitates. Because of the rapid cooling process associated with AM, as-fabricated C-18150 samples have supersaturated Cr and Zr solute atoms in the Cu matrix, which significantly scatter electrons. This supersaturated nonequilibrium state leads to a muchreduced k value [ 100 W/(m . K)] for as-built L-PBF C-18150 parts at room temperature [164].
