General considerations for evaluating AM parts can be found in SAE International Aerospace Information Report AIR7352, “Additively Manufactured Component Substantiation” [169]. This guidance document provides general considerations for substantiation of the integrity of AM parts. It includes comments on process controls and references specific SAE AM process specifications. Additionally, it emphasizes the need for fracture-control rationale for fracturecritical AM parts and recognizes the need for the evaluation of fracture properties that are representative of the hardware build process. For NASA applications, detailed requirements for AM processes are defined in NASA-STD-6030 and NASA-STD-6033 (Chapter 9). AM fracture-critical hardware would be expected to meet these standards or an equivalent industry standard to satisfy material process-control requirements. Rigorous process-control requirements mitigate risks associated with variability in hardware manufacture as it relates to material performance. With specific reference to NASASTD-6030, an Additive Manufacturing Control Plan (AMCP) outlines the manufacturing requirements, quality control, and quality assurance (verification) for the hardware. An AMCP, or its equivalent, provides the command media necessary for process controls related to the AM part. Additionally, NASA-STD-6030 requires a Part Production Plan (PPP) identifying nondestructive evaluation (NDE), proof testing, witness sample testing, metallurgical process qualifications, and material property characterization requirements for the AM hardware. Collectively, the AMCP, PPP, and part drawing provide process controls unique to the build that govern the part production process. This detailed level of process control is a necessary element in a fracture-control program for a fracture-critical AM part. This is discussed in more detail in Chapter 9. Specifically, with respect to fracture-control requirements, NASA-STD-6030 precludes AM parts from being classified as Non-Fracture Critical Low Risk according to NASA-STD-5019, or as Fracture Critical Lines, Fittings and Other Pressurized Components according to NASA-STD-5019. This requires that all AM parts should be evaluated in accordance with fracture-critical requirements. This is due to the classifications being rooted in the assumption that the parts are manufactured with mature, well-characterized alloys and processes to minimize risks associated with defect frequency and distribution. At this time, processcontrol risks for human spaceflight associated with AM are not sufficiently mature to take advantage of the assumed low probability of defects required by these part classifications in NASA-STD-5019. Alternative approaches that may use some of the logic of these classifications may be used with proper review by the responsible fracture-control engineering organization.
