4.5.5. FLAW SCREENING
Defects in AM parts can take the form of voids, surface porosity, microcracks, unmelted powder, LOF, and/or excessive spatter contaminants that occur during the process. These are observable defects in micrographs that can help determine the process or feedstock root cause. The type, size, and distribution of defects varies with the alloy and process conditions, as discussed in Sec. 4.2.4. In fracture-critical AM parts, methods of flaw detection capable of finding the critical defects must be implemented. AM parts in fracture-critical spaceflight hardware must receive surface and volumetric inspections for manufacturing flaws or defects. Typically, flaw screening is accomplished through quantitative NDE, as discussed in Chapter 5. Such methods are supported by a statistical probability of detection and confidence level determined for an inspection method with a prescribed methodology. For AM parts, these techniques usually include x-ray radiography, x-ray CT, ultrasonic testing (UT), and liquid penetrant testing. Specific NDE techniques applied to AM parts are discussed in Chapter 5. Standard NDE flaw sizes for metallic materials in spaceflight hardware are defined in NASA-STD-5009 [174]. NASA-STD-6030 Class A parts must meet the special NDE requirements of NASA-STD-5009, which requires substantiation of the capability of the technique. The NDE approach for a fracture-critical part should be documented in
