the PPP. Note that manufacturing process controls alone are generally not recognized as an acceptable substitute for quantitative NDE in Class A parts. Additionally, proof testing may be used as a flaw screening technique. In general, proof testing is applied as a part of acceptance testing to validate workmanship and is required for all NASA-STD-6030 Class A and Class B AM parts. Proof testing as a quantitative flaw screening approach for Class A parts is based on the premise that flaws that do not cause failure during the proof test would survive the subsequent service life cycles. However, a proof test is only a single cycle demonstrating that a part can withstand the static load with some margin. This approach requires the stress state of the hardware during the proof test to exceed the limiting stress state of the hardware during flight by a factor high enough to demonstrate mission life capability. The approach generally requires testing of flawed samples to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proof factor. Quantitative flaw screening by proof testing requires careful consideration of the proof stress states, flight stress states, proof test environment, flight environment, and failure mode (i.e., leak or burst). Proof testing as a flaw detection method requires coordination with structures and fracture-control representatives. Finally, flaw screening may be accomplished through material process controls. This approach may be based on destructive evaluation of witness coupons and periodic destructive evaluation of parts. In this approach, the size and type of defect associated with the process build can be estimated and used as a basis for the safe-life analysis. In general, this approach carries some elevated risk because it assumes that the defects found in the witness coupons and destructive evaluation of parts are representative of what is in the flight part. As noted in the preceding text, flaw screening by process control is not recognized as an acceptable substitute for quantitative NDE in Class A parts. Flaw screening based on process controls requires coordination with materials and fracturecontrol representatives.
4.5.6. SAFE-LIFE EVALUATION
Safe-life evaluation consists of determining the service-life capability of a part containing a flaw. This can be done by test or analysis. Test approaches typically employ a specimen that is designed to be a reasonable analog of the part or structure with an as-manufactured defect or an artificially introduced defect in the part. The analog specimen is then subjected to service-life load cycles and environments. The test can be conducted until a cyclic failure occurs, or it may be terminated after the required mission life factor is reached (typically 4 times service life). More typically, safe-life evaluations are conducted by analysis employing commercial analysis software. Analysis software uses numerical techniques and analytical models based on fracture mechanics to estimate crack growth due to a part’s cyclic stress spectrum, part geometry, flaw geometry, and AM alloy fracture properties. Mission life capability is determined on the basis of service cycles
