4.5.7. FRACTURE-CONTROL SUMMARY
Fracture control for AM parts must be grounded in material and part production process controls. Rigorous process control in accordance with government or industry specifications is required to mitigate risk against variability in the AM product throughout a given build and from build to build. Traditional fracture properties can be obtained through sample testing and should be part of material property databases developed from qualified material processes. Flaws in parts should be screened through quantitative surface and volumetric NDE. In some cases, proof testing or material process controls may serve as a flaw screen. Mission life requirements can be verified through conventional safe-life analysis based on the crack growth rate and fracture toughness test data representative of the AM process and build or through a test program of AM parts with artificial or as-manufactured defects.
4.6. CONCLUSIONS
This chapter has presented an overview of the various metallographic characteristics of metal AM and their subsequent influence on the physical and mechanical behavior of AM metal alloys. Various factors that influence the resulting microstructure and, hence, the resulting properties include the process selected and the process parameters, in addition to the geometry of the part. In general, the mechanical properties of metal AM parts can meet near-wrought or cast properties when proper processing and heat treatments are applied. This provides some guidelines for material properties when establishing a baseline design in addition to considerations for selection of a metal AM process. As with any design application, the material requirements of an AM part are also dictated by the operational requirements and the impact of the operating environment. Some applications, such as load-bearing components or fatigue applications, require more stringent requirements and validation of properties.
