5.1. OVERVIEW
The focus of additive manufacturing (AM) tends to reside in the design and build process, but the key to successful part implementation goes well beyond the build alone. Post-processing involves many critical operations that must be understood for metal AM parts to produce desired geometry and metallurgical characteristics to be considered for flight worthiness. Post-processing is a term that encompasses subsequent steps that occur after the completion of the build process. This includes all the necessary steps immediately after the last layer has been deposited during printing and ends with the part being placed into service. One of the challenges with post-processing is the variation depending on the part design, metal alloy, AM process, and requirements for the operational environment. Depending on the requirements for the parts, many applications require post-processing to obtain the desired material properties or geometric performance. The objective of this chapter is to introduce the various post-processing methods, how they are used, and some of the rationale for why they are used. This will help the engineer distill and trade the importance of each. Post-processing can add considerable cost to a part, so all steps must be carefully considered to understand the implications for the end application [1]. The post-processes discussed herein include build unpacking, powder removal and cleaning, build-plate and support removal, heat treatments, machining, cleaning, welding, brazing and diffusion bonding, inspections, nondestructive evaluation (NDE), and surface enhancements (or polishing techniques). Post-processing is one of the critical areas that has inherent risks and can contribute to scrap rate if not planned properly. Parts can fail during the build process, but it is more painful to the engineer when a build is completed successfully, only to find that there is trapped powder that cannot be removed because of a design flaw. This is one of many issues during post-processing that need to be considered. How does one determine whether the powder is adequately evacuated
