The built part is buried in powder, and the first step after opening the process chamber is to gradually raise the build platform while removing powder from around the part in a process known as “unpacking” (Fig. 5.3). A scoop is used to manually transfer powder from the build chamber to the overflow. Some machines come equipped with a powder vacuum (Fig. 5.4) system within a glovebox, which allows the operator to remove the bulk powder by plunging the suction house inlet into the powder locations. The build platform is gradually raised, and as surrounding powder is removed, powder inside the part drains from horizontal downward-angled passages or drain ports. Eventually, the build is raised to the topmost position with the build plate fully exposed. The area is further scooped, swept, and vacuumed clean until the bolts holding down the build plate are accessible for removal. Bolt removal can be difficult, particularly when parts have a high cross-sectional area in contact with the build plate, as this will transmit residual stress loads to the plate and induce substantial plate warpage, particularly if the plate is thin. After the bolts are removed, the build is ready to be removed from the process chamber. Larger parts can be very heavy, and it is recommended that a moveable lift table be used to transport the build in such cases. For large-format platforms (e.g., EOS M400, GE Concept Laser X-line 2000R), the build plates have threaded holes to accept eye bolts, along with lifting straps to remove the build using an overhead crane. Next, the part is transported for a more thorough powder removal.
