5.5.3.1. BORESCOPE
Visual inspection works to view interior passages for parts with simple geometries and large openings; however, simple geometries are rarely the case, as AM is often used for complex parts. An optical borescope with a built-in light source is an excellent cost-effective tool for inspecting internal passages for powder. The cost of a borescope increases with decreasing probe size (down to 0.5 mm), and borescopes may not be able to inspect very small or difficult-to-access passages that are not straight or that have severe curvature. The channel must have sufficient area to allow movement, and the borescope will tend to hang up on the rough walls of the internal geometry. Therefore, other inspection methods are used to verify that powder has been sufficiently removed from an AM build. An example of trapped power observed with a borescope is displayed in Fig. 5.10.
