5.6.5. CHEMICAL MILLING AND CHEMICAL MECHANICAL POLISHING
The use of chemical milling in conjunction with chemical mechanical polishing (CMP) is another effective method of eliminating support structures and partially sintered or melted powder from AM components. These technologies use specific chemical formulations capable of reacting with the metal at a uniform and specific rate, dissolving the metal, and achieving a certain level of planarization on the exposed surface. Chemical milling is a technology that has been widely used in the aerospace sector for decades. The chemical formulations are usually combinations of acids or alkaline solutions that dissolve the targeted metal very effectively. The reactions are usually dependent on temperature and are usually exothermic; therefore, they are typically performed in temperature-controlled baths to regulate the temperature and the metal removal rates. With this application, the component is immersed in the bath for the specific amount of time necessary to dissolve the support structure. During the immersion process, the chemical formulation must contact all of the surfaces at any given time to ensure uniform surface material removal. The solution can also be pumped into internal passages to remove support structures or residual powder in inaccessible regions. The exothermic nature of the reaction must be considered, because the reaction can get out of control at elevated temperatures in the internal passages. If performed correctly, this is a relatively easy and effective method of removing support structures; however, there is a safety and environmental risk involved in using dangerous chemicals. The produced toxic byproducts require careful handling and disposal. It is essential to mention that, very often, the point of contact
