5.7. HEAT TREATMENTS
The AM process completes three aspects of production: it generates a near-net- or near-final-shape part, in the desired chemistry, with the desired density. To obtain the thermomechanical properties of a specific alloy, post-process heat treatments are required to create a desirable microstructure. These structure–property relationships are process-dependent, and each AM process and metal, or metal alloy, combination has an individually tailored series of heat treatments. The heat treatments are metal-alloy-specific and can also be specific to the desired properties in the end-use application. Heat treatment can include stress relief, homogenization or hot isostatic pressing (HIP), solution annealing, and aging. The heat treatments are introduced in Chapter 3 and covered in great depth in Chapter 4. There is a specific order in which these treatments must be performed. The typical order of heat treatments for AM components is shown in Fig. 5.21. Prior to any heat-treatment operations, parts should be thoroughly degreased and dried to prevent undesirable chemical reactions at elevated temperature. If a clean surface finish is desired, the parts can be wrapped in a sacrificial metal foil that has a high affinity for oxygen to prevent oxidation of the part. Foil wrapping is particularly important for oxygen-, carbon-, nitrogen-, and moisturesensitive metals such as Al-, Ti-, Nb-, Mo-, and Ta-based alloys [25, 26]. As discussed in Chapter 4, residual stresses result from the rapid solidification of the melt pool that generates stressed grains, which, in combination with the geometric difference between the heated part during printing and the ambienttemperature part post-print, results in stresses throughout the part. The stress relief most commonly occurs after the powder has been properly removed and verified but before the part has been removed from the build plate. If the stressrelief cycle is not completed, the stress will be mechanically relieved through warping and cracking of the part, which is what the stress-relief cycle is meant to avoid. The magnitude of the residual stresses can be considerable and can induce significant part and build-plate warping for even thick geometries. Some
