5.8. BUILD-PLATE REMOVAL
All metal AM processes require a build plate that provides a substrate onto which parts are built. It is important to understand how and when this build plate will be removed post-print, so it can be properly integrated into the design or used for other post-processing operations, such as a fixture for machining. After components have been built, have had powder removed, and have undergone stress relief, the next step is to remove the build plate. It should be noted that powder removal and verification is critical prior to the build-plate removal, because this step could introduce fluids that could cause powder caking or concretion. Options for build-plate removal include horizontal band saw, wire-EDM, and break-away methods [27].
5.8.1. BAND SAW
The build plate is attached to a fixture block, also known as a tombstone, that has the same threaded hole pattern as the build plate. For build plates without bolt hole patterns custom fixtures are normally required. The block is placed in the vice, and the position is adjusted to where the band saw will traverse along the build plate surface to minimize the material to be cut from the part while allowing the surface of the build plate to be refinished for reuse. Generally, a band saw thickness of 3.175 mm (1/8 in.) is used. A sacrificial feature, known as a sacrificial base, is printed between the AM part and the build plate so that no cuts are made into the desired part geometry. (This is an example of post-processing information needed by the design engineer.) The thickness of the sacrificial base must be at least 3.5 mm to allow the cut to remove the part from the plate but also provide additional material from which the rough surface can be machined to the desired datum and surface finish. As with any use of a band saw, cutting fluid must be applied generously to ensure a clean and straight cut, particularly with harder metals such as nickel-based superalloys. Some alloys can be cut dry prior to stress relief to aid with powder removal. Plates can often exhibit significant warping due to residual stress in the part. The degree of warping can make a cut plan difficult because the blade would make a nonuniform cut. This is another reason for stress-relief heat treatment prior to plate removal, because not only is the part distortion reduced but so is the build plate distortion, resulting in a more
