5.8.3. PART SACRIFICIAL BASE
Since bases are sacrificial, it behooves the engineer to minimize the volume in order to minimize the print time, machining time, and waste. Bases generally taper from the part bottom cross section to a reduced cross section on the build plate. As described previously, it is important for the engineer to minimize the part surface area in contact with the build plate to minimize the residual stresses transmitted from the part to the build plate commonly responsible for plate warpage. In addition, a tapered base aids engagement of a band saw blade or an EDM wire during cutting. Caution must be taken that the base is sufficiently robust to survive the AM process stress loads and not debond from the build plate. Bases can also be designed to include powder drainage passages, making powder removal from the part far more feasible.
5.8.4. BREAK-AWAY PART BASES
If parts have a high aspect ratio and are not very large, the parts can be printed on a tapering base to allow the parts to be broken off the build plate. Such a breakaway base (Fig. 5.24) should taper down to an angle constrained by selfsupporting overhang surface angles to a contact point approximately 2 mm in diameter. The small part-to-plate contact area allows the specimens to be removed by hand by rocking them back and forth until they break off, often within only one or two cycles. It must be noted that break-away supports are not recommended for complex geometries, particularly those with multiple contact points. Geometries that are spatially asymmetric along the X and Y axes can induce distortion in the part prior to stress-relief heat treatment. If the breakaway part bases are sufficiently tapered to minimize contact to the build plate (and minimize build plate damage during part removal), in some cases the build plate can be reused without the
