5.8.5. POST-CUT PART CLEANING
After the build plate has been removed, the AM parts will be coated in cutting fluids and/or chips and powder. These parts should undergo a simple cleaning with compressed gas (normally air) followed by water. Depending on part complexity, parts can be sonicated in isopropyl alcohol. Parts must be clean of debris and fully dried before heat treatment to prevent contaminating furnaces or inducing surface chemical interactions at elevated temperature. Further discussion is included in Sec. 5.11, “AM Part Cleaning.”
5.8.6. BUILD-PLATE RESURFACING
Resurfacing of the build plate usually involves milling away contact points from previous support structures, followed by a grinding step to ensure the plate has been sufficiently leveled to return to service. Following the resurfacing steps, the plate may be returned to service until it has become sufficiently thin that warpage is unavoidable, at which point the plate may be scrapped or sent to metal recycling.
5.8.7. CASE STUDY: GREEN PROPULSION CHAMBER
An example is provided in this section to give the reader an idea of how all of the different processes can culminate into design decisions that affect all of the aspects discussed considering AM part design, powder removal, and part removal from the build plate. The goal is printing a well-designed green propulsion thrust chamber from Ti6Al4V using L-PBF AM, as shown in Fig. 5.25. The chamber is small, so the base plate thickness is set to 4 mm to allow for band-saw removal. Sufficient metal is included for final machining. The chamber could be printed directly to the build plate by extruding the cross-sectional projection at the bottom of the chamber by 4 mm; however, the resulting hollow cylinder will trap the powder within the chamber. The nozzle throat diameter is very small, and although powder could be drained through the throat, it would be like trying to drain all of the salt from a salt shaker through the cap holes: although achievable, it would take a long time. Therefore, the base features perforated channels to facilitate powder draining. The channel overhang surface angles are 358 from the Z axis to ensure a successful print, the inner and outer diameters taper slightly to minimize waste, and changes in geometry are filleted to minimize stress concentrations that could cause sufficient distortion to damage the recoater blade and fail the build. The reduction in mass of the base plate also decreases the amount of material through which the band saw must cut, preserving the band saw life and optimizing the overall plate removal process.
