5.9. NONDESTRUCTIVE EVALUATION (NDE)
5.9.1. UNIQUE CONSIDERATIONS FOR NDE IN AM
NDE is the application of a variety of physics-based methods to interrogate the internal structure of a part without alteration. It is most commonly used in aerospace to screen for defects just after the manufacturing of a part or periodically throughout the service life. The discipline is closely tied to damage tolerance and fracture control. In the risk-averse yet weight-sensitive fields of aviation and spaceflight, knowing something about what defects may exist in a part is crucial to optimizing the safety and capability of the system. The ability to nondestructively detect possible flaws in AM parts provides a valuable quality control step, which can be key to the safe use and application of the technology. Various NDE methods exist, and their application to metal AM, in particular to aerospace and propulsion components, is discussed in this section. When any new manufacturing process, material, or part design is developed, the NDE techniques must be tailored to some extent to optimize the response. One particular challenge that prompted the development of several new NDE methods was the advent of composites manufacturing [29]. In this case, particular inspection needs and challenges arose because of the inhomogeneous layered construction, complicated and often manual manufacturing process, and highly process-dependent properties. Similarly, AM poses specific unique challenges for NDE, primarily because it represents a paradigm shift from traditional, subtractive, top-down manufacturing to more of a bottom-up manufacturing approach [30].
