To illustrate the significant difference in approaches, picture a box. Its sides are smooth, continuous surfaces with perfectly flat, orthogonal faces. This represents the raw stock that would be inspected, either at a vendor or upon delivery, before the beginning of a manufacturing process. The smooth, flat surfaces offer an ideal scanning surface for ultrasonic or eddy-current probes and clear indications of cracks using dye penetrant. The orthogonal surfaces simplify the interpretation of radiographs. As critical machining or joining processes are completed, the part can be periodically inspected, catching defects as they are formed. By the time the part is finished, even if its geometry is complex, there can be some confidence that it was created without critical defects. Further inspections are made easier thanks to the smooth surface finish and full density (i.e., low porosity). Now, picture the rectangular box again. Imagine opening one side like a lid, reaching inside, and pulling out a rubber duck. It has curved surfaces, hollow areas, and thin walls, not to mention a porous and imperfect surface. This will be much more difficult to inspect. In this analogy, the rubber duck is representative of an as-built AM part. These inspectability challenges are compounded when you consider the true size and complexity of AM propulsion parts and the nature of AM process variability. Any one (or combination) of these areas could be the possible source of flaws or errors. Inspecting a complex part after the manufacturing process presents an uphill battle of limited access and many unknowns. In cases such as this, CT is generally used as the catch-all NDE method, capable of the inspection and interpretation of complex geometries. CT is a very good solution in many cases, and we discuss its capabilities at length in this section, as well as some limitations. Most NDE technologies present an inherent trade-off between depth of inspection and resolution. The high-energy x-r