5.9.2. WHY USE NDE?
There are many different applications of NDE. Most commonly, it is used to screen for defects to inform damage tolerance assessment and certification. It can also be used to investigate the presence of voids, cracks, trapped powder, and foreign object debris (FOD) in AM hardware to inform build and processing steps in a quality management system. Finally, it can be used for dimensional metrology to verify dimensions or reverse engineer a three-dimensional model of a part.
5.9.3. DAMAGE TOLERANCE AND CERTIFICATION
Damage tolerance is widely used in the aerospace industry, where a high failure margin approach to design could prohibit the use of lightweight structures needed for flight. For more details about damage tolerance, see Chapters 4 and 9. Damage tolerance is based on the assumption that flaws exist in any structure and that knowing the size and location of those flaws is key to calculating the safe life of a component. The critical initial flaw size that will lead to failure should be determined through damage tolerance analysis, as discussed in Chapter 4 [32]. NDE is used to screen for defects of that size. Therefore, it is critical to verify that an NDE method is capable of detecting defects at least as small as the critical initial flaw size using an approach such as the probability-of-detection (POD) methodology. Damage tolerance is also called “safety by inspection” [33]. A crucial aspect of this is understanding if a part can be re-inspected throughout its lifetime. Often, spaceflight hardware can be inspected only prior to entry into service. Single-use rocket engine components need to be designed to withstand the harsh environments and extreme loading of their initial use, but their service life does not need to be very long. On-orbit components such as satellites or habitats need to be designed to last their entire service life without re-inspection, although in-space inspection technologies are emerging. Reusable components such as airplanes, space capsules, and ground support equipment can be re-inspected throughout their service life, so the damage tolerance approach often accounts for this and requires routine inspection intervals.
5.9.4. TRAPPED POWDER, VOIDS, CRACKS, AND FOD
NDE is often used to screen for flaws created during the manufacturing process. Such an approach can be used to better understand the AM process and determine ideal parameters. It can also be used before and after heat treatment to assess the
