5.9.5. DIMENSIONAL METROLOGY
NDE can also be used to verify the geometry of a part. This is an important consideration for AM. With traditional subtractive manufacturing, the part can be measured before and after each manufacturing step, and very precise machining can be executed. Since the AM process needs to account for metal consolidation to predict the resulting geometry, there is much more room for error. Insufficient support structures can lead to sagging, residual stress can induce distortion, and various build errors can cause otherwise undesirable deviations from the intended geometry. For very complex geometries, manual measurements or traditional metrology tools such as coordinate measuring machines or optical comparators would be difficult and time-consuming to use. Structured light scanning is the preferred metrology tool for AM because it enables rapid and relatively low-cost reconstruction of very complex geometries and automated comparison to the design model. CT can also be used for dimensional metrology. Its key advantage over structured light scanning is that it can image internal features at very high resolution and accuracy. It is most commonly used for measuring internal features. It can also be used for overall geometry comparisons, especially for small parts. In the case of large or dense parts, edge effects and artifacts of the image reconstruction may limit its geometric accuracy. Structured light scanning gives more accurate renderings of surface texture. CT is also much more time-consuming and, therefore, expensive.
5.9.6. INSPECTION CHALLENGES FOR AM
The AM process can create inspectability challenges because of several unique aspects of the build process. Parts created using AM often have more complex geometries than conventional parts because those are the parts with the greatest value proposition for AM. Surface finish can vary based on the AM technique, but it is typically rougher than that of wrought, cast, and machined materials. Although most AM processes aim for near-full density and use heat treatments to homogenize microstructures, AM materials tend to be associated with increased porosity and atypical microstructures, which disrupt traditional inspection methods through added noise and interference, with major differences being possible between different parts or similar parts from different machines. These
