5.9.6.1. GEOMETRY
Complex geometries can complicate NDE for a variety of reasons that are unique to each inspection method. For ultrasonic inspection, the pitch–catch method relies on sending ultrasonic waves into the part and detecting the signal as it returns from the back surface or a discontinuity inside the part. For a rectangular billet, flat panel, or weld with predictable geometry, it is feasible to interpret these reflections based on the expected interactions within the part. For a complex geometry, it is difficult to scan the transducer along the surface and interpret the complicated interactions inside the part. For radiography, it can be difficult to interpret the resulting two-dimensional image when there is a great deal of complexity within the part. CT has the advantage of three-dimensional image reconstruction, so it is more feasible to interpret the resulting images. However, very complex geometries and porosity can result in scattering of the x-ray beam, which will reduce the CT image quality. Dye-penetrant and eddy-current inspections are often used for surface-breaking or near-surface inspections but cannot be used if surfaces are not accessible.
5.9.6.2. SURFACE FINISH
The surface finish mostly affects the results of inspections that target surfacebreaking or near-surface flaws. Dye penetrant can be pulled into many different features in the surface of the part, and it can be difficult to clean excess penetrant off the surface. This noise makes it difficult to differentiate defect signatures. Ultrasonic inspections can also be made difficult by a very rough surface finish, which makes it hard to scan a transducer across the surface and interferes with the ultrasonic transmission into the part, introducing noise.
5.9.6.3. MORPHOLOGY/MICROSTRUCTURE
The internal structure of a part mostly affects volumetric inspection methods such as ultrasonic testing, radiography, and CT. The columnar grains lead to directional differences in the attenuation of ultrasonic signals. Excess porosity can also cause ultrasonic attenuation and can complicate the interpretation of radiographic images.
