5.9.7.1 VISUAL INSPECTION
Benefits
Visual inspection is an important first step in the evaluation of AM parts. Documenting photographs of the part while it is still attached to the build plate can provide a point of reference for all other analysis. Oftentimes, it will be visually obvious if a support structure has detached from the part, if the part is warped or displays cracking, or if the deposition was irregular. Noting these flaws from the start can help make early decisions about the quality of the part and can direct all further NDE efforts.
Limitations
Visual inspection can only show obvious deviations from the expected geometry or normal deposition process. Incorrect dimensions or slight warping cannot easily be perceived. Exact measurements require the use of metrology tools such as structured light scanning or laser profilometry. Most notably, internal features cannot be seen visually. Verifying the integrity of internal structures, as well as detecting unwanted defects such as cracks, pores, and inclusions, can only be done using more invasive NDE methods.
5.9.7.2. STRUCTURED LIGHT SCANNING OR LASER SCANNING
Benefits
Structured light scanning projects a light pattern onto the surface of a part and then interprets its interactions with the surface to create a three-dimensional model of the part with detailed surface texture. This process can be very fast and accurate, using a handheld (Fig. 5.26) or fixed (Fig. 5.27) camera system. Most structured light scanning systems are able to capture all features that are visible from the outside of the part, even those that are inset to some extent. Software tools are used to interpolate missing data points, translate the point cloud into a mesh, and display the resulting three-dimensional model of the part. Measurements of overall geometry and feature dimensions can be extracted from this model. The software tools can compare the mesh model to the design model for the part and export a visual representation of the extent of variation for each dimension (Fig. 5.28).
