Limitations
A radiograph is a two-dimensional projection that represents the cumulative effect of all of the material through which the x-rays passed. For a complex AM part, it can be very difficult to interpret a radiograph, because the geometry and internal features are all projected to the same plane. This can make the resulting radiograph too busy to discern anomalous indications that correspond to defects. Also, the ability of radiography to detect cracks can be limited. A tight crack may not show up because of its limited effect on the x-rays and the resolution of the imaging system. A crack parallel to the x-ray direction will be difficult to discern, so it is recommended that a radiograph be taken perpendicular to the expected direction of crack growth or in several orthogonal directions if the preferred orientation is unknown.
5.9.7.5. CT
CT scanning was described earlier in this chapter in relation to powder removal because it is often used for the verification of powder removal for internal features in AM parts.
