As described in the preceding section, x-rays are effective at interrogating the internal features of parts, but two-dimensional representations can be difficult to interpret. CT overcomes the key limitations of radiography with the added advantage of three-dimensional image reconstruction. This is the key benefit of CT: the full three-dimensional visualization of defects or features internal to parts or in hidden locations, perfectly suited to complex AM parts. The part is imaged with x-rays, one section at a time, while being rotated to capture the geometry from every angle. Software reconstructs these slices into a threedimensional representation of the part and all of its internal features. This threedimensional voxel (volumetric pixel) data can be visualized with virtual cross sections in any orientation. Examples can be seen in Fig. 5.29. Other software tools can then be used to automatically detect and highlight defects in three dimensions, showing their exact location within the overall part. This is particularly useful for the unique porosity distributions that can occur in AM processes. For example, Fig. 5.29 shows a clustering of pores under the top surface of the part and is attributed to keyhole porosity at the upskin regions of the particular process used. CT is often used for AM components to detect trapped powder, internal geometric features, and porosity as shown in Fig. 5.30. CT detection capability
