5.9.7.8. EDDY CURRENT
Benefits
Eddy-current inspection generates electromagnetic eddy currents in the surface of conductive metals and detects any surface-breaking or slightly subsurface discontinuities. It is well-suited to the detection of cracks or pores in or near an AM surface. It can be used on machined AM surfaces without etchant. Eddy-current arrays record a two-dimensional C-scan, reporting all indications of potential defects in the area scanned.
Limitations
Eddy-current inspection methods can only be used on conductive metals. The depth of detection is limited and difficult to quantify, so surface defect indications are the main application. The surface needs to be reachable with a long, straight probe, so many inset interior surfaces may not be reachable even if they are visible. Eddy-current probes require manual scanning and defect location recording.
