Benefits
Resonance testing is ideal for part-to-part comparisons. The baseline resonance response of one part can be compared to that of an identical part. Any defects will result in a different response. Software enables automated comparisons of the resonant response. This is ideal for parts that will be produced in volume. It can also be used to compare witness specimens for a quick check of overall build quality. Another use is part-to-itself comparison throughout the service life.
Limitations
Some AM aerospace parts are large, expensive, one-off parts that will not have duplicates for comparison. The exact nature and location of the defect cannot be determined from the resonant response alone. All of these methods are described only briefly here in the context of metal AM propulsion components. For more detailed descriptions of NDE methods, see the American Society for Nondestructive Testing website [39], ASTM E3166, and a recent book chapter describing NDE for L-PBF of metals [40].
5.9.8. NDE CONSIDERATIONS FOR AM
5.9.8.1. ARTIFICIAL FLAWS
A typical method of investigating NDE detection capabilities in any material system is to create artificial flaws in a part. The type and method of defect creation is specific to the manufacturing process. For a weld development panel, this can include varying parameters to create wormholes and inadequate penetration.
