5.9.8.1.1. Seeded defects
In AM, the most common artificial flaws are seeded or engineered defects. This refers to intentionally creating a flaw that is designed into the CAD model for the part. Such flaws are typically spherical, cylindrical, cubical, or oblong and are created by using no or reduced laser power at the specified location. The simplest method is to create a void in the part model. For L-PBF, this “void” will be filled with trapped, unmelted powder, which can complicate detection using CT and even metallography. HIP may affect the size and shape of the void and the powder within, so it may be worthwhile to investigate the pre- and post-HIP conditions for comparison. An example of a powderfilled cavity imaged by CT before and after HIP is shown in Fig. 5.34. Studies at NASA Marshall Space Flight Center (MSFC) have shown that, for an L-PBF process using Inconel 718 powder in 30 mm layers and a certain parameter set, six consecutive unfused layers is an ideal number for creating a seeded defect. Using fewer layers resulted in the keyhole of the melt pool penetrating the unmelted powder from the previous layers and essentially healing the defect; a similar effect is shown in Fig. 5.34, where the design intent was a sphere but the end result was a semisphere with the top part remelted. These partially healed defects may be worth investigating to determine their effects on fatigue properties, but they were not detectable by CT or metallography. If seeded defects are too large, they can begin to affect the subsequent layers of the part, resulting in a dramatic effect that is more easily detected than intended. Experimentation is the best way to determine the conditions for creating seeded defects with a specific AM process. Researching or studying the melt-pool depth provides a good starting point for how many skipped layers may be needed in a powder-based system. For wire-fed systems, only one layer of interrupted deposition may be sufficient to create a detectable defect.
