5.9.8.1.2. Other artificial flaws
Many other artificial flaws can also be created using the L-PBF process. One common flaw is skipped layer defects, where an entire layer of the part (or multiple layers) is left unmelted. This is often referred to as a restart line or defect, as this same type of defect can occur when a stopped build process is restarted. Another common L-PBF flaw is a recoater blade defect. If the recoater blade contacts a previous layer of the part, the blade can start to shake and leave an uneven layer or clumps of powder on the surface. If the blade is damaged, it can leave a ridge of powder on each layer of the part. This type of defect can be simulated by using a damaged recoater blade or by sprinkling extra powder on the part surface. Finally, artificial defects can be created by varying build parameters. Increasing the laser power typically increases the size and number of spatter particles, which can fuse to the part and create defects. Altering this or other parameters, such as laser speed, hatch spacing, and gas flow in the chamber, may result in more natural defects than seeded defects. The downside is that the defect location, type, and extent cannot be predicted or controlled.
5.9.8.2. RADIOGRAPHIC QUALITY INDICATORS
Representative artifacts can be built to test the radiographic detection capability. Radiographic quality indicators (RQIs) can be built with seeded defects using the AM process, or they can be built using the AM process and have defects machined out. These can be used to assess the quality of the radiography system and its settings by testing their ability to resolve internal flaws in a range of sizes within the part. They can be used to standardize inspection procedures and ensure repeatable quality. It should be noted that the ability to detect a defect of a certain size in an RQI does not necessarily guarantee that a defect of the same size can be found in a different part, even one that is the same metal and the same approximate size and is built using the same AM process. This is due to the effects of the part geometry and size and the nature of the defect.
5.9.8.3. PROBABILITY-OF-DETECTION (POD) STUDY
The NASA standard for NDE [42] of fracture-critical parts uses a POD approach to quantify the detection capability, requiring at least 95% POD with at least 90% confidence. This is based on an understanding that the ability to detect a defect of a certain size is probabilistic, with the probability decreasing for smaller flaws and increasing for larger flaws. The typical POD curve corresponding to the 90/95 limit is shown in Fig. 5.35. The plot includes the POD curve (solid navy line), an empirical plot developed by testing the POD of discontinuities of various size, with 95% confidence bounds (dashed aqua lines). There is a 90% probability of detecting the indicated defect size with 95% confidence.
