5.10. MACHINING
Although the focus of this book is AM, subtractive manufacturing (i.e., machining) is still a necessary operation for proper part fabrication of some components. Machining for AM parts can present new challenges not observed with traditional machining from wrought stock or castings. Machining can take several forms, including turning, milling, drilling, tapping, boring, reaming, grinding, and EDM. The focus of this section is on the more common operations of turning, milling, drilling, tapping, and reaming. Although AM can produce near-net or near-final shapes for many components, some level of machining is generally needed. This may include mating final interface machining; drilling, tapping or reaming holes; final machining of contours for flow or fatigue enhancement; and even grinding operations. Often, the metals used with AM are not easily machined, and their being produced with AM can add additional challenges. This, of course, is one of the advantages of AM: having the ability to fabricate parts with superalloys or refractory alloys that traditionally have long lead times to procure wrought metals or are difficult to fabricate otherwise. In general, the machining time for AM parts is greatly reduced because of the ability to build a part as a near-net or net shape. The ability to AM exotic alloys does not come for free, and challenges may arise in the machining stage for these materials. Several considerations should be evaluated during the AM process, many of which can be initiated in the design stage. The topics that are covered in this machining section are design-integrated machining; surface conditions and support structures; datum establishment and setup; and Material differences, including mechanical characteristics.
5.10.1. DESIGN-INTEGRATED MACHINING
To properly make use of AM, the entire build flow must be well understood because each step must be taken into account early in the design process. An important aspect to this is to fully understand what surfaces will require final machining and what surfaces must be built as part of the AM process to establish datums. Although many complex features can be fabricated using AM, there are some design guidelines, which are covered in more detail in Chapter 7. These guidelines should be noted in this section, as they are important to establish and successfully meet tolerances during the machining process. End-to-end process consideration is also important, as machining can introduce FOD such as machining chips during final machining. Extensive planning early in the design process is needed to help prevent FOD during final machining steps. Although complex shapes can be readily formed during the AM process, machining considerations need to be taken into account as part of design. Cylindrical shapes, when feasible, help with providing a datum centerline, whereas a freeform surface may be difficult to indicate. A secondary datum needs to be established with a flat surface that is independent of the build-plate removal
