process. The portion of the part that is removed from the build plate should not be used as a datum unless it was cut with a high degree of accuracy relative to another part datum. There are some scenarios when the build plate is used as part of the machining process [44]. This can be a successful method of tooling to help center a part or indication in the machine, but one should be aware of the potential build plate distortion. Use of a build plate as tooling must also be properly captured in the design process and communicated throughout the supply chain. One of the primary considerations of final machining is to ensure that tolerances are met on critical features. These features should have extra stock added to the design to account for subtractive machining. The stock required is highly dependent on the design, the AM process being used to build the part, and the final intended application. The surface finish and the angle of the surface during the AM build are also key considerations when adding stock, as some AM features will have very high roughness in the form of waviness and texture. Critical hole diameters should be final machined, as the roughness from AM will not meet tight tolerances. Depending on the metal, if high positional tolerances are required, it may be better to machine the holes directly and avoid starting holes from the AM build. Larger-diameter holes could affect the final geometry, and the machining tool will tend to walk or follow the path of the AM-built hole. This can cause out-of-tolerance conditions if there is any residual-stress induced distortion in the part. It is always best practice to drill and tap threaded holes to final dimensions, as threads cannot be printed. Good design practice for metal AM is to undersize holes to allow for drilling, threading, or tapping. Although traditional tapping can be used for AM parts, a preferred option can be thread milling to reduce the wear on or eliminate breakage of taps.
