5.11. AM PART CLEANING
Interim and final cleaning operations are a critical step for AM aerospace components, as such components may have more surface contamination than their traditional counterparts. The largest source of contamination is residual powder from the build process, but it can also include contaminants from other postprocessing operations. As previously described in this chapter, gross and detailed powder removal is completed prior to heat treatments. However, some loosely adhered powder may still be liberated during operation and pose a FOD risk. Additionally, post-processing can introduce additional contaminants such as cutting oil from machining; liberated metal powder during vibrations from postprocessing operations; machining chips; or other contaminants from welding, brazing, or surface enhancement operations. An adequate process plan for AM parts should include cleaning steps at the appropriate times to ensure part cleanliness to meet the final requirements. Although the complexity of AM parts is touted as an advantage, this is an area where complexity is a disadvantage for AM. Cleaning operations should include both interim precision cleaning in addition to final cleaning for operation in fuel and oxygen environments. It is important that such contamination be removed prior to subsequent joining or heat-treatment steps, as it could induce failure during that process, such as sintering of loose powder. This may result in blocked passages or increased surface roughness, causing operational issues. Several methods exist for cleaning AM components, and their successful deployment is highly dependent on geometry, so a single point solution is not often possible. The methods described in Sec. 5.5 on powder removal can be used for gross depowdering, such as compressed air, vacuum, high-pressure differential flow, and CO2 methods. Cleaning of internal cavities can be challenging and may require mechanical methods to remove powder. It should be noted that wet processing with fluids should generally be avoided prior to confirmation of powder removal. If powder removal can be confirmed through NDE, then further cleaning methods using chemicals, including solvents, may be employed. For lightly adhered particles, methods such as thermal deburring or chemical milling may be used. Final cleaning methods generally include ionic cleaning, ultrasonic, laser cleaning, plasma treatment, and more standard chemical passivation and silt sampling to ensure that no loose powder remains in the part. This may not remove all of the particles adhered to the walls. These surfaces may require surface enhancements as later described (electropolishing, abrasive flow machining, and CMP) not only to improve surface finish but also to remove adhered particles. Standard precision cleaning operations should still be
