5.12. WELDING
Due to the constraints on build volume for commonly used AM systems, some components are too large to be manufactured in a single build cycle. To work around these constraints, two or more separate smaller components must be built and joined together. Other reasons for joining may include working around complexities, such as powder removal from internal passages, and reducing the overall risk to a part from machine failures by designing it as two or more components. Some assemblies may be better optimized with AM using a few parts instead of a single part to aid with post-processing or final design functionality. Welding has traditionally been the preferred joining method for combining two metallic components into a system assembly or final part, often allowing for high levels of design flexibility without compromising properties to a great extent at the joint. Welding also allows for the joining of AM components to traditionally manufactured components (i.e., castings, forgings, and extrusions). AM components are often compared to castings or parts made of a series of continuous welds. Castings have traditionally been considered weldable by today’s aerospace industry. Welding is often employed for joining and repairing cast parts. Early studies on the welding of AM components have shown that AM parts can be welded, but several considerations must be taken into account when performing a weld on an AM part. Welding on AM components is still the subject of much investigation by academia and industry alike. AM components are considered to be a new class of metal alloys, as compared to castings or forgings. There are several key differences between AM metal alloys and traditionally welded metal alloy types. An example of this is differences in grain size and grain morphology. Considering an AM component to be similar to a series of welds stacked together to form a final geometry, the grain structure of each pass will be heavily directional, and heavy segregation of alloying elements is to be expected. Both of these characteristics can play a major role in metal weldability and need to be taken into consideration during the design of a weld. Additionally, AM components require post-processing steps that may vary from those used for their traditional metal counterparts (heat treatments, surface cleaning, etc.). The condition of the AM component must be considered when conducting a weld. From past experience, it is known that the best condition for welding an AM component is in the annealed and homogenized or solution heat-treated condition. This will ensure that a majority of the residual stresses have been removed and that the grain structure has become relatively homogenized. Welding in the solution condition will also ensure that the metal’s alloying
