elements have had enough time to redistribute and should be less segregated than in the as-built condition. Surface finish and surface quality are also imperative when considering conducting a weld on an AM part. Waviness, surface roughness, oxidation layers, and trapped powder are all issues that can occur in an AM part that will impede the quality of a weld. Several design considerations should also be taken into account when designing an AM component to be welded. These considerations are discussed later in this section. The content of this section is based on experiences at NASA and publications in the literature. It should not be considered an all-encompassing guide to the welding of AM components, but can be used as a general basis for approaching welds containing AM parts.
5.12.1. WELDING PROCESSES
In theory, all welding processes designed to join metals should be able to be used to weld metallic AM components. Generally, the welding process is chosen by considering several factors: the geometry of the joint, the end use of the component or system, and the type of metal being welded. Each welding process also has unique considerations that must be taken into account during the design of an AM part; these aspects of the weld process are the same for both forged and AM components. According to a review of the literature, several examples of different welding processes have been demonstrated to work on AM components. These processes include gas tungsten arc welding (GTAW), laser beam welding (LBW), electron beam welding (EBW), laser-arc hybrid welding, resistance welding, rotary friction welding, and friction stir welding. Again, the metal type and end-use application are often the deciding factors in choosing a welding process to weld a selected AM component. When a filler metal is used for a fusion weld, which is commonly done when performing a GTAW weld, the same considerations that are taken into account for a traditionally manufactured component still apply. To date, there has been no evidence that specialty filler metals are needed to weld AM components. As long as the filler metal is compatible with the base metal, it can be used to weld an AM component composed of that same base metal. For example, an AM part made from 316L stainless steel powder can be welded with a traditional stainless steel filler wire (308L, 316L). Printing filler metal within an AM build has also been found to be acceptable if a unique or custom alloy is being built.
5.12.2. WELDING INSPECTION METHODS
In the same way that any welding method can theoretically be used on an AM part, so can any common weld inspection method. That being said, some unique considerations must be made when inspecting an AM component, many of which are discussed in Sec. 5.9 on NDE.
