For surface inspections using the eddy-current and dye-penetrant techniques, the quality of the surface of the component matters immensely. A recent study by NASA showed that conducting dye-penetrant inspections on the as-built surface of a DED part will show false indications because of the surface roughness produced by the AM method. It is recommended that, when conducting a weld that requires a surface inspection, the weld land and the area adjacent to the weld land be machined smooth to avoid false indications. For ultrasonic volumetric inspections, the surface must be prepared by a method similar to that used for surface inspections. Ultrasonic inspection requires the piezoelectric probes to be in intimate contact with the weld and the area adjacent to the weld being inspected. If there is severe surface roughness, the probe will not be able to properly transfer ultrasonic waves to the metal being inspected. Removal of any surface roughness or residual build lines is recommended to properly conduct an ultrasonic inspection. Volumetric inspection by radiography is another welding inspection method that is very commonly used by the aerospace industry. This inspection method does not require a prepared surface for inspection. However, if there are irregularities on the surface of the component, they may appear in the final radiographic image as artifacts, which may be interpreted as indications or flaws. Again, a machined or prepared surface works best, but false indications can also be avoided by using inspection demonstration articles. A demonstration article allows the inspector to understand how a surface feature attributed to the inherent build state will appear on digital or film radiographs.
5.12.3. DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS
When designing an AM component that will require welding, this fact must be taken into account during the design process to ensure that a robust weld can be completed on the components. The first thing to consider is what type of welding process is planned to join these components. Knowing what welding process is to be used will allow the design engineer to include joint considerations in the final design. A part being resistance spot welded needs to be designed such that an overlapping section is included between each component. If a GTAW is desired, the engineer needs to consider whether a groove will be employed to make the weld. It is recommended that these joints be machined and not welded in the as-built surface condition for AM components to ensure a robust and sound joint. This may require additional stock using DfAM (Chapter 7). Although AM parts can increase in complexity, other considerations that can affect design should be evaluated, including the following:
. Does the weld require a thicker section to be welded?
. How will the final weld be inspected?
. What level of access is required to conduct the weld?
