5.12.4. PREWELD PREPARATIONS FOR AM COMPONENTS
The considerations that must be taken into account for the welding of AM components are similar to those for the welding of traditionally manufactured components. The joint design should be prepared similarly to traditional components. For the welding of flight-worthy components, it is imperative to have the cleanest surface available. Oftentimes, the specific cleaning procedure is dependent on the class of metal being welded. As a general rule for the welding of AM components, the surface should be free of all residual powder. It has been observed that AM surfaces can hold excess oxygen in the as-built condition, which makes welding operations challenging. Additionally, a higherquality weld will be produced if the mating surface and the weld area are free of residual surface roughness remaining from the build, including areas adjacent to the weld fusion zone. The mating surfaces should also be free of any remaining grease, oils, or remnant oxide layers prior to welding.
5.12.5. FINAL RECOMMENDATIONS
It is recommended that AM metal alloys be considered as a new class of materials for weld procedure qualification. Therefore, if a component is switched from a casting to an AM component, a new welding procedure should be designed and qualified. This is due to both variations in the metal and changes in the properties of the base metal. Similar filler metals can be shared between traditionally manufactured components and AM components. All cleaning measures previously carried out on traditionally manufactured components should also be applied to the AM components. Be aware that melt-pool fluidity is subject to variation between traditionally manufactured components and AM components. Additionally, it should be understood that there are variations in chemistry between powder lots and final built components. The variations may be slight, but it has been shown that final built components often pick up more atmospheric oxygen than the corresponding initial feed stock chemistry. This can be controlled during the building process with shielding gases, but it often cannot be fully avoided and may affect welds. For the highest-quality joints, the mating surfaces and surrounding weld area should be free of all residual build surface roughness, unfused powder, and build line remnants. The part may also require a special weld land to be built into the part, especially if the part contains a lattice structure or is exceptionally thin-walled.
