A significant amount of research is needed to help quantify and elaborate on these metallurgical differences. The study described in this section investigated weldability differences between wrought, AM-DED, and AM-PBF plate metal. The metal alloys investigated were Inconel 718 and Inconel 625. The weldability test used to help quantify differences between these alloys and their different manufacturing processes was the transverse varestraint weldability test. The transverse varestraint weldability test involves creating a molten weld puddle using a GTAW torch and traversing along the plate. At a designated time after an adequate weld bead has been developed, the torch is extinguished, and after the torch has been extinguished but before the molten weld pool has fully solidified, the plate is subjected to a bending stress transverse to the weld puddle. The bending stress induces strain at the weld puddle and is intended to induce solidification cracking within the weld fusion zone. The level of severity of the cracking is quantified using different measurements; this case study reported maximum crack distances (MCD). MCD is a measurement of the largest single crack observed on the surface of the weld fusion zone. The level of strain induced in the test plate is controlled by varying the stroke of the hydraulic ram and the radius of the die block. Testing alloys across a range of strain levels allows a relationship between strain level and severity of cracking to be developed. This relationship can then be compared to those of other metal alloys tested using trans-varestraint testing to rank materials on their susceptibility to solidification cracking in the weld fusion zone.
5.12.6.1. EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE
For this study, three samples were run at each strain point between 0.5% and 5% augmented strain. The plate used for this testing was 50.4 x 152.4 x 4.78 mm (2 x 6 x 0.188 in.). The AM samples were machined to the final thickness to remove any remnants from the build process. All samples were welded in the solution heat-treated condition with the AM samples being stress relieved after the AM process. The chemical composition of each metal alloy was obtained using a combination of optical emission spectroscopy (OES) and Inert Gas Fusion. The composition results are shown in Table 5.3. Following the completion of welding, samples were examined under a microscope, which was used to measure the length of the solidification cracking observed in each sample. To maintain consistent measurements, the same microscope and a set magnification were used. Additionally, two individuals independently measured the resulting samples and averaged their measurements to reduce bias.
