5.13. BRAZING AND DIFFUSION BONDING
Some component designs may require the use of traditional joining methods, such as brazing and diffusion bonding, to provide permanent and strong bonds between dissimilar metals. For brazing, a brazing filler metal (BFM) is melted and distributed everywhere between the closely fitted faying surfaces by capillary action. Like diffusion bonding, brazing does not melt the parent metals, but unlike diffusion bonding, brazing requires the liquid phase of the BFM [65]. This design solution may provide a potential advantage over some bimetallic AM processes to avoid brittle intermetallics or deleterious phases that could weaken the joint or cause early failure. Metal AM parts will present new challenges for the use of the brazing and diffusion bonding techniques. The overall brazing process for traditional and AM components is similar; however, some inherent features of AM may impact the joint design and process slightly. First, the faying surfaces to be joined must be properly cleaned, which means that they must be free of dirt, oil, and other contaminants. A cleaning process should be specifically designed for AM parts, where residual powder can be present on the surface, causing contamination, even after rough or ultrasonic cleaning. Parts must also be free of surface oxides, even when a controlled atmosphere (i.e., vacuum or inert gas backfilled) is used, unless a braze flux designed for deoxidation is used. A study comparing traditional and L-PBF samples of 316L stainless steel revealed pores between the BFM and L-PBF joint, which resulted in a strength reduction. The mechanism of pore formation was not fully understood but was hypothesized to result from oxide inclusions or open porosity within the L-PBF metal alloy [66]. These results further
