5.14. POLISHING OR SURFACE ENHANCEMENTS
The surface texture produced by AM will be rougher compared to traditional manufacturing and must be well understood for component applications (Chapter 8). The surface texture (roughness and waviness) affects many aspects of the design, including loss in fatigue life and increased pressure drop because of increased friction factors. Due to the higher surface texture, components with as-built surfaces often show inferior fatigue performance as compared to machined surfaces. The data for both surface conditions exhibit a large amount of scatter. Beyond the functional application, the increased roughness also impacts post-processing operations during fabrication, including machining, joining (welding, brazing), cleaning, and inspections, as described previously. This section provides an overview of various surface enhancements, or polishing techniques, that can be used to modify functional performance and component aesthetics. AM-produced components show serious complications such as significantly high surface texture, surface defects, and near-surface porosity. The extremely high surface texture is a product of the combination of a complex surface roughness (mostly partially sintered and melted powder) and a high surface waviness inherent to AM manufacturing techniques, often referred to as layering [6972]. These surface texture characteristics are exhibited in parts produced by all of the metal AM processes. Surface and near-surface defects are generally found in the form of incomplete powder fusion, V-notches (“deep and sharp valleys”), and near-surface pores or voids (usually produced by keyhole effects, lack of fusion, and/or the hatching-contour overlap failure). The combination of these defects hinders the mechanical strength and dynamic performance of AM components and impacts other performance characteristics such as cleanliness and fluid drag, depending on the component type and application [73–76]. The extent of these surface and near-surface defects has been identified to depths up to 1 mm (0.04 in.) from the surface for L-PBF parts; thus, significant effective surface metal removal is needed to produce a robust and reliable AM component with desired mechanical properties. In many cases, the as-built component’s surface finish does not comply with the requirements of the application, thereby impeding the broad-scale adoption of AM as an improvement on or replacement for traditional, subtractive manufacturing processes. Post-processing technologies can be used to improve the surface finish, thereby adding many potential applications and maximizing AM’s potential in all industries [77–81]. Secondary subtractive finishing processes such as grinding and milling, may be used to remove some of the surface
