ts in the sintering of additional surrounding powder. These loosely bonded powder particles increase the surface roughness. As a result, susceptibility to crack initiation increases in the downskin [89]. An optimal solution may be a combination of surface enhancement techniques. It is difficult to provide exact values of the surface roughness for a process. The roughness is highly dependent on the alloy, process parameters, feedstock, design, and build orientation, along with other process parameters and the build setup. Figure 5.50 provides some general estimates compared to traditional machining operations. These are provided only as rough estimates. The machining operations described in Sec. 5.10 are often used for mating propulsion components, but due to the design complexity of AM, they are not feasible for every surface requiring modification. Various methods have been explored for enhancing (polishing) external and internal surfaces with complex geometries; see Table 5.4. A more detailed description of each method is also provided in the text, along with selected examples and references. Surface enhancements are a significant area that is being researched for AM to help maximize performance.
