5.14.1. LASER POLISHING
Laser polishing makes use of the primary laser or a secondary laser during the fabrication process or as a secondary process to remelt regions on the surface, creating an improved surface finish. This process has been applied for both L-PBF and DED components in various metal alloys [90–93]. The laser polishing process, when used in situ, can be performed between layers, but it presents several challenges because it requires direct access to the surface, which can be difficult because of complex geometries or masking by powder (within L-PBF) or other features. The laser parameters used for local smoothing are a much lower power and smaller spot size but a higher scanning rate than for the build laser parameters. The areas where the laser is focused are melted, creating a liquid melt pool, which levels the surface based on surface tension and gravity (Fig. 5.51). The region solidifies quickly across the surface and into valleys [94]. Laser polishing has been shown to reduce the surface roughness of L-PBF and LP-DED parts by 80–96% [95]. Another form of laser polishing uses material ablation, which vaporizes surface metal, creating a remelted surface [96]. This process is also referred to as laser-based machining and uses a high-pressure gas assist to help eject metal from the surface and limit the heat-affected zone (HAZ) when using a femtosecond or nanosecond pulsed laser [97]. A single pass to smooth the surface using laser polishing may not offer the desired surface finish, and multiple passes may be required [98]. This remelting has been shown to create recast layers demonstrated to be about 22 mm (866 min) thick in Inconel 718 LP-DED samples [99]. A significant gap in the
