5.14.2. CHEMICAL AND CHEMICAL MECHANICAL POLISHING
Chemical polishing, or chemical etching, has been used for decades for traditional fabrication to remove large areas of material on thin-wall parts in a variety of alloys. The process is also referred to as chemical milling. The process uses a chemical solution that attacks the grain structure and dissolves the metal when immersed. For example, hydrofluoric acid and nitric acid are used for the chemical polishing of Ti-based AM parts [101]. The process is controlled because the material removal rates (MRR) are known from the etching, passivation, and deactivation rates of the metal. There are several variables in the process, including the element composition of the chemical solution, the pH of the solution, the temperature of the solution, and the agitation rate. The chemical polishing process allows for the internal milling of features because only the solution is required [102]. Internal features may require active flushing to remove metal or reduce the accumulation of hydrogen bubbles, which can be mitigated by part rotation, vibration, or solution agitation. Chemical polishing can also help dissolve excess powder trapped in regions or adhered to surface areas that the liquid solution can access [103]. Chemical polishing development has been explored on various L-PBF components (316L stainless steel, Ni-Ti, Inconel 718, Ti6Al4V), including internal features [104]. A disadvantage of the chemical polishing process is the limited surface smoothing that is possible. The chemical solution will follow the contour of the surface roughness, so it may reduce the peaks of the surface, but it will not remove the valleys and, in some cases, may even exacerbate them [105]. CMP uses a combination of chemicals to create an amorphous self-assembling passive layer that is then removed by the mechanical rubbing action of any kind of media [106]. This technology, also referred to as chemically accelerated vibratory finishing (CAVF), increases the surface metal removal rate that leads to planarization [107, 108]. The mechanism of this method focuses on the preferential removal of the surface peaks, until the peaks meet the valleys, followed by excess material removal in the valleys to achieve a smooth surface [109]. A vibratory bowl or specialty tooling for movement of the media is required to allow the media to remove the proper material. The media used in CMP can vary
