5.14.3. ABRASIVE FLOW MACHINING
Abrasive flow machining (AFM) describes a nontraditional machining or honing process in which an abrasive fluidlike material is forcefully flowed along a surface, thereby abrading the geometry [110]. The advantage of AFM is that the abrasive fluid can reach the most inaccessible areas and treat multiple holes, slots, or edges in one operation [111]. Abrasive flow polishing is considered to be a lapping operation in which the abrasive media becomes a self-forming grinding stone, or lap, as it proceeds through the microbore inner wall [112–117]. Studies have found that AFM is very effective at deburring, edge contouring, removing certain surface defects, and reducing surface roughness that results in a polished surface [118]. AFM (also referred to as extrusion honing) results in uniform, repeatable, and predictable results on a range of finishing operations [119]. Abrasive media suspended in a fluid polymer carrier is fed through the channels or a component’s geometric features at pressures ranging from 7 to 220 bar (from 100 to 3000 psig). The media is based on polyborosiloxane or an alternative viscoelastic polymer, and the viscosity varies depending on the hardware
