5.14.4. ELECTROPOLISHING (EP) OR ELECTROCHEMICAL MACHINING (ECM)
EP or ECM is a method that applies a local current to dissolve metal, providing polishing to the adjacent wall in a controlled process. Direct current (dc) is used, with the part being the anode and the tooling being the cathode. A reverseshaped conformal electrode tool is created that has an offset gap from the geometry or passage being processed for polishing. There is no direct contact of the tooling, but a gap with the proper distance between the tool and the part surface must be maintained for proper material removal rates and surface topography. The entire assembly is submerged in an electrolytic liquid solution, which, in most cases, is a pH-neutral salt such as sodium chloride (NaCl) or sodium nitride (Na3N). The current is applied and dissolves metal adjacent to the tooling. Two types of phenomena occur during EP at the macro- and microscopic scales [127]. First, an anodic leveling takes place, which is the result of various dissolution rates based on the current applied and the peaks being targeted. Second, an anodic brightening occurs, which aids in further improvement of the surface finish [128]. An important aspect of applying the ECM process is maintaining a gap of the proper distance between the tooling and the part to control the proper current and also flushing the dissolved material and rejuvenating with fresh liquid.
