Cavitation methods for peening can be energized by high-pressure water jets or ultrasonic transducers. In water-jet cavitation, a high-pressure water nozzle introduces a water column into a fluid medium, generally water, causing bubbles to form, grow, and then collapse on the surface [142]. When the bubbles collapse, a high-pressure microjet is generated that impacts the surface, creating localized peening. Ultrasonic methods are similar, with a high-frequency sinusoidal ultrasonic pressure wave introduced into a liquid medium where bubbles form, grow, and then collapse in proximity to the surface. Introducing abrasives or micro-abrasives into the process allows for surface finish improvements of 45–90%, as shown in Inconel 625 and AlSi10Mg L-PBF specimens, including internal channels ranging from 1 to 5 mm in diameter [143–145].
5.14.7. OTHER TECHNIQUES
Abrasive grinding operations have been demonstrated on AM components to reduce surface roughness, as shown in coating operations to improve wear resistance [146]. These methods may include the use of polishing wheels or abrasive stones, pastes, or fluids applied to the surface by a hand or machine operation. This is a line-of-sight technique allowing for processing on simple geometric surfaces and requires specialty tooling. Honing is also commonly used on AM components, but it is limited to accessible internal surfaces. Grit blasting is also a common technique for improving the surface finish of AM parts. Runyon et al. demonstrated a 40% reduction in Ra of raw L-PBF components using a post-build grit blasting operation. Although this technique is simple and accessible, it is a line-of-sight technique and would not be feasible for internal flow surfaces [147]. Whereas grinding, grit blasting, and polishing can be limited to the skill of the operator, research has been conducted to automate polishing on AM surfaces using conformal vibration-assisted tooling. Vibration-assisted polishing uses a conformal molded tool along with integrated abrasive media and a rubbing action to remove material and reduce roughness [148]. The tooling is a reverse of the surface, so it is limited for complex surfaces and internal features. Another process discussed by Bao for the improvement of surface finishes is high speed burnishing [149]. The burnishing provides local yielding of the top of the surface, which has been investigated for decreasing the surface roughness, but it also increases the surface hardness [150]. A majority of the use of the burnishing process is on external surfaces, so it will be limited in use. However, there is limited research regarding internal burnishing in which a burnishing tool having interference with a bore is pressed through a sleeve [151]. Although minimal details were presented by Bao [158], it was noted that burnishing surfaces with high roughness from additive processes, such as DED, is not feasible. The study presented by Bao required machining prior to final burnishing.
