Existing methods that are used, such as honing and lapping, are timeconsuming processes and do not change the properties of the machined part. Burnishing is a surface finishing process that is very fast, improves the surface properties of machined part, and yields a surface finish that is comparable to that of a conventional part. Fluidized-bed machining (FBM) rotates a specimen on a central axis or dips the specimen while abrasive media impacts the surface using an updraft air source and has been demonstrated on AM AlSi10Mg samples [152, 153]. Although reference has been made to use of this process for internal surfaces, it is very limited for internal complex features, so it is generally considered applicable to external surfaces [145]. It can be used for AM parts with long channels or tubes, but this requires a direct opening normal to the FBM flow [154]. Vibratory surface finishing (VSF), tumbling finishing, and drag finishing (DF) methods have been investigated for AM L-PBF. These methods have also exhibited reductions in Ra surface finish (Fig. 5.58).
