CHAPTER 6
Feedstock for Metal Additive Manufacturing
Christopher Kantzos
NASA Glenn Research Center, Cleveland, Ohio
6.1. BACKGROUND
6.1.1. INTRODUCTION
In a majority of the metal additive manufacturing (AM) processes, the feedstock is joined, deposited, or built, through a melting or fusion process. Although there are many types of feedstocks available in traditional manufacturing, including forgings, castings, bars, plates, and sheets, feedstocks with small characteristic widths such as powder and wire are ideal for the common melting AM processes. Feedstock is central to the metallurgical characteristics of the AM parts being formed, and designers must be aware of the feedstock requirements. Requirements for the feedstock specifications must be properly noted in the design process. Powder is the most common feedstock used in AM, with a number of alloys readily available in a variety of particle size distributions (PSDs). Wire is the second most common feedstock and is also available in a variety of chemistries. Although AM is a relatively new manufacturing process, metal powder is available because of the maturity of the separate manufacturing processes of powder metallurgy and thermal spraying. Similarly, welding processes have allowed metal wire to be readily available. Other emerging AM processes, such as solid-state processes, have unique feedstocks, such as metal sheet/foil or tape, as well as bar stock, which are discussed briefly.
6.1.2. POWDER
Based on current process maturity, powder is the main feedstock for AM because it is sufficiently small for building fine features and because a large variety of powder manufacturing processes and powder metal suppliers exist. Complex chemistries can be more easily controlled because segregation is confined to the length scale of the powder particles, and accurate size distributions (usually in the range of 10 – 200 mm) can be maintained by reliable sizing techniques such as sieving and air classifying. A variety of techniques exist to make metal powder, but in applying AM to aerospace applications, powder is most commonly made using inert-gas atomization techniques [1, 2].
