In some instances, a user may attempt to use off-the-shelf alloy powder for implementation with AM processes. However, all powder is not made equal. For an alloy that is commonly used in powder metallurgy, powder may be available but not necessarily within the AM process specification. Off-the-shelf powder not specifically optimized for an AM process has limitations, because the powder specifications cannot be controlled (compared to custom feedstock). Because of the processes used to create the powder, non-AM powder (such as that intended for powder metallurgy) is generally angular as opposed to spherical and can be mixed powder instead of pre-alloyed, and the PSD may be out of the desired range, rendering the powder unusable for the intended AM process (see Sec. 6.3.2.1). Also, for melting-based AM, because of the difference in vapor pressures of different elements, multicomponent alloys may not have a chemical composition in the as-built condition that matches the specified composition. Furthermore, a single alloy (e.g., Inconel 718) can have a range of possible compositions that still meet specifications but have widely different microstructures and corresponding properties [5]. Finally, in the case of powder, available feedstock may not flow or shear sufficiently to create a uniform powder distribution over a build plate in laser powder bed fusion (L-PBF) or to flow through nozzles in LP-DED. All of these considerations must be taken into account when purchasing feedstock that is available but not being sold for a specific AM process. Because of these considerations, when ordering non-OEM feedstock, it is recommended that the design engineer work with the feedstock manufacturer to understand the chemistry, the feedstock sizing, and any other characteristics required for feedstock use within the various AM processes. Industry specifications for feedstock can greatly assist the end user in generating minimum requirements, and additional specifications generated b