6.3. POWDER FEEDSTOCK
With the current popularity of L-PBF and LP-DED processes, powder feedstock is the most common feedstock used in AM, because it lends itself to a variety of processes (L-PBF, EB-PBF, LP-DED, etc.). Each AM process has different powder specifications, and powder can be made in a variety of ways. Ensuring that a powder meets these specifications and that the powder is handled appropriately is essential for producing quality parts.
6.3.1. POWDER PRODUCTION PROCESSES
6.3.1.1. ATOMIZATION
The atomization process melts raw metal, or virgin billet, within a crucible and uses some type of pressurized medium to break the liquid into small droplets, which are then cooled. These droplets, when solidified, form the powder. There are a variety of atomization techniques, but inert-gas-atomized powder is the most commonly used in AM powder production.
6.3.1.1.1. Inert-gas atomization
Inert-gas atomization is the most common method for producing metal powder. The process begins with feedstock elemental constituents or an ingot of the alloy brought to a molten state within a crucible, as shown in Fig. 6.1. The molten metal, either drained from the bottom of the crucible or poured from the top, is allowed to flow downward, through a ceramic filter, forming a liquid metal stream. Eventually, the stream passes into the atomization chamber, where a nozzle blows gas (usually argon or nitrogen) onto the molten metal, causing the formation of small droplets, which is known as atomization. The dispersion of liquid metal is shown in Fig. 6.2. Depending on the configuration of the nozzle, the atomization process can be called either close-coupled, in which the nozzle is very close to the liquid stream (which is somewhat more efficient for fine powder), or free fall, in which one or more gas nozzles are somewhat lower [8]. As the droplets fall through the chamber, they are cooled because the chamber wall is water-cooled, and they solidify into individual powder particles that are collected at the bottom of the chamber.
