6.3.2.3. CLEANLINESS
One of the primary concerns regarding metal powders is cleanliness. For example, exposure to oxygen or moisture can significantly affect the quality of parts. Therefore, unlike with other feedstocks, powder is often carefully controlled, including being stored in sealed metal containers and having a cover gas such as argon or nitrogen. Another area of cleanliness regards impurities due to the powder production, such as through interactions with the crucible or crucible liner and through contamination from previously atomized alloys. Some examples of common contamination issues include titanium alloys containing tungsten and Ni-based alloys with nonmetallic inclusions such as Al2O3.
6.3.3. SAFETY
Metal powder has a very high surface-area-to-volume ratio, which requires that additional safety considerations be taken into account. All federal and local laws should be followed when handling powder. Metal powder can cause a range of health effects depending on the metal alloy, size distribution, amount inhaled or ingested, and so on. Powder handling is done with a pumped air
