6.3.4. CHARACTERIZATION
6.3.4.1. SIZE DISTRIBUTION
The size distribution is one of the most essential characteristics of a powder lot. The simplest way to list the powder size is by the cut based on the sieve and micrometer size, but the d10, d50, and d90 diameters are somewhat more accurate. For these diameters to be determined, the entire size distribution must be known. One way to measure the powder PSD is laser scattering particle size analysis, which is a technique in which powder falls through a laser path and causes the laser light to scatter. The angle of the scattered light is affected by the size of the particle, allowing the size of the particles to be back-calculated. This technique is usually rather fast (on the order of minutes) and hands-free. A simpler way to measure the size distribution is to image powder spread on a surface and measure the powder dimensions directly. Automated machines are available to do this, or the analysis can be programmed from scratch with optical or SEM images. The major benefit of this approach is that the particle shape statistics are also measured, but it takes much longer (up to hours for tens of thousands of particles). A combination of these two methods allows for both increased speed and shape analysis. Such systems have powder fall past a light source, and a camera images the particles and measures their shapes. Other techniques include using a Coulter counter, sieving a powder into many cuts and weighing them, or performing x-ray micro-focus-computed tomography (micro-CT) of the powder and analyzing the shapes in three dimensions. One important distinction to note in the analysis of histograms of powder size distributions is whether they are number-weighted or volume-weighted. Volumeweighted histograms show a bin of powder as a fraction of the total volume (or area, mass, etc.), whereas number-weighted histograms show the fraction of the
