One of the most basic measures of flowability is the Hall flow [42], which allows powder to freely flow through an orifice under the force of gravity into a cup. The time it takes for a given mass or volume of powder to finish flowing is the measure of flowability. Figure 6.14 shows images of a Hall flow setup. Another increasingly common method of measuring flowablilty is the rotating drum technique. This technique places powder in a drum, which is placed on rollers. As the drum spins, the powder falls, and the angle (among other measures) of the silhouette can be related to the energy necessary for flow to occur or the ability of the powder to shear. One property of powder that is more relevant for other powder metallurgy techniques is the packing density. Powder packing density measures how compact powder is in different states, including as-poured and tapped. Packing density is mostly relevant to AM in terms of the thickness of the powder layer. Because the powder layer is not 100% dense, the solidified metal alloy thickness after melting is less than the thickness of the spread powder layer, which causes the spread powder to end up being larger than the as-built layer thickness. Powder packing density [43, 44] can be measured with a number of automated testing machines.
