6.3.5. ADVANCED TECHNIQUES
Optimization of metal powder in AM is an active area of development, with emerging technologies of note. One recent development involves the printing of coated powders. Powder can be coated with oxides, for example, for dispersion strengthening using a mixer [7, 57] or through coating by a deposition technique. These techniques allow for the creation of unique alloys with improved properties. The mixing of different powders has also been used to achieve new compositions of alloys during the development phase. This requires sufficient mixing in the melt pool to achieve alloys with limited segregation. Although this in situ mixing process may not yield industrial production quantities or be economical for larger batches, it is important to note that it can be used for early development. Also, the repeatability and consistency of chemistry may not meet that of atomized feedstocks. Some examples of the application of this approach include the in situ alloying of GRCop-42 for L-PBF [58] and the in situ alloying of highentropy alloys by Moorehead et al. [59]. A more robust supply chain will include the final state of powder supplied to the process. Advanced powder characterization techniques have also emerged to capture more nuanced aspects of powders, including complexities of morphology.
