6.4. OTHER FEEDSTOCKS
6.4.1. WIRE
Metal wire is used in AM processes, such as EBW-DED [62] and LW-DED [63], and robotic welding systems including AW-DED [64]. The main benefit of using wire instead of powder is the significantly higher deposition rates that wire options offer because of the larger-diameter feedstock. Various wire-based processes have been successfully used in forming large AM structures [65]. Although wire-based processes can deposit metal faster, there is an inherent limitation in feature size (Chapter 2). Powder bed processes can use powder in the 20 mm range and have been shown to build features as small as 100 mm [66]. In contrast, the wires used in wire-based AM processes are often between 1 and 10 mm and are most commonly 0.6 – 3.2 mm [67].
6.4.1.1. SUPPLY CHAIN
A significant number of standards and documents are available for wire feedstocks based on the extensive history of the welding field, and a large variety of alloys are available. Wires made of Ti-based alloys, Ni-based alloys, and Fe-based alloys are all common. Wires of Al-based alloys and other alloys exist to some extent as well. For common alloys such as 316L stainless steel, wire can be much lower in cost than powder. However, this depends on the alloy, as well as the wire size and chemistry required. Custom alloys can significantly exceed powder in costs because of the expense of large custom batch melting and drawing operations.
6.4.1.2. SAFETY AND HANDLING
Another significant advantage of wire is safety concerns. Unlike powder, wire is not an inhalation concern and has simplified handling. This has significant implications for the use of wire-based AM, including for repairs on large vehicles where a safe environment for powder is hard to maintain. Although most metals are not a flammability risk in wire form, reactive metals such as titanium are often shipped and stored in vacuum bags to minimize the risk of ignition.
6.4.1.3. TYPES FOR DIFFERENT PROCESSES
Because various metal AM processes have different requirements, the wire diameter must be properly matched to the process and the part geometry being created, as shown in Table 6.1. The wire feedstock is dependent on the component design,
