process, and objectives during the process. Typically, smaller-diameter wire can create smaller features, whereas larger-diameter wire is used for higher deposition rates. The requirements for the wire feedstock, such as chemistry and sizing, are also very important for the final metal alloy microstructure and the mechanical and thermophysical properties. Wire is available in both solid and cored forms. Solid wire matches the chemistry of the specification in a homogenous form. Cored wire uses a sheath with an elemental metal and includes the proper mix of other elements that, when deposited into a melt pool, will mix and create the desired chemistry. This is used to reduce the overall cost of creating custom melts and drawing. It is not applicable for all metal AM applications, however, as it can result in elemental segregation. Another form of cored wire uses deoxidizing additions, but this type of wire is not often used in aerospace applications. Selection of a shielding gas along with the wire type and process is very important. Gases are used for many of the wire-based processes and could be considered an accompanying feedstock. For example, wire chemistries necessitate the selection of gases. Shielding gases (e.g., Ar, He, CO2, H2, N2) vary depending on the type of metal being deposited and can affect the deposition rate, final chemistry, geometry, and defects observed during the process.
6.4.1.4. MANUFACTURING METHODS
Metal wire is almost exclusively made by wire drawing processes that have existed for hundreds of years [68]. Originally, wire could be pulled through dies by hand or with simple human-powered tools. Now, wire drawing is a highly automated process that often includes additional treatments such as descaling, annealing, and cleaning. Before a wire is drawn, it must first be cleaned to remove scale or rust. This can be done mechanically by routing the wire around various rollers at a high angle so the scale flakes off or chemically by a variety of processes. Depending on the number of dies through which the wire passes, it may be necessary to anneal the wire first. Annealing removes cold work from previous manufacturing steps, allowing the wire to not fail because of a lack of ductility. Optimization of the annealing process is necessary to finish the process with the desired strength. Intermediate annealing steps may be necessary for wire that goes through many reductions. Wire drawing entails plastically deforming a wire by pulling it through a die. An example of a die is shown in Fig. 6.17. The diameter of the die decreases such that the wire’s diameter is
