6.4.1.5. SPECIFICATIONS
The most commonly used specifications for wire feedstock have been developed by the American Welding Society (AWS). AWS specifications vary based on metal alloy; for example, AWS A5.9 covers “Bare Stainless Steel Welding Electrodes and Rods”. These specifications also list a designation for identifying types of welding feedstock. An example is ER316L (under AWS A5.9). In this designation, “ER” represents “electrode or rod”, “316” denotes the composition (i.e., 316 stainless steel), and “L” represents a low-carbon version (0.03 wt % maximum). More elaborate designations can exist under different specifications (e.g., different metal systems). Along with the designation, the diameter of the wire and the length are necessary to specify the desired wire feedstock, along with spool size and other characteristics. Wire manufacturers and vendors often test and list certain properties of the deposited metal alloy (usually in the context of gas metal arc welding) including the composition, tensile properties, and Charpy impact toughness.
6.4.2. SHEET OR FOIL
UAM is an emerging form of solid-state combined additive –subtractive manufacturing, which is discussed in Chapter 2. The feedstock used is metal foil that is joined using a solid-state ultrasonic friction weld. The foil is commonly produced by traditional rolling methods and is not discussed in much detail. Currently, a limited number of companies are working on this process, so the low volume of demand for this custom-made feedstock results in long lead times.
