6.4.3. BAR STOCK OR SOLID ROD
The solid-state AFS-D process can make use of both powder and solid rod, or bar, stock as the feedstock to form large structures [3]. The rod, or bar, stock used in this process is formed by traditional methods. Various sizes can be used and may require machining to final dimensions. The metal alloy requirements generally do not differ from those of traditionally produced solid rod or bar stock.
6.4.4 OTHER FEEDSTOCK FORMS
As metal AM continues to evolve, other forms of feedstock will likewise continue to evolve. Recycled feedstock, such as shavings and chips from machining, can be used to create feedstocks. The binder jet process uses a powder feedstock, but it also requires a binder to hold the green part in shape prior to sintering operations. The binder also needs to be considered in the feedstock supply chain to create high-quality parts.
6.5. SUMMARY
There is considerable variability in the feedstocks available for AM. For powder feedstocks, the size distribution, chemistry, morphology, and flowability are all essential characteristics that vary based on the powder manufacturing process and other factors. Characterizing and tracking these powder properties is important to maintaining high-quality and consistent feedstocks. Atomization techniques are the primary powder manufacturing method, but a large body of research has investigated the extent to which nonstandard powders can be used to reduce cost and waste. The supply chain is another significant consideration. Cost, turnaround, and batch size all affect the procurement of powder. Wire-based AM processes have gained popularity and draw the majority of their manufacturing processes and specifications from welding standards, whereas emerging AM processes using nonstandard feedstocks are in the early stages of feedstock production and specification.
