7.1. INTRODUCTION
Design limitations have always been based on manufacturing limitations. The simple reality is, if a design cannot be fabricated, it is confined to the imagination. Metal additive manufacturing (AM) has radically loosened manufacturing limitations by greatly opening up the design space and allowing for more creativity and complexity in aerospace propulsion components. The purpose of this chapter is to provide highlights, design guidelines, limitations, and considerations–collectively referred to as design for additive manufacturing (DfAM)for laser powder bed fusion (L-PBF) and laser powder directed energy deposition (LP-DED) processes. It is crucial that the engineer has a basic understanding of all aspects of AM to properly deploy the technology. Preceding chapters have focused on processes (Chapter 2), alloy selection (Chapter 3), metal alloy considerations (Chapter 4), post-processing (Chapter 5), and feedstock (Chapter 6). This chapter will integrate all these AM concepts into the design process. Evaluation of the performance and functional application of AM components is covered in Chapter 8. As emphasized throughout this book, the design process presented for AM applications is iterative, and adjustments are usually required for the successful manufacturing of a part. Traditional manufacturing processes such as welding, brazing, and subtractive machining (often with complex tooling) can be substantially reduced with the utilization of AM. AM has created unique design options that have broadened the trade space, but the constraints of AM must be well understood for successful implementation. The engineer must have a thorough understanding of the specific AM process to be used, as well as knowledge of the projected life cycle of the part.
