7.1.1. WHEN TO USE AM
Before implementation of the AM design methods described in this chapter, the designer should consider whether AM is an appropriate manufacturing method for the candidate part. Generally speaking, parts with simple geometries and parts for which stock is readily available and easily machined do not benefit from AM. The considerations of when AM should be used, as discussed in Chapter 1, can truly help highly complex parts succeed, such as those used in many propulsion systems. A part with a very simple geometry, such as an orifice plate or a cylinder, derives little to no benefit from AM. In fact, the mechanical properties, cost, and lead time could likely be worse than those for the traditionally machined part. For example, an orifice plate is a component that is quite easy to machine, and it might require the flexibility of a change-out to a different orifice internal diameter during the course of testing. It is a trivial matter to machine orifice plates and include several “blanks” in which different size holes can quickly be drilled as needed during the course of testing. In other cases, the size of the part alone will determine its applicability. Metal AM technology is currently limited to machines less than 3 m along any dimension because of the build volume limitations discussed in Chapter 2. Because technology is always evolving, designers should research the capability within the industry as they undertake a part design. The advantages of AM are realized most often for assemblies that consist of a large number of parts, with liquid rocket engine injectors being a prime example. For typical injectors, it takes an incredible amount of time and effort to machine the small, individual elements and then precisely assemble and braze them into the injector body. These complications are completely eliminated by designing the elements into an AM injector body. This ability to save time, labor, and cost makes injectors—and many propulsion hardware components—worthy candidates to consider for AM. Most parts will fall somewhere between these two examples. The decision of whether or not to use AM is almost always left as a judgment to be made by the designer. Many examples of applications and performance characteristics to consider when using AM are outlined in Chapter 8. As alluring and exciting as new technologies are, AM should still be viewed as another manufacturing option. As such, AM should still be traded with all traditional techniques. The optimal solution for a complex component will often include a mix of AM and traditional processes. The following sections will provide a brief introduction to the different DfAM areas covered later in this chapter in more detail.
