7.1.2.1. WHAT MAKES THE AM DESIGN PROCESS UNIQUE?
AM is not a one-stop solution. All manufacturing processes should be considered; AM should be used only when it makes technical, economic, and programmatic sense. The uniqueness and limitations of AM processes should be well understood, as discussed in preceding chapters, before diving into a design. There are many trade-offs in fully enabling the design space and features that AM offers. The function of the part and design solution must carefully be reviewed to include the limitations of AM. These limitations may include various alloy properties, increased surface texture when using a part in the as-built condition, or the additional post-processing. These unique design considerations will be presented and addressed throughout this chapter.
7.1.2.2. COORDINATE SYSTEMS
For purposes of standardization, the X and Y axes are defined as being parallel to the build plate, with the build direction (Z ) perpendicular to the build plate, as shown in Fig. 7.1. This is also discussed in Sec. 2.1.2, “AM Coordinate Systems,” in Chapter 2 and Sec. 3.2, “Introduction to Metals for AM” in Chapter 3.
7.1.2.3. METAL AM PROCESS SELECTION
Since each AM process has its own costs, build schedules, material properties, and post-processing that must be taken into consideration, the process to be used must be traded early in the design phase. Part of the selection process may include providing a rough conceptual design to vendors who specialize in different AM processes. In doing this, a dialogue can be established regarding equipment capabilities and whether they align with the design requirements. The feedback gained from this exercise will likely help downselect AM processes. For example, a complex part such as a liquid rocket injector is much better suited for the L-PBF process than for the LPDED process because current
