7.1.2.4. MODELING CHALLENGES
Although AM allows for greatly expanded design solutions, there are some general rules that constrain potential designs. For example, in L-PBF, a hole printed horizontally to the build direction (Z ) will induce a build failure if the hole diameter is larger than about 5– 8 mm. Smaller-diameter holes and similarly sized rounded openings can be printed, but there may be issues with meeting required geometric tolerances and minimal hole size limits for adequate powder removal.
7.1.2.5. COMMUNICATION WITH AM SUPPLY CHAIN
In the development of AM parts, it is critical that the supply chain be reviewed and integrated early in the process. There are many nuances during each step of the process, and those steps must be completed in a specific order, as discussed in preceding chapters. Early communication during each step, with all disciplines involved, will help ensure a successful part. Some designs may not be easily printed and, consequently, may require support features or modifications. This may be obvious to an experienced machine operator, but not the engineer. Postprocessing operations, especially machining, can challenge even an experienced machinist because of the setup requirements. It is important to have the design engineer, AM part supplier, and machining teams work together in the development of AM parts.
7.1.2.6. UNDERSTANDING THE OVERALL PROCESS
All AM parts require pre- and post-processing schemes that can vary based on the performance or features required. Executing a step out of sequence could result in scrapped parts or compromised performance in the finished part.
7.1.2.7. EFFECT OF AM ON PART FUNCTION
AM allows for the construction of complex internal features not possible with traditional techniques, which is a significant benefit for propulsion applications. However, the benefit of opening the design space with AM must be weighed against the consequences of using AM. For example, a rougher surface finish in the as-built part, imperfect holes, or property variations between thin and thick sections may have negative impacts on certain aspects of the part’s performance. Fluid dynamic performance may be degraded through increased pressure drop and expected part life may decrease if the rough surface finish increases the
