7.1.3. DESIGN PLANNING
There are standard practices that go into the design of hardware, and those practices are well understood and used in industry. For instance, the specification of tolerances to account for dimensional uncertainty associated with manufacturing processes is a universally accepted practice. Planning for tightly linked operations that must be performed in sequence to create the specified component is also part of standard industry practices. When designing and manufacturing parts using the AM processes discussed in this book, it is essential to revisit those very traditional aspects of design [1– 4]. The true capability and breadth of opportunity offered by AM processes must be balanced directly with the design cycle and other considerations associated with AM parts. Although specific design practices of various engineering disciplines are referenced in this chapter, the scope of this book does not include the fundamental theories behind those practices. Other literature beyond this book should be consulted if those details are needed. The design aspects covered in this chapter are divided into several sections. The first section offers a philosophical approach to the development of an AM part. The second section, somewhat parallel to the philosophical approach, delves into the unique processing of AM, the factors that drive these processes, and how to plan for them. Many opportunities for failure are present in the process and must be mitigated early in the design. The third section of the chapter addresses design techniques, considerations, examples, and processes based on best practices from the viewpoint of the authors. This prescribed approach is not allencompassing and is certainly not the only way to achieve a successful design. The objective is to provide an approach to AM design that references the guidelines, steps, and recommendations covered in preceding chapters. The combination of the different subject areas in addition to general design practices define the AM design cycle.
