7.3. DfAM: LASER POWDER BED FUSION (L-PBF)
L-PBF is a common AM process for high-complexity components. This section provides an overview of various design considerations. Although these rules are based on lessons learned and industry experience, they may not work for every design and alloy. As noted in many sections throughout the book, there is no defined universal sequential nature or flow by which to apply all of these design considerations. Each part is unique and requires a specific process flow to be defined. As discussed in the DfAM process overview, there are many aspects that are interdisciplinary, interconnected, and iterative. The reader should go through the entire section to grasp all considerations before applying these lessons. The L-PBF process enables the manufacturing of designs that were once prohibitively expensive or impossible with traditional manufacturing techniques. Although AM capabilities initially appear to be endless, limits do exist, and the engineer needs to learn how to navigate within the boundaries of the technology to develop and produce quality AM parts. It is imperative to have a thorough understanding of the material, part geometry, and AM machine when designing for L-PBF. One of L-PBF’s greatest merits is the high fidelity of the manufactured part to the CAD model. However, even with this capability, error can still be introduced into the final product, and the surface finish will not match the smoothness of a machined surface. Although L-PBF can build features as small as 0.1 mm (0.004 in.), the feature geometry can vary considerably and may be unreliable, especially for smaller feature sizes, with significant variation, as discussed in Chapter 8. With this in mind, L-PBF parts almost always require final machining as part of their design. The notion of printing features such as seal grooves, threaded inlets, and interfaces should not be entertained. Another consideration when designing for L-PBF is that the build boxes of L-PBF machines are limited in volume. A majority of the L-PBF machines available for component fabrication are sized to accommodate dimensions of 250 mm 250 mm 300 mm (9.8 in. 9.8 in. 11 in.), but new machines
