1. Overhangs: A designer should avoid exceeding the maximum unsupported overhang surface angle to prevent build failure. When an angle is required that is beyond the advised angle limits, support structures must be added to the design. These support structures provide design challenges of their own, as discussed later in this chapter.
2. Abrupt thickness transitions: The thermal effects from printing can cause accuracy issues and warping as brought on by abrupt thickness transitions.
3. Trapped volumes: Enclosed volumes trap powder; these are fixed with access features, which can be easy when properly designed into the part. They can also be very challenging based on part geometry and requirement design limitations.
4. Layering: Layer marks or stair steps result from the intrinsic nature of the printing process; final machining can remove these features if the region is accessible.
5. Cleanliness: Traces of metal powder remain on the surface after a build and should be carefully removed when cleanliness is required.
6. Fine part details: Small features can be warped during the build process or become damaged during post processing. Test builds of the required small features can be used to determine which features might be susceptible.
7. Nondestructive evaluation (NDE) requirements: Inspection of internal features often requires nondestructive testing (NDT) to verify and validate. There is no easy design solution for this. It is an inherent consequence of DfAM.
8. Tessellation: Because the CAD file is broken up into triangles when exported to a file type that can be used with AM machine software, such as an STL file,
