7.3.1. L-PBF FEATURE RESOLUTION AND FEATURES
L-PBF, with its minimal layer thicknesses (20 –60 mm), small laser spot size (40 120 mm), and smaller powder particle size distribution (PSD; 10 –53 mm), is a much higher-resolution process than DED. This higher resolution capability allows finer detail to be printed, but smaller features come at the expense of dimensional accuracy. The design of the AM part also impacts feature resolution. For example, certain angles between the axis of the laser beam and the axis normal to the part surface can cause stair-step features on the surface. The stair steps are derived from layer heights and corresponding slicing. AM methods never produce surface finishes as smooth as those obtained with traditional machining. Post-machining may be required to smooth the surface to an acceptable level. Common design constraints with respect to feature resolution include aspect ratio, maximum bridge or overhang distance, wall thickness, horizontal and vertical hole size limitations, and maximum overhang angle [6]. Examples are shown in Fig. 7.10. Pillars, support structures, and posts are all constrained by aspect ratio, which is defined as the height-to-width ratio of a part. Maximum bridge or overhang distance defines the maximum length of material overhang that can be unsupported in the orientation parallel to the build plate (see Sec. 7.3.4 on overhang angles). Usually, a support structure should still be used when approaching the limit of the maximum overhang distance. Determining wall thickness limitations involve finding the minimum printed thickness without pockets or warping of the wall. This method can also be used for defining minimum wall thicknesses for channel and manifold walls found in cooling jackets for thrust chambers. Determining maximum unsupported horizontal hole sizes involves finding the maximum overhang distance where no support is needed. Horizontal hole sizes also have minimum and maximum limits, such that the minimum feature size cannot be printed and the maxim