7.3.12. BUILD-PLATE CONSIDERATIONS
The build plate is the substrate onto which the feedstock is deposited during the AM process. Not only must the build-plate material be metallurgically compatible with the alloy being deposited, it must also be of sufficient thickness to withstand the considerable residual stresses imparted on it by the part under construction. Generally, if the build-plate material is similar to the deposited material, there is compatibility. However, the decision of which build-plate material to use is highly dependent on logistical and economic factors such as cost and availability. Ease of machining of the build plate should also be considered because it is common practice to resurface the plate for future use after the current part is removed. Because of its low cost, high availability, and ease of manufacture, stainless steel (SS), such as 304 SS or mild steel, is the most widely used build-plate material for AM processes. Table 7.1 lists several common build-plate materials that can be used for L-PBF materials. Build-plate thickness can range from 10 mm for parts on small AM platforms to 50 mm for large-format AM machines. The thickest build plates are reserved for thick parts that impart enormous stresses onto the plate. More detail on this aspect of L-PBF can be found in Chapter 2. If the build plates are not sufficiently thick, the resulting distortion can make removal of the bolts holding the plate to the AM platform incredibly difficult. In extreme circumstances, these bolts may need to be broken, which is never a good option because this can damage the threaded holes on the AM machine. To avoid this scenario, thicker
