7.3.17. WITNESS SPECIMENS
Components in development are normally printed without witness specimens to allow for quick design iterations. Witness specimens differ from materials development and characterization specimens, which are used to determine properties or to develop a qualified metallurgical process, as discussed in Chapters 4 and 9. Once a design has been finalized, witness specimens are required to establish the build quality of the final part. Witness specimens and characterization specimens should each include unique identifiers to allow traceability of the build and sample location. Depending on the operating environment, post-processing, and certification requirements (Chapter 9, Table 9.2), it is common to include individually serialized witness specimens. This will depend strongly on the component classification (A, B, or C), but some general guidelines include the following:
1. Metallographic specimens consist of two specimens with full-build height, one characterized in the as-built condition and the other in the fully heat-treated condition.
2. Tensile specimens comprise 6– 15 specimens machined into ASTM E8 geometries from full-build-height cylinders that complete all heat-treatment operations along with the part. The gauge sections are centered at the same Z height or in groups at various Z heights. An alternative is to print ASTM E8 specimens directly to characterize properties with the as-built surface finish as opposed to the machined surface finish.
3. Fatigue specimens comprise 5– 10 specimens that complete all heat-treatment operations along with the part. It is recommended that these specimens be tested in the as-built surface condition (worst-case scenario) and that a second set be tested if machining or a different surface enhancement technique is to be used.
