7.4. DfAM: DIRECTED ENERGY DEPOSITION (DED)
The DED process provides many advantages for the design of AM components, including the ability to manufacture parts on a much larger scale and with a higher deposition rate than the powder bed fusion (PBF) processes (see Chapter 2). Multiple materials can also be used within DED processes, allowing additional design opportunities for local optimization of material properties. To take full advantage of the DED process, a new set of design rules is required to address the challenges of properly manufacturing these parts while also minimizing post-processing [10]. As discussed in Chapter 2, the DED process uses a larger spot size than PBF, and as a result, the build feature resolution is lower than for PBF [11]. Although thin walls and basic internal features can be accomplished with DED, the process does not offer the same type of complexity as PBF. Another major design difference is that DED does not accommodate removable or lattice support structures such as those used for L-PBF, although some software advancements do allow limited arch supports for DED. Examples of various LP-DED parts can be seen in Fig. 7.36.
