7.4.1. RESOLUTION OF FEATURES AND WALL THICKNESSES
DED technology allows for larger feature sizes than are possible with PBF techniques [12]. This capability is driven by the spot size of the directed energy source, usually a laser. The spot size will determine the wall thickness, with the wall always thicker than the melt pool as the material deposits. The resultant wall thickness is strongly dependent on the alloy, but a typical DED minimum wall thickness is 1 mm (0.039 in.), although larger spot sizes on the order of 3 mm (0.12 in.) are often used to increase deposition rates [13]. The resulting wall thickness achieved with a less than 1 mm spot size ranges from 1 to 1.2 mm (0.039 to 0.047 in.) when deposited as a single bead [14]. The required spot size for a part is highly dependent on the required feature resolution and overall rigidity of the part. Decisions regarding spot size should be made early in the design phase because spot size will dictate the design concept. Thinner walls have been deposited down to 0.5 mm, but walls with such thin profiles further limit geometrical parameters such as build angles [15]. With multiple passes or hatch patterning, thicker walls can easily be produced with DED, and the re-melting of material that comes with multiple passes offers microstructure advantages by causing grains to break up [16]. As observed with traditional methods such as casting, thick and thin regions of AM parts can also vary in properties based on varying heating and cooling rates as discussed in Chapters 3 and 4, and so wall thickness should be taken into account [17].Although there is no practical upper limit on wall thickness, thicker walls must be balanced with residual thermal stresses because distortions may occur as more heat is injected into the part. Similarly to traditional manufacturing, all AM processes experience some level of thermal shrinkage due
