With the larger spot size of DED, the resolution of features is less than that of L-PBF. Small radii (less than 0.254 mm [0.01 in.]) are generally included in designs to avoid stress concentration sites during printing and end-use operation. Although small radii are easy to fabricate using L-PBF, they can be more challenging in DED, and small radii less than 0.254 mm (0.01 in.) should be avoided. These fine radii are not easily created because of the lower resolution of the DED process parameters being used. Radii can create a secondary issue in that the toolpath programming software will attempt a correction and create a path based on the relationship between the bead width and the radius. This can cause errors or increase the deposition time. Partial or incomplete radii may be created because of the surface tension of the material. This is dependent on the viscosity of each alloy. Two examples of partial radii can be seen in Fig. 7.45.
