7.4.9. SURFACE ROUGHNESS
Because of the relatively larger melt pool and coarse powder used, surface roughness can be higher for DED parts than for L-PBF parts. The larger melt pool results in a more distinct stair step or staircase on angled surfaces, and undulations or waviness on vertical and angled surfaces is caused by the deposited beads [25– 27]. The stair-step effect is a function of the overlap and height spacing of the deposited material. The stair step can be more pronounced as the build overhang angle increases [28, 29]. Reducing the stair step, which is derived from the layer height, can reduce the surface roughness, but at the expense of increasing the build time [30]. Surface roughness has been documented for the LP-DED process and can range between 13 and 70 mm (line average roughness), but is highly dependent on the laser spot size, feature geometry, angles, deposition parameters, and material. The optimal parameters will vary among the different alloys [31, 32]. A discrete value is not easily provided based on these factors and should be tested or confirmed before full implementation in a design. Chapter 2 provides an overview of surface roughness for LP-DED, in addition to describing the effects of basic input parameters on surface roughness. Generally, a higher power will reduce surface roughness, but this increase in power also results in a lower resolution of features that can be built. In the DED process, different phenomena can account for the varying levels of surface roughness. There is also a difference in surface roughness on vertical or angled surfaces compared to horizontal surfaces. The primary and often prominent waviness of the surface is caused by the larger deposition beads and the overlap and stair-stepping effect, or layer height and offset, of those beads. The overlap, bead width, and bead shape are often the primary causes of the waviness on horizontal surfaces. The stair step, defined by the layer height, is the primary cause of the waviness for vertical or angled surfaces. The secondary roughness is attributed to the adhesion of excess powder and is common because not all of the powder is fully melted in the melt pool. This roughness is also affected by the melt pool because the powder blown into the melt pool is larger than the melt pool, which is also larger than the beam spot size. (See Fig. 2.50 in Chapter 2.) The excess powder that causes the roughness is partially melted or adhered onto adjacent walls or features, which increases surface roughness. This can be observed in Fig. 7.54. As discussed in Chapter 6, the most common powder size for LP-DED is 45– 105 mm or 45– 150 mm. Changes in parameters and powder size to a finer cut (10– 45mm) can improve surface finish, but it may impact build geometry limitations or cause challenges with machine operation such as powder feed rate and consistency. Geometry can also impact surface finish. It has been shown that surface roughness can differ on internal and external surfaces, where closed
