7.4.10. DATUMS
Datums with DED can be more challenging than those with L-PBF. For L-PBF, datums are often created on the part and not on the build plate. This is also true for DED, as the build plate can distort. However, the part datums may not have a high tolerance because of increased surface roughness or waviness. DED may require machine probing and three-dimensional scanning to determine the locations that require machining to ensure that proper dimensions are met. A common technique using three-dimensional scanning techniques is to best fit or “find” the part within the available stock to set up a local coordinate system for further post-processing such as machining. Datums are further discussed in Chapter 5 regarding machining.
7.4.11. BUILD FOUNDATION
DED requires a build foundation such as a build plate or, in some cases, the surface of an existing part, as discussed in Sec. 7.4.8, “DED Nonsymmetrical Features and Part Complexity.” Unlike PBF, however, the initial build foundation does not need to be a planar surface. The initial build foundation, in theory, can be almost any shape as long as it is of sufficient area and thickness, with enough thermal mass, for deposition of the powder without distortion of the build foundation. The build foundation does not need to be the same material because multiple materials may be used within some LP-DED machines. Preliminary work is required beforehand to ensure that all of the build parameters are properly set to maintain a satisfactory bond between the two materials.
