7.4.12. POWDER REMOVAL
The DED process deposits powder locally rather than in full layers as in L-PBF, which means that there is less likelihood for tightly packed, trapped powder. There are still opportunities for trapped powder, however, and it can be a challenge to detect and remove this powder. Therefore, powder removal must be considered early in the design process. As shown in Chapter 2 (Fig. 2.50), the powder efficiency is based on the spot size used and the subsequent melt pool. The smaller spot size approaching 1 mm will result in only 20% of the powder being fully melted in the melt pool. The residual powder will dispense locally onto parts of the surrounding exposed tooling or build chamber. This can be problematic if internal features are being fabricated because powder may become trapped. Although not as densely packed as L-PBF powder, DED powder still requires drains or exit paths for those internal features. With properly designed powder drains, powder can often be observed exiting from internal features during the build.
7.4.13. CLOSEOUT OF DEAD-HEADED VOLUMES
Unlike L-PBF, the DED process allows for the possibility of completely closed volumes with very little internally trapped powder, depending on the build strategy. The closure of volumes likely requires multiple axes to be used. If no amount of trapped powder can be tolerated within the part, the same design rules for powder removal should be used to successfully close these volumes. Powder removal ports may need to be included and closed out with an alternative method such as wire welding. Parts with hermetically sealed chambers will need some form of venting before any post-deposition heat treatment or HIP process. Heat treating parts with a hermetically sealed cavity can cause overpressurization within the cavity, leading to a catastrophic failure of the part.
7.4.14. CNC/DED HYBRID MANUFACTURING
Advances in manufacturing technology have led to the development of machines that combine both additive and subtractive manufacturing by merging DED with multi-axis CNC machining. These hybrid DED/CNC manufacturing machines, unlike PBF machines, are not restricted to a single build direction or to a single metal, and they offer the advantage of smooth, machined finishes in locations where smooth walls might not be possible with PBF. This technology is also scalable, allowing for larger parts to be built.
