7.5.1. METHOD FOR AM DRAWINGS
A minimum of two drawings are typically generated when designing for AM: an as-built drawing and a post-processing or as-machined (i.e. end use part) drawing. The as-built drawing, along with the associated part file, helps to identify potential issues with the AM build, whereas the post-processing/as-machined drawing is a typical machining drawing with some extra AM-specific notes. As-built and final machined drawings generally stem from a single design that acts as the skeleton for these other drawings. The information provided by this initial skeleton should include the overall outer geometry, functional interfaces, build plate, and added materials. Some designs can start with the final part geometry and then add stock material/features to define the AM build. Other designs will start with the as-built, or printed part, and then remove material/features to create the final as-machined part. Regardless of the strategy, it is prudent to understand both the as-built design and the machined design to prevent any problems or design difficulties during the design process. The as-built drawing provides a reference to verify that the as-built part matches the CAD model. The as-built drawing also provides machining
