7.6. MODEL-BASED CONFIGURATION MANAGEMENT AND VERIFICATION
Once a functional AM design has been achieved, the next step is preparing the AM design for printing through a combination of “model checking” and “model tracking.” Both terms are forms of model verification, whereby the engineer is verifying that the model’s geometry remains constant during the model file transformation process from native CAD format to manufacturing file format. Model checking is done to evaluate the quality of a design, with the goal of preventing build failures. Model checking is primarily performed toward the end of the design process—although it can be incorporated throughout the design process—to ensure that the CAD geometry will properly build under the given parameters. Model tracking is done to ensure that the as-built part is geometrically the same, to the extent possible, as the native CAD model. Model tracking requires that attention be given to how the model changes through the model format transformation process, from the native CAD model to a generic file. As discussed in Sec. 7.3, some design critical features may require sample prints to determine whether they are properly being printed. For example, a particularly critical injector element might require model tracking to determine whether the small inlets make their way from the CAD file to the STL file. Further checks may be required by printing a test part of just the element to ensure that it prints properly. The overall process of model checking and tracking is known as model verification as shown in Fig. 7.58.
