7.6.2. MODEL TRACKING
Model tracking can be broken down into the digital and physical realms. The digital aspect of model tracking—tracking how the native CAD file transforms into the AM machine file format—should always be investigated for consistency across the original design and subsequent file transformations. The physical side of model tracking ensures that the AM part meets intended dimensions. This is usually done by inspection methods, as discussed in Chapter 5. File conversion involves taking the native CAD file and converting it into a generic file format that can be read by the build layout software before parameters are applied and it is loaded onto the AM platform. The middle conversion file format is part of the slicing process, which converts the CAD model into individual layers that can be used by the AM printer. A common file format, STL, converts the CAD file into a surface represented by an array of triangles. STL is a heritage file type developed for SLA machines, which are the earliest forms of AM machines. The nature of the triangular array of an STL file results in tessellated surfaces that vary based on the chosen chord heights. When designing for propulsion applications, it is recommended that the resolution of the model be as high as possible when converting to STL to capture the finer details of the various surface features. Unfortunately, this does result in larger file sizes that can be prohibitive to generate. An example of the same model converted into an STL using various options is seen in Fig. 7.59a. The print results of a faceted surface that was derived from the STL file, not the original CAD file, can be seen in Fig. 7.59b. During the file conversion from parametric CAD to STL, an engineer should use the highest-resolution settings to prevent excessive tessellation. Although the STL file sizes are substantially larger when high-resolution settings are used, it is common practice to do so and particularly important when the part contains fine geometric features. Under certain circumstances, a highly tessellated part will not meet performance requirements or will thus constitute a build failure, as shown in Fig. 7.59b. Although unusual, the outputs of tessellation algorithms can also generate inaccurate results, which is why it is important to always check the STL file against the native CAD file to ensure that critical design features are captured. It is common practice to convert parametric CAD files to a software-agnostic format that can be used by various formats. These
