7.6.3. MODEL AND IT SECURITY
Digital documentation, such as component requirement files and CAD models, are used throughout the entire AM process. In many ways, digital documentation defines the process, as it does with nearly all modern-day manufacturing processes. Information technology (IT) security is extremely important, as the exchange of this information can be vulnerable. An engineer should always adhere to organizational cybersecurity policies to protect information.
7.7. TOPOLOGY OPTIMIZATION AND GENERATIVE DESIGN
AM unlocks vast new levels of geometric complexity for engineers to employ in their designs. On the upside, these new design spaces can offer previously inaccessible part performance, especially where intricate or organic-looking geometries are desirable. Often, however, there is so much design freedom that it can be difficult to determine where to begin. The integration of topology-optimized algorithms into AM part design is a topic that is being explored and used. Components can be designed with extremely efficient packaging to reduce overall volume, enhance flow paths and loads paths, and ultimately reduce overall mass. AM has also brought about new design and analysis philosophies that were not previously possible. These include organic designs that result in highly complex organic-looking shapes that minimize mass with a load path equivalent to that of a traditional component, as well as similar margins. Mass savings are a tremendous design opportunity with AM through increased freedom of part design. Although organic mimicry designs produced using topology optimization and generative design methods are an opportunity for additional mass savings, load paths (mechanical and thermal) and material properties must be well understood for these techniques to be applied with certainty. Examples of parts designed using various types of topology optimization and generative design algorithms are shown in Fig. 7.60. Figure 7.60a displays an example of a turbopump housing with conformal ribbing generated on the surface to increase stiffness and decrease weight. Figure 7.60b shows a structural
