7.7.3. ASSUMPTIONS AND LIMITATIONS
7.7.3.1. INPUT QUALITY: GARBAGE IN, GARBAGE OUT
A part generated through an algorithm is only as good as the algorithm and the input data employed. The computer science phrase “garbage in, garbage out” certainly applies here, even with established commercial codes such as topology optimization tools. As with any other simulation, sufficient mesh quality and selection of representative boundary conditions are critical to the outcome. These factors directly influence the resulting part geometry.
7.7.3.2. SUPPORTED PHYSICS
Not all physical effects can be simulated, let alone in the independent and iterative manner required for topology optimization. Some tools can accommodate simple nonlinear effects in the optimization loop, but they often require simulations external to the main optimization loop. It is critical for users to recognize which physical effects are not considered in their optimization. A common occurrence is the exclusion of buckling effects in many structural topology optimization examples, as evidenced in many marketing demonstration parts with long and skinny load-bearing members. The results of topology optimization must always be validated with detailed simulation before proceeding.
7.7.3.3. OPTIMIZATION VERSUS INSPIRATION
Topology optimization algorithms optimize only the mathematical problem posed to the software. Rarely is this fully representative of the real engineering problem at hand. In practice, many engineering problems have factors that cannot be succinctly described in a single tractable optimization
