problem. This is common in AM, where factors such as part orientation influence the cost (and potentially even the economic go/no-go viability) of a part, but also what geometries are even possible. Simultaneously optimizing a part for structural performance, manufacturing success, and economic viability is an unrealistic task for current tools. Engineers should consider topology optimization tools as initial design inspiration, not “one-click” shortcuts. Similarly, more abstract generative algorithms should be considered design accelerators rather than independent designers. Human engineering is still required.
7.7.3.4. DIMINISHING RETURNS
Topology optimization is an efficient method of identifying where material is needed in a design space. If there is already design inspiration, whether through a predicate part or intuitive design knowledge (e.g., I-beams, arches, honeycombs), then sometimes, the problem is not worth the computational cost of running a topology optimization. For maximum savings, topology optimization is best employed on broad design spaces rather than predicate parts that have been refined over the years (e.g., a tie-rod or spring).
