7.8. SUMMARY
DfAM is an integrated process that incorporates many multidisciplinary factors to consider for a successful AM part to be built and placed into service. The major steps (metal alloy selection, part design and analysis, AM process selection, supply chain, post-processing, testing and validation, and certification) all have dependencies that must be balanced to fully realize the benefits of AM for propulsion applications. The engineer should construct a process plan that details all of the steps in the manufacturing process from design to finished part. Once the design has been created, it is recommended that all of the steps be iterated to ensure the issues are addressed early and risks of failure during the manufacturing process are reduced. The principles of DfAM that allow an engineer to put these concepts into practice are provided in this chapter. It is important for the designer to maintain clear communication with all parties involved in the AM process steps. Proper integration of AM parts into systems requires that strict GD&T and configuration management be followed, whether in the form of drawings or digital model notes. Topology optimization and generative design are emerging tools that can allow for full optimization of structural, thermal, and other disciplines when end-use environments and model inputs are well known. DfAM is central to the entire AM process and critical for successful implementation.
