8.2. PERFORMANCE CRITERIA AND THE AM PROCESS
A thorough understanding of hardware performance criteria is paramount for design for additive manufacturing (DfAM; Chapter 7). First, performance can be quantified by overarching topics, such as mass, mechanics, heat transfer, and fluid dynamics. Mechanical performance is heavily determined by the metal selection (Chapter 3), build process (Chapter 2), and heat treatment (Chapter 4). Mechanical performance, such as fatigue life, can be limited in the as-built condition for many alloys. Fluid flow is primarily influenced by component build features such as average surface finish and flow geometry. Mass reduction can be inherent to DfAM through novel design approaches such as topology optimization and part count reduction. AM also allows for localized optimization, such as adding extra material only where necessary or using multiple alloys as needed in specific areas to optimize strength-to-weight ratios. Heat transfer can be augmented with DfAM using local features such as optimized (fluid and heat) flow paths, increased surface area through lattice structures, and high surface roughness to increase convective heat transfer. All of these aspects are iterative in the design cycle. There are many interdisciplinary connections and operations in the overall AM life cycle. An example is shown for a combustion chamber in Fig. 8.5 using an N2 diagram. Although this is a specific example, it demonstrates how intrinsically woven the AM process is and how failure to consider a single step impacts performance. Multiple aspects of AM component performance are highly dependent on the surface finish. For heat-transfer devices, the surface finish of internal passages can alter the total pressure drop and enhance the convective heat-transfer coefficient to a modest or even significant extent. Studies have reported a positive correlation between surface finish and cooling effectiveness, with a greater than 100% increase in heat transfer observed using a channel with high roughness (areal average roughness Sa 1⁄4 12 mm) [3]. Alternatively, hydraulic losses can compromise an AM component’s net performance. Modifications to Inconel 718 L-PBF swirl coaxial injectors (to reduce wall-fluidic shear stress and flow separation) demonstrated empirical improvements of 19% and 8% for fuel and oxidizer discharge coefficients, respectively, when comparing the as-built surfaces to smooth surfaces [4]. It was suggested that further mechanical honing or abrasive flow machining (AFM; see Chapter 5) would further increase the flow area and improve the associated discharge coefficients.
