If it is not yet apparent, the entire AM process from conception to post-processing is integrally linked to the component’s overall performance and must start in the design phase. However, the fundamental limitations on the geometric features that can be printed using AM can account for a large percentage of the component’s performance capability. The complexity of design allows an engineer to achieve certain performance criteria. Critical design features can be affected by alloy selection and build parameter selection allowing for adjustments to be made within the build process (Chapter 2). The post-processing must also be carefully considered in combination with the build process as part of the initial design considerations [31]. Designing the bulk features of an AM component is easy to produce the majority of performance gains. However, understanding the performance limitations is more challenging for an engineer. Part refinement to achieve less than a few percentage points worth of performance gains is a common situation for aerospace engineers, especially near the end of the design process. These gains can come at a loss of time and cost efficiency for the project. An engineer should further understand that, through an improvement in component performance, they may inadvertently increase the total possible failure modes through increased component complexity. Every manufacturing process, including AM, has inherent constraints. Certain aspects of the component’s design may not be possible with AM or may result in a higher risk of failure during printing or postprocessing (i.e., incomplete powder removal, distortion, cracking, residual stresses). High rates of build failures are much more likely to be encountered than the complete inability to produce a component. To mitigate build risk, AM-process-specific limitations, as discussed in Chapters 2 and 7, must be understood before design optimization. For example, an inner port diameter may have a manufacturing tol