8.3.3. SURFACE ROUGHNESS
Surface roughness can be a significant challenge with metal AM components. Several factors impact the surface roughness, including build angles, recoater type (L-PBF), dross (L-PBF), surface geometry, directions of recoater and gas flow, metal alloy feedstock, powder size, component complexity, build parameters, and melt-pool stability [33– 35]. It is generally not feasible to provide a single surface roughness value, as surface roughness depends on a number of factors described, including the specific geometry of the component. Surface roughness has a negative impact on fatigue life because of the stress risers resulting from the AM build itself. This roughness also has impacts on increased friction factors for flow applications. Although this effect is negative for pressure losses, it can be positive for heat-transfer applications, increasing surface area and promoting turbulence in the flow. The surface roughness parameter used ubiquitously across the field is the arithmetic mean deviation of the assessed profile, with the symbol Ra, or its equivalent Sa from three-dimensional areal profilometry. These measurement techniques largely fall into two categories: contact and noncontact profilometry.
