8.4.6. HEAT-TRANSFER ENHANCEMENTS USING AM
Mounting struts are used to fix and isolate cryogenic tanks from the spacecraft to minimize heat transfer from the vehicle structure to the tank that would induce boil-off. The traditional approach is to use an S-glass tube bonded to Invar 36 or Ti6Al4V clevises with multilayer insulation (MLI) within the tube. These struts are not mass-optimized and require significant touch labor and time to manufacture. The increased design flexibility of AM allows for either passive or active isolation of cryogenic tanks while in orbit. Use of DfAM can develop performance-, mass-, and cost-optimized cryostruts. For example, heat transfer across a strut can be minimized by using lattices to minimize conduction (Fig. 8.31). Analysis and experimentation have found that lattice effective thermal conductivity is inversely proportional to the void fraction (Fig. 8.32). Use of lattices reduced the mass by approximately 30 –40% compared of fulldensity AM designs and reduced the thermal conductivity to approximately 10% of that of fully dense designs. Effective thermal conductivity in these parts is proportional to the percentage RD (solid-volume-dominated) and is dependent more on unit cell size and strut thickness than on specific topology types.
