8.5.1. FATIGUE FRACTURE MECHANISM
It is essential for clarification purposes to cover some basic topics, including the typical fatigue fracture mechanism and why the surface texture plays such an important role. The three stages of fatigue fracture are crack initiation, followed by crack propagation, and ending in fracture and component failure [62]. Crack initiation generally occurs at the surface, or near the surface, at points known as stress concentration sites or fatigue initiating sites [63]. Stress concentration sites are usually discontinuity points from the crystal structures at or near the surface (Chapter 4), in the form of a notch that, when under a fatigue load over time, becomes a small crack. If the component’s surface is perfectly polished these surface stress concentration sites can be significantly remediated, and the fatigue life of the component should significantly improve. Based on this simple principle, many researchers use the surface roughness of a component to predict mechanical performance. The basics of these measurements and approach are described in Sec. 8.4.3. A possible option for dealing with these fatigue initiation sites is to accept that they are going to be present and hidden at the surface and near-surface areas of all AM components. They can be accepted with a significant impact on fatigue life, as
