8.6. COST COMPARISONS FOR AM COMPONENTS
Although much of this chapter is focused on operational performance such as flow, fatigue, mass, and heat-transfer characteristics, it is necessary to point out cost comparisons as an advantage of AM, as discussed in Chapter 1. Generally, AM is selected based on a potential schedule and cost savings over traditional methods. These cost savings can result from many aspects such as a simplified supply chain, reduced development time (i.e., quicker to the test stand), and overall reduction in fabrication time. A typical example of this is shown Fig. 8.49, through a comparison between a traditional fabrication of a chamber and an AM fabrication (US Dollars 2020). The chamber consists of a copper-based liner with integral coolant channels and a Ni-based structural jacket to withstand operational loads. This specific chamber is used in a 155.7 kN (35,000 lbf) engine. The traditionally fabricated chamber uses electrodeposited (ED) nickel plating for closeout of the liner channels fabricated from copper alloy GRCop-84 with manifold weld preparations for welding of the Inconel 625 manifolds.
