8.7. SUMMARY AND CONCLUDING REMARKS
This chapter has presented several examples of performance characteristics for AM components. Advantages and disadvantages of various performance modifying techniques and considerations for their use have been reviewed, along with numerous examples. This chapter is intended to give the reader an idea of how the AM production technique; metal alloy type; design approach and features; parameters; resulting material after build, which can be driven by feedstock; and post-processing all play critical roles in determining the overall performance of a component. In addition, proper DfAM encompasses a highly multidisciplinary approach including materials, fluid mechanics, structural and fracture mechanics, heat transfer, dynamics, management, manufacturing, and post-processing. Although an engineer does not need to be an expert in all of these fields, a fundamental understanding of each is critical to component performance optimization. Also critical to success is obtaining input from established vendors and experts in the field. It can be observed from the various case studies on fatigue that there is additional work to be completed on understanding fatigue mechanisms and surface finishing techniques. Fatigue differences are observed among the potential finishing processes (Chapter 5), in addition to differences observed for the different metal alloys and the post-processing methods such as HIP. If post-processing operations are to be used, to obtain their full benefits, sufficient material should be added to critical surfaces to allow for adequate surface removal. Material addition must be established in the design phase and must be planned for adequately.
