9.1. INTRODUCTION
Hardware made from metal alloys obtained by additive manufacturing (AM) represent a unique product form. Although they have similarities to products made by traditional fabrication processes, AM products are in the early stages of establishing consistent and well-understood processes. They have not yet benefited from decades of refinement by original equipment manufacturers (OEMs) and specialty manufacturing service providers or decades of service by end users. Therefore, the long-standing empirical foundation of traditional processes, such as casting and forging, is only now beginning its early evolution for AM. With conventional manufacturing processes, a user can rely on an approved wrought material supply chain to provide products that are produced to aerospace specifications, meet applicable material requirements, and are delivered with a certificate of compliance (CoC). This mature supply chain in the form of wrought products, often referred to generically as the “mill” or “foundry,” represents decades, or even centuries, of refinement and proficiency in the production of quality metals. Although a CoC alone does not provide a certified product, there is an implicit expectation that significant experience and rigorous traceability uphold the certificate. With AM, each machine becomes a mill in a sense through the process of turning feedstock into the final AM material product form, typically as a final or near-final part. Just as with traditional products, the metallurgical characteristics of AM metals must be controlled throughout the process, from start to finish. This control begins with the starting feedstock and is continued through the machine process parameters that provide the initial material quality, as well as the subsequent post-processing that results in the final microstructure and associated material properties. This fundamental sequence is discussed as part of the process –structure-property – performance relationship in Chapters 3 and 4. The responsibility for assuring metallurgical integrity throughout the AM build process and throughout the volume of the AM part belongs with the entity operating each individual AM machine. This chapter discusses NASA’s current approach toward AM certification and methodologies for qualification of AM human-rated spaceflight hardware. As the
