AM process is still evolving, the approach to certifying AM parts for aerospace applications is still young. The overarching goal of NASA’s approach is to ensure that the certification process meets the intent of the part requirement(s) in a consistent and reliable manner. The NASA certification requirements discussed in this chapter are written to be tailorable through the evaluation and acceptance of deviations by the governing program. Given the tailorable nature of the standard(s) and early certification methodologies, it should be noted that there are other paths unique to specific programs in how certification was achieved for critical human spaceflight program AM parts flying today. This chapter provides an approach evaluated by NASA, but the ultimate certification methodology is determined by the program or company through appropriate certifying organizations. It is recognized that there are many other standards in development from international standard organizations and certification bodies [1, 2]. Some of these standards are listed in Table 9.1, and a broad list of standards (as of this writing) covering various aspects of the AM process is provided in Appendix C. Most of these documents target specific aspects of the AM process, whereas the NASA certification approach, implemented in NASA-STD-6030, “Additive Manufacturing Requirements for Crewed Spaceflight Systems” [3], and NASA-STD-6033, “Additive Manufacturing Requirements for Equipment and Facility Control” [4], endeavors to embody all aspects of the end-to-end AM process for human spaceflight. NASA has been transparent in providing these standards to the aerospace community. The approach has been vetted across numerous industry organizations and adapted by some for use in flight projects [5]. The NASA methodology is intended to provide significant rigor for AM part production, along with the necessary flexibility. With a certification approach, it is recognized that approaches and plans are often tailored for specific part needs, so it is up to the program or company to address those needs. No single plan will fit all needs. This chapter presents an overview of the NASA methodology contained in NASA-STD-6030 and provides guidance and practical approaches regarding how to establish a qualified process and certifiable hardware according to the NASA requirements. Once an AM machine has been qualified according to the specification and proper part planning is in place, the machine can be employed on a variety of hardware builds using the qualified process. NASA-STD-6030 and NASA-STD-6033 provide NASA with a framework for programs and projects to develop and manufacture AM hardware. Although it is understood that this approach will be tailored by each program, these standards establish a commonality across different organizations applying AM certification under NASA auspices. It is ultimately up to the program adopting and implementing the AM technology to decide which approach best fits its situational needs and risk posture, while maintaining requisite technical rigor. It is also important to note that the AM certification methodologies covered in this chapter address only the actions that ensure that the manufacturing process will meet the intent of
