9.3. OVERVIEW OF THE NASA AM CERTIFICATION METHODOLOGY
The definitions of qualification and certification vary by industry and even by culture within industries. The following interpretations are fairly common: Qualification applies to parts, components, and processes, whereas certification applies to designs, subsystems, and integrated subsystems. Certification is typically the highest level of approval and is granted by the responsible reviewing authority when the verification process is complete, assuring that both the design and the as-built hardware will meet the established requirements to complete the intended mission safely and reliably. AM has not changed the certification of the design side of spaceflight hardware, but it has affected how hardware is made. AM hardware certification requires integration across disciplines and throughout the certification process, as the AM process is itself highly integrated. Changes or issues in any one area of the AM process can have far-reaching effects on downstream properties or processing. For example, if metallic powder is not properly removed from an L-PBF part, solidification of residual power during thermal processing can result in a scrapped part (see Chapters 5 and 7). In some cases, surface finishing or out-of-sequence machining could result in the failure of a nondestructive evaluation (NDE) technique. A full understanding and appreciation of the AM process is required to fully implement AM certification approaches. Certification of AM hardware requires discipline to define and follow the established certification plan. It is important to note that most of the traditional certification framework remains the same. Parts, subsystems, and devices are still certified to the same requirements as for conventionally produced hardware. There are only a few activities that are unique to AM certification, but they are significant. Two examples of unique aspects of AM certification are the transitioning of roles and responsibilities to other entities, such as AM part production facilities now being largely responsible for material integrity, a responsibility previously held at the mill and covered simply by ordering certified metal feedstock, and statistical process control (SPC) methodologies that are now required in manufacturing environments that are unaccustomed to using it. NASA-STD-6030 begins with the general requirements for an Additive Manufacturing Control Plan (AMCP), which along with a Quality Management
