9.5. AM CONTROL PLAN
When starting a program that involves AM, it is important to begin with a highlevel plan for the implementation of AM. In the NASA framework, this plan is the AMCP. Figure 9.4 clearly illustrates how an AMCP is a natural boundary defining all relevant processes and procedures. As a foundational document, the AMCP enables clear communication of policy both across the organization and to any subtier vendors or suppliers that may be involved. The AMCP defines how the foundation for certification is structured and how it operates in the unique confines of any given program, project, or organization. As discussed previously, this foundation includes equipment and facility controls, personnel training, process machine qualification, material properties, and SPC. The AMCP also establishes policy for part classification, PPPs, operational norms, and many other topics. The cognizant engineering organization (CEO) is responsible for creating the AMCP. The CEO is the entity that is responsible for establishing and maintaining the certified design state of the AM hardware and for delivering AM hardware compliant with all applicable requirements. The CEO should deliver a plan that
