9.7. EQUIPMENT AND FACILITY CONTROL PLAN
The equipment and facilities that produce AM hardware for aerospace applications constitute an important part of the foundational process controls required for the safe implementation of AM. Within the NASA AM standards construct, the requirements for equipment and facilities are controlled by NASA-STD-6033, which is invoked by NASA-STD-6030. The focus of this NASA technical standard is to define the minimum requirements for the control of AM equipment, facilities, and training. These controls are defined and implemented by the AM part producer through an Equipment and Facilities Control Plan (EFCP) and approved by the CEO. EFCPs are typically unique to an AM facility. The EFCP defines procedures and work instructions for production operations (e.g., feedstock management, contamination control, computer security, and AM machine maintenance and calibration). The requirements in an EFCP are clear and well-documented and must be governed by the QMS.
9.7.1. EQUIPMENT AND FACILITIES
The EFCP will contain controls for all AM equipment throughout the process and associated facilities, such as sieves and powder removal equipment. Different machines and specific methods (directed energy deposition [DED] or PBF) will require different controls and calibrations. The metals used in these processes have different needs, such as storage and shelf-life requirements, that must be governed by specific controls. Given the complexity of the AM process, a significant number of controls are needed in areas that include, but are not limited to, the following: Tracking machine configuration status, Tracking machine qualification status, Maintenance intervals, Calibration intervals, Feedstock storage and handling, Contamination controls,
