9.8.1. FEEDSTOCK
Defining a candidate material process for any AM process begins with specifying the feedstock. It is critical that any feedstock satisfy a comprehensive, configuration-controlled specification to ensure proper consistency as new lots of material are procured over time. The feedstock specification may be a common industry standard, a proprietary company standard, or a combination of the two. The feedstock specification should address all characteristics of the powder, including production and packaging, that may influence the AM process (Chapter 6). Examples include QMS-controlled powder production; method of powder manufacture; chemistry requirements, including acceptable methods of measurement and tolerance; particle size distribution (PSD) requirements and acceptable methods for powder sampling and determining the PSD, including explicit limits on the quantities of coarse and fine particles outside the PSD range; mean particle shape (powder morphology) and limits on satellite and agglomerated particles; rheological behavior (flow and spreading) of the powder and the method of verification; controls on blending of virgin powder
