9.8.2. AM BUILD PROCESS
The second core piece of the candidate QMP is the AM build process itself. The AM build process is defined as a comprehensive list of fixed, key process variables and machine configuration details that have potential influence on the AM build process for the AM machine. The machine configuration, unique for each serial number, includes such items as the current software and firmware versions, any machine settings not controlled by the parameter build file, and the installed configurations of the physical hardware in the machine (e.g., the recoater configuration in PBF machines). This can be thought of as a snapshot in time of the AM machine as it exists at the time of qualifying the candidate QMP. Beyond the physical characteristics of the AM machine configuration are the key process variables used by the candidate QMP. These may include, but are not limited to, environmental conditions within the AM build chamber (e.g., temperature, gas flow rate, humidity, gas chemistry), process parameter settings (e.g., layer height, beam power, travel speed, beam spot size), toolpath settings (e.g.,
