9.8.4. POST-PROCESSING
The third foundational part of the candidate QMP is the post-processing of the material to a final metallurgical state for use. This is not to be confused with postprocessing that occurs at a part level, such as surface finish improvements. The processing of interest here is focused only on bringing the metallurgical state of the AM material to a stable, more uniform, and predictable condition to ensure that consistent material properties and performance are maintained over time; therefore, all post-build thermal processes that evolve the material bulk microstructure toward this uniform and orderly state are documented in the QMP. For the material to be compliant with the QMP and all associated implications regarding material properties, the AM part must be processed according to the documented post-processing procedure of the QMP. This is done to mitigate risks, both known and unknown, associated with material performance due to the complex as-built microstructure from the AM process. For a metallic AM process, such as L-PBF, these steps may include stress relief, hot isostatic pressing (HIP), annealing, or solution treating and aging, depending on the alloy. Thermal post-processing is discussed further in Chapters 3, 4, and 5. It is also important to define in the QMP any variations in the thermal post-processing that are allowed.
9.8.5. QUALIFICATION OF THE CANDIDATE MATERIAL PROCESS
Once the feedstock specifications, build process and thermal processes are defined in the candidate QMP, the next step is to formally qualify the QMP. Qualification of the material process ensures that the defined candidate QMP yields material of the intended quality and reliability. Three evaluations in the NASA methodology must be completed to qualify the material process: 1) material microstructural qualification, 2) surface texture and detail resolution, and 3) evaluation of mechanical properties.
