9.8.5.1. MATERIAL MICROSTRUCTURAL QUALIFICATION
The first evaluation to qualify the candidate QMP is the acceptability of the microstructure in the as-built condition and the final condition following thermal postprocessing. The quality of the as-built microstructure produced by the machine, before any post-processing, establishes the basis for AM part integrity. The as-built microstructure provides the best opportunity to evaluate the fundamental quality of the AM process, particularly in regard to the defect state. The intent of the evaluation is to demonstrate that the AM process exhibits a level of refinement that precludes detrimental defects and yields a starting microstructure that will react as intended to the post-process thermal processing. The as-built grain structure in the AM material is important primarily because it provides a starting point for the evolution of the grain structure in subsequent heat treating. In limited cases, the as-built microstructure may remain largely intact; thus, the quality of the as-built structure is critical, as it will directly dictate the material performance in the application. The NASA methodologies in NASA-STD-6030 are predicated upon material processes that evolve the microstructure in a consistent manner from the complex, as-built state to a final state that more closely resembles a mostly equiaxed structure of common product forms. Evolution of the material microstructure minimizes potential failure mechanisms and other complications associated with native AM microstructures (e.g., strong anisotropy, complex residual stress states, and fatigue and crack growth characteristics). Some as-built microstructures lack the stored energy to initiate full recrystallization. This is often true in cases of epitaxial grain growth (columnar grains in the build direction), where the ability to refine that grain structure in the thermal post-processing is lost or limited. This scenario defeats much of the efficiency intended by the QMP process regarding material properties and leaves the part to be evaluated extensively using a point-design approach. Evaluating the AM metal for defects in the as-built state, prior to recrystallization, provides helpful information regarding the source of the defects based on the surrounding microstructure. The expectation is that a properly refined AM process under controlled conditions during qualification should produce material with a minimum of defects. It is acknowledged that the AM process cannot be defect-free and that the level of process refinement for reducing the defect state needs to be managed for the application and pragmatic needs such as reducing the build time. Any defects present in the refined process should be randomly dispersed, not systematic or patterned. An elevated level of defects or defects that occur systematically (e.g., based on a hatch pattern) reflect a process that lacks sufficient refinement and should not pass qualification. The presence of systematic defects or a high density of defects (defects per unit volume) in the as-built structure is considered detrimental not only if they impact material performance but also because they indicate either a lack of sufficient process refinement or a loss of process control. HIP is used for void closure, but it should not be used to
