9.8.6. QMP REGISTRATION
Once a candidate QMP is fully developed and defined with a feedstock specification, build process parameter sets, and post-processes, it is carefully evaluated for microstructure, surface texture and detail resolution, and mechanical properties. When these evaluation steps are complete, a candidate QMP is ready to be qualified and approved for service. All development activities to this point have been related solely to the machine and associated processes. To make the process applicable to the production of parts in a certification framework, the QMP needs to be registered to an MPS. Only then can it be used for flight applications. QMP registration is the process of demonstrating that the material characteristics and properties from the qualified process are equivalent, in an engineering context, to those in the applicable MPS for the same material and condition. Registration is simply a term chosen for use in NASA-STD-6030 to mean that the overall process is evaluated to determine whether it fits with past data and material characteristics in the database so that the material property assumptions can be legitimately used in design. Another common term for this activity is “buy-in,” that is, can the new process “buy into” the existing data and material property framework by demonstrating engineering equivalence. Because of the sensitivity of AM process implementation, it is not legitimate to simply assume that material allowables or design values associated with previously used AM processes are applicable. The applicability must be confirmed by an assessment of engineering equivalence that will typically include some level of statistical analysis of property data in addition to semiquantitative evaluation of the microstructure, including defect states. The term “engineering equivalence” is used because an assessment of the equivalence of the candidate material to that in the MPS must be done on data sets of small size for practical reasons. This limits the ability to make rigorous statistical assessments with the desired confidence, that is, to demonstrate statistical equivalence. Instead, engineering judgement is used to look at the process holistically by evaluating the available data across many material characteristics. The registration process evaluates commonality in metal feedstock controls (Chapter 6) and microstructural characteristics, as well as key failure mechanisms in mechanical properties: strength,
