1. Feedstock specification and feedstock controls are the same as those in the MPS.
2. Documentation is available to verify that the material process definition was established and adhered to continuously during the development of evaluation metals.
3. Microstructural characteristics are consistent with those of the MPS.
4. Tensile strengths and ductility are accepted using the tensile PCRDs of the MPS.
5. Measured HCF life is accepted with respect to the fatigue PCRD of the MPS.
6. Measured LCF life is consistent with MPS data.
7. Measured fracture toughness is consistent with MPS data.
8. If applicable, tensile properties at temperature are consistent with MPS data.
These evaluations are for Class A and Class B parts. However, the evaluations are greatly simplified for Class C parts. Part classifications and their implications are discussed in Sec. 9.12.2. Upon successful registration, the material property data from the QMP should be added to the MPS database. The CEO is responsible for the registration process and final approval of the candidate process as a QMP. This responsibility is held by the CEO because it is the entity responsible for the delivered hardware. The CEO has latitude in making the final determination of registration of a material process to an MPS. If aspects of the registration data do not fully meet the specified criteria but the MPS appropriately accommodates the difference, such a rationale may be provided in the QMP record. If the data do not register to the MPS, then the CEO may opt to gather additional data, continue to improve the process, or develop a new MPS that represents the measured material performance.
9.8.7. BOOTSTRAPPING A QMP TO AN MPS
If a CEO or AM part producer is working on a new alloy and AM process, an existing MPS may not be available for QMP registration. In such cases, the QMP and MPS are developed simultaneously, with the material test data generated to evaluate the candidate material process also used to generate the initial MPS (i.e., bootstrapping). The QMP is registered to the new MPS by default. Additional data generated from the QMP are used to populate draft MPSs,
