9.9. MATERIAL PROPERTIES SUITE
At this point in the process, the work that lies within the dotted box in the top half of Fig. 9.4 has been completed. One can consider that most but not all of the foundation has been developed. The equipment and facility controls are in place, the personnel training plans are in place, and the qualified material processes are in place. Depending on the CEO’s or AM part producer’s history, the MPS that has been developed could either be bootstrapped and very small, or it could be vast if it includes all of the alloy- and process-specific data that the AM part producer has generated. If the MPS already exists and contains data from multiple machines and powder lots that have produced statistically equivalent material properties, the AM part producer may be ready to move forward with establishing process control reference distributions (PCRDs), SPC criteria, and confirmation of design properties. If the MPS has been bootstrapped, significant development is still needed to account for variability. To maintain consistency with developing aerospace industry consensus in terminology, the NASA Technical Standards use the terms “material allowable” and “design value” when referring to the properties of AM metals. Material allowable is used to represent the bulk properties of the AM material, ignoring the influencing effects of environment, surface finish, and other factors that can impact material performance. Material allowables and design values in AM require modifications to the typical approach used for traditional metallic materials. The requirements are similar to Composite Materials Handbook-17 (CMH-17) [13], which is used by the fiber-reinforced-composites community to derive design values. Important distinctions arise because of the process-sensitive nature of AM and the individual aspects of each AM machine. Remember that each machine is a foundry or mill producing the base material in addition to the part geometry. As a result, some of the traditional roles and 