9.10. PROCESS CONTROL REFERENCE DISTRIBUTION
As discussed previously, a subset of the MPS data is used for the PCRD. A PCRD statistically describes the nominal behavior of witness specimens from a specific machine. It is a simple statistical model used to create acceptance criteria for the evaluation of witness specimens that are used to monitor the AM process. The goal of the PCRD is to employ SPC to protect the process from drift. By identifying process drift early, an AM part producer can keep the properties consistent with those in the MPS over time. The use of the PCRD in witness testing justifies the continued validity of the material allowables during ongoing AM operations. Control charts are one example of acceptance criteria derived from PCRDs. Another is to use minimum and average checks for the witness specimens based on the PCRD. Under the NASA methodology, NASA-STD-6030 contains tables describing the required witness specimens for each of the hardware classifications. These tests and their quantities constitute what NASA considers the minimum required to understand the health and performance of the AM process. Within the NASA standards, there are also separate tables for AM part producers who are operating continuously (Table 9.3) and for those who are operating only intermittently, which is considered an independent build (Table 9.4). These witness specimens provide vital insight into the health of the process, as well as traceability from the initial qualification to the quality of the production hardware. The witness sample requirements for an independent build are scoped to generate enough evidence during a single build to conclude that the material produced by the machine and feedstock at the time of the build is consistent with past performance and supports the assumptions in the MPS. An AM machine that is running an identical process repeatedly without changes is considered a continuous production build process, which allows for a reduced quantity of witness specimens compared with independent builds. For property evaluations when PCRD criteria have not been established, candidate data are determined to be consistent with the MPS if the average result of
