9.12.2. PART CLASSIFICATION
Each project or program that uses AM will have varying applications, ranging from flight feasibility to subscale testing to crewed flight vehicles, and thus varying degrees of risk tolerance. Within the NASA certification methodology, part classification enables quick and consistent communication of the risk posture for a piece of hardware that is the same for all AM programs. A parts classification is defined by metrics for consequence of failure, structural demand, and AM-associated risks. Each decision gate has a set of associated criteria to prevent them from being too subjective, aiding in consistent application across all NASA programs. Throughout the NASA certification requirements, these classifications determine appropriate levels of process control, qualification, and inspection. Figure 9.12 presents a graphical representation of the primary and secondary classification system. The part classification system uses a three-tiered system divided into a primary classification based on consequence of failure and a secondary classification based on structural demand and AM risk. The primary alphabetical class is determined by the consequences of failure, with Class A being the highest, Class C being the lowest, and Class B covering the range between them. The numerical secondary subclasses for Class A and B parts are determined by a combination of the structural demand on the part and the risk associated with the AM implementation for the part. Within the NASA certification framework, the quantity of work associated with certifying a part scales with the classification of the part. For example, qualification of an AM process that will be used for a cable bracket will be far less demanding than qualification of an AM process that will be used for turbomachinery in a liquid rocket engine. This scaling was established to make process qualification and witness testing easier for AM suppliers who are manufacturing only noncritical parts. AM suppliers who will be using a process only for noncritical applications can take advantage of reduced qualification and validation requirements that will be necessary to reduce the risk associated with the hardware they produce. However, it is critical
