At this stage, production readiness is nearing. An agreed-upon and approved AM PPP is completed that has guided a preproduction article evaluation. When this evaluation is complete, an AM manufacturing readiness review should be conducted. This is the final gate to ensure that the process is ready and that the verified processes are in place to begin production of flight hardware. All stakeholders need to agree that the part development is complete and was successful. This review demarcates the point when changes to the AM part definition (digital files, engineering instructions, etc.) are locked. There should be no more changes beyond this point. After a successful review, the CEO designates it as a qualified part process that is documented in the QMS and is now ready for production.
9.15. AM PART PRODUCTION
At this stage, the CEO and its vendors are in full production, and maintaining documented and controlled processes becomes vital. Whether it is maintenance schedules, calibration intervals, or SPC, following the established process and executing to the plan are critical to part consistency over time. Remember that small perturbations in the AM process can result in unwanted deviations in the final part. Part reliability rationale comes from the sum of both in-process and post-process controls. Weaknesses in one area need to be compensated in the other (Fig. 9.15). AM part production for critical applications requires the established plan to be followed without any shortcuts. Any changes to the qualified part process will need to be requalified because any subtle changes that occur can affect the final material structure. In this chapter, witness testing as a form of process monitoring was only briefly discussed, but its importance cannot be understated. Efficiency in process monitoring is critical to minimizing the inevitable disruptions that occur
