in AM. Witness tests such as for HCF and tensile fatigue can take considerable time (weeks or months) to complete, depending on the requirements. That delay creates a gap in time between when a process escape can occur and when it is detected. During this time, production of substandard hardware may be ongoing and not detected. Should this occur, the AM part producer will need to flag any hardware in the system associated with failure of these tests. The costs associated with dispositioning these flags or potentially scrapping the hardware can be incredibly high. Because of this, it is best to get the turnaround time as short as possible for witness specimen test results. In addition to short turnaround times, the limits set for SPC criteria must be tight, so they can protect the design values. The sooner a performance drift is detected in the witness specimens, the sooner the issue can be corrected and the process restored to specifications before a significant quality issue arises. If the AM process drifts too far without the proper criteria, part producers will find themselves in the same situation they would be in with a time lag. They may find themselves with a collection of hardware in process that is no longer usable or in a situation where designs have to be reanalyzed to account for lower design values. An important aspect of AM part quality is that AM part producers need to emphasize the importance of inspection for every part. As discussed earlier with respect to reference parts, visual inspections can be a great indicator of as-built conditions by watching for changes in part appearance such as color, surface texture, support structure issues, and witness lines. Documentation of these is important. Quite often, changes in any of these metrics indicate a change in the process that must be understood. Each AM system is prone to occasional small issues or failures that are considered “normal”. By considering the systematic implications of all nonconformances or evidence of process drift, an AM part producer can recognize potential weaknesses in the process. When these weaknesses are understood, actions can be taken to prevent cost and schedule impacts later due to these failures. AM part production for critical applications requires the established plan to be followed without any shortcuts. Any changes to the qualified part process will need to be requalified because any subtle changes that occur can affect the final material structure.
9.16. CONCLUSION
This overview of the NASA AM certification methodology is intended to demonstrate, at a fundamental level, the primary aspects of establishing certification rationale for the implementation of AM parts. The concepts covered in this chapter are agnostic to material. This methodology has been applied to multiple additive processes and material systems with some level of tailoring. It is understood that this is one specific example of a certification framework and that many
