knowledge base. As this knowledge base improves within NASA and across the industry, NASA’s methodology for qualifying and certifying AM hardware will improve. New tools such as in situ monitoring, closed-loop control, and digital twin technology show promise to further improve knowledge and potentially streamline the certification methodology. Certifying AM hardware for crewed spaceflight requires the integration of multiple engineering disciplines. In AM, there are some differences whereby parts of the certification process also begin at the initial stages of conceptual part design. As this book described previously, many decisions made in the design stage have wide-ranging effects on how the part performs and how it will ultimately be certified. An example of such effects is highly complex parts that may not be inspectable. AM requires close coordination of all of these disciplines working together to fully optimize a part design and operations for the intended performance. When functioning in this manner, it is easy to lose track of the sensitivities and interactions of an AM process. To become proficient at all aspects of additive certification, personnel must develop a breadth of understanding spanning many or
