10.1. INTRODUCTION
The field of additive manufacturing (AM) is continuously and rapidly evolving, which makes it difficult to stay informed on the state of the art. By the time you read this book, nearly all aspects of AM will have advanced in ways that are difficult to anticipate. Although the fundamental physics remain constant and the core processes will continue to be used and matured, some advanced material, process, and supplemental developments are ongoing. Novel metal alloys and ceramics and the use of AM processes to form these materials, including alloys that were not previously printable or extremely difficult to process using AM, are some of the important developments. Other advancements include the use of complex design features, such as lattice structures to allow for weight reduction and functional tailorable properties. The advancement of AM systems is another area of growth, including the use of in situ thermal processing and rapid build rate development using diode array methods, multiple laser sources, and other methods for higher deposition rates and productivity. Supplemental modeling and simulation tools are also an important aspect of AM, allowing risk reduction to increase chances of successful builds by understanding
