10.2. FUNCTIONAL LATTICE STRUCTURES
Lattice structures comprise a series of repeating topological unit cells, usually consisting of interconnected nodes and struts. A variety of topology types or designs are available from software libraries or can be designed by the user. Topology strut thickness (t) and void space normally scale proportionally by the user-defined lattice unit cell (a), as shown in Fig. 10.1. Lattice structures are also called cellular structures, which is a broader definition that includes regular and irregular structures, essentially any porous material with “cells.” Figure 10.2 shows an optical micrograph of an AM Inconel 718 octet truss lattice with a 4 mm unit cell differentiating struts, nodes, and void space.
10.2.1. ADVANTAGES
Lattice structures are used in a number of applications to generate regions of high void space by making use of their porous nature [1]. One of the major applications is to reduce the mass while ensuring some minimum stiffness value [2]. These structures closely follow the mechanical property relationships of Ashby and Gibson, which provides effective elastic modulus values as a function of porous structure density [3, 4]. The stiffness will always be lower than that of the solid bulk material, but it can be tuned to specific ranges below this by changing the structure density. Another relevant application is in heat exchangers, because the high surface area of these structures enables high heat transfer between the solid and fluid in the void space [5, 6]. The entire void space is interconnected in lattices, resulting in high permeability and good flow properties, which can also be optimized depending on the design and density selected. The advantages of lattices are therefore widespread, and many new applications are being investigated. Physical, mechanical, thermal, and fluid properties can be tailored through the topology and design in ways that could not be achieved using the printed material alone [7].
