10.2.2. DISADVANTAGES
Lattice structures have high computational expense and large file sizes during simulations and design models. Not all topologies included in software libraries are actually printable, and software vendors do not provide guidance on which topology is printable with a specific AM process, material, or unit-cell size range. Printability must be determined experimentally, and the usable engineering properties have to be characterized for each case [2]. There are many design options for the designer. The best solution is not always clear to the designer and no clear or fixed design approach for the use of lattices has yet been established. An important disadvantage is the minimum feature size and manufacturing limits for small lattices with thin struts. Small lattices with thin struts can cause imperfect manufacturing with consequences for the structural performance. Powder removal also becomes more difficult as the lattice structure becomes smaller, and in certain circumstances, when the powder has sintered in the void space, it may be impossible to remove. Problems in manufacturability may also occur for larger lattices when near-horizontal struts become unsupported overhang surfaces. This is a situation that can induce failure and/or unacceptable downward-facing surface roughness. All of these issues may lead to lower-than-expected performance, anisotropy, and potentially other unexpected problems. Mechanical performance and especially fatigue performance have been found to be highly variable in different studies, as discussed by Benedetti et al. [8].
