Powder removal is a function of void size, which is dependent on a and t. At very small a values, powder removal can be difficult, and the powder removal methods used must be more involved to remove concerns about foreign object debris (FOD). The combination of a vacuum and compressed gas can remove gross powder trapped within the part, but care must be taken to flow gas through a part from multiple directions; otherwise, embedded pockets of powder will remain. Figure 10.11 shows an example of a test part with 1 mm wide channels containing a lattice from which the powder could not be removed. Powder must be removed from void spaces; otherwise, it will sinter during stress-relief heat treatment or clump when the powder comes into contact with cutting fluids during build-plate removal. If lattice structures are printed directly onto the build plate, wire-EDM should be used to remove the build plate; otherwise, the structures will be damaged using a band saw. As discussed in Chapter 5, higher-precision cleaning methods are required to remove loosely adherent powder from strut surfaces.
