10.2.9.2. CASE STUDY: PROPELLANT MANAGEMENT DEVICES (PMDS)
Propellant management devices (PMDs) are used to transfer fluid from a storage tank to the engine feed system. A mesh screen over the PMD inlet acts as a wick to use capillary forces to facilitate communication of the fluid in the tank under microgravity conditions. The mesh screen is normally welded or brazed onto the PMD inlet, and this welding or brazing can be difficult to do. Replacing the mesh screen with an AM lattice structure with a small value of a allows for the PMD body and wick to be printed in one piece, as shown in Fig. 10.27, eliminating the joining step. This is an area with some ongoing development efforts.
10.2.10. SUMMARY
Although substantial progress has been made in AM lattice development and testing, many areas still require maturation. For example, design topology optimization that results in lattice gradients offers potential solutions but requires additional investigation to better characterize print behavior, properties, and so on. Another area of interest is local property variations within a part to optimize the part performance. This is a field of significant potential for development and application. The main challenges at the present time include understanding and overcoming manufacturing constraints in the context of the wide choices of lattice designs. Many designs and studies have reported widely varying levels of success, especially in terms of fatigue performance. Although some fatigue-tolerant designs and manufacturing rules can be used to ensure structural integrity, the post-processing of lattices is still key to their performance, and further work is needed to fully resolve these types of concerns, ideally for each test case.
