10.3.1. AM OF REFRACTORY METALS AND ALLOYS
Refractory metals are used for service in extreme high-temperature environments. Aerospace examples of such applications include reaction control system (RCS) thrusters, nuclear thermal propulsion fuel cladding, hypersonic-wing leading edges, green propulsion chambers and catalysts, power conversion, electric propulsion, and advanced in-space propulsion such as fusion or antimatter concepts. Traditional refractory-metal manufacture is expensive because of the high cost of the feedstock, in addition to near-net-shape forming, machining, heat treatment, and waste disposal. It is not uncommon for commercially available refractory alloys to have minimum order quantities, with the cost per unit mass varying by vendor, customer use rate, the market price of raw materials, and other factors. Because refractory alloys tend to be used in primarily thin-walled designs such as converging – diverging geometries and cladding, part production routinely results in 95 – 98% of the stock being machined away, constituting of a buy-to-fly ratio of between 20:1 and 50:1. Assuming a 20:1 buy-to-fly ratio, the cost of a part is 5% in feedstock alone and 95% in machining waste, not including machining time or machining waste disposal, which is proportional to machining mass. In addition, refractory metals are relatively difficult to machine, resulting in a limited number of machine shops with the requisite equipment and experience to meet exacting aerospace specifications. Ultimately, a machined refractory-metal part can cost several thousand dollars, but the waste can be several tens of thousands of dollars. AM refractory investigations have found that the L-PBF AM of refractory metals and alloys offer significant cost and schedule savings. For example, C103 (Nb10Hf1Ti) L-PBF powder is approximately 33% more expensive than the average cost of wrought feedstock. Therefore, to generate the same part by AM, the material used to form the part will cost approximately 33% more, not including print time, heat treatment, final machining, and waste disposal (oversized powder and machining). However, unlike machining, AM allows for the desired near-net shape to be produced with minimal machining. AM waste is typically 5– 10% of the mass of the printed part, resulting from oversized powder, support structures, and sacrificial geometric features at interfaces that are machined away to meet surface finish needs. Consequently, the AM buy-to-fly ratio is approximately 1.1:1. In addition, the vast majority of AM powder may be reused for additional builds, as long as the PSD, morphology, and chemistry remain within feedstock specifications, as with any other AM powder feedstock that is recycled. Like a machined part, an AM part will also cost several thousand dollars; however, unlike for traditional manufacturing, the waste will cost a few hundred dollars, not tens of thousands. The difference in waste costs alone to produce an equivalent part constitutes an order-of-magnitude cost reduction by using AM. The cost savings; reproducibility; schedule control; and in certain cases, properties of AM parts, have
