10.3.2. DISPERSION-STRENGTHENED ALLOYS
10.3.2.1. INTRODUCTION
During melting-based (fusion) AM, thermal gradients form between the melted region and the base-temperature bulk solid material. These temperature gradients can be particularly problematic during the printing of high-melt-temperature materials such as refractory metals and materials with high coefficients of thermal expansion. A high thermal gradient or large coefficient of thermal expansion combined with low ductility can induce residual stresses that result in microcracking in fracture-prone materials or hot tearing in nonweldable materials. Nanometer-sized dispersoid particles blended with the parent metal act as heterogeneous nucleation sites in the melt pool during solidification and induce the formation of more equiaxed grains, which decrease residual stresses and can alleviate microcracking [1]. This process is known as dispersoid stabilized and strengthened AM and MMC AM. The dispersoid is normally selected using a thermodynamic and/or solidification kinetics modeling software. Candidate dispersoids must be chemically stable (have a high affinity for the metal matrix) and thermodynamically stable (have a more negative Gibbs free energy above the metal matrix solidification temperature), which normally limits candidates to ceramics such as ZrO2, ZrC, and HfC. The dispersoid must be crystalline, with one or more crystal planes with an atomic spacing close to that of the metal matrix. The dispersoid must have good spatial distribution uniformity, which is why AM is advantageous in that each metal powder particle can be decorated with ceramic nanoparticles and homogenized by processing the mixed powder through a radio-frequency plasma spheroidizer [24]. During the spherodization process, the metal particle melts, and the resulting molten metal droplet absorbs solid ceramic particles on its surface and then solidifies as it drops out of the plasma flame, locking the nanoparticles within the parent particle. This homogenization process prevents segregation during subsequent powder reuse and greatly mitigates the inhalation and pyrophoricity hazards associated with nanoparticles. It has been found that the dispersoid
