10.3.2.2. ALUMINUM REACTIVE ADDITIVE MANUFACTURING (RAM) CASE STUDY
Reactive additive manufacturing (RAM) has been demonstrated as a method for producing dispersion-strengthened alloys and MMCs and for improving the weldability and printability of alloys through the nucleation of fine, more equiaxed grain structures. In fusion-based RAM processes, precursor materials undergo thermodynamically favorable chemical reactions in situ during the fusion process to form new product phases. This versatile approach is suitable for forming a wide range of intermetallics, oxides, nitrides, borides, carbides, and other compounds [28]. The reactive approach offers many advantages compared to the use of pre-synthesized product phases. Well-distributed nanoscale dispersoids can be formed in situ from micrometer-scale precursor powders. The use of micrometer-scale feedstock constituents promotes better flowability, spreadability, and bulk density, as well as improved consolidation and part density. Larger feedstock particle sizes also offer reduced hazards for inhalation and pyrophoricity, as well as improved environmental stability. Using traditional approaches, high-melting-temperature presynthesized ceramic phases typically remain solid when processed with lower-melting-temperature metal phases. Wetting of these high-surface-area presynthesized ceramic materials can be difficult on the short time scales of typical AM processing, leading to increased porosity in the printed material. The RAM approach allows for the selection of lower-melting-temperature precursors to improve processability and printed density while still precipitating out high-melting-temperature product phases. With these processes, exothermic reactions also provide a portion of the energy
