10.3.3. IN SITU ALLOY AM
In situ alloying in AM is an experimental process with several exploratory forms in the literature. In most cases, this process involves the mixing of powders, that is, two-phase, two-component alloys that operate in a hyper- or hypoeutectic region [47 – 55]. Others have worked in tertiary alloying fields [56], cermets [57], and even MMCs, similar to ODS alloys [58]. In situ alloying demands a reaction take place during the AM process and often in current research that reaction involves the desired matrix element [59]. However, many alloys contain matrices that do not have a relatable elemental makeup, which introduces a new level of complexity into in situ alloying. NASA Glenn Research Center worked with Case Western Reserve University to develop processes for the in situ alloying of GRCop-42. GRCop-42, a Cu-based alloy containing 4 at.% Cr and 2 at.% Nb, has a nearly pure Cu matrix with Cr2Nb precipitates [60]. This leads to the challenge of printing with an element that is not involved in the reaction taking place. Figure 10.33 shows a partially reacted particle in a polished sample of in situ – alloyed GRCop-42. The energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) map on the right shows the particles of elemental Cr and Nb clearly, with a region of reacted Cr2Nb
