10.3.4.1. VAT PHOTOPOLYMERIZATION
Stereolithography (SLA) vat photopolymerization is an indirect AM method in which a reservoir or vat is filled with a photosensitive resin mixed with about 50 vol% solid [65, 66, 69]. The build plate is lowered into the ceramic slurry at a specific distance from the optically transparent reservoir window. A low-power UV laser scans across the build plate, causing the photosensitive resin to polymerize (solidify) and adhere to the build plate, which also locks the ceramic particles in place [65, 66]. UV photopolymerization works best with light-colored powders that exhibit low UV absorption such as SiO2, SiC, Al2O3, ZnO, and ZrO2 [65, 66]. Even with low-absorption powders, layer thickness must be relatively thin to print, which forces the use of small PSD for the energy density to be sufficient to induce polymerization. Dark particles suffer from higher UV absorption of the incident UV radiation, meaning that the layer thickness must be very thin (1 – 10 mm); otherwise, too much UV energy will be parasitically absorbed by the particles and not available to polymerize the resin. The thin layers result in very long build times, during which the slurry also experiences sedimentation, with particles settling to the bottom of the slurry vat making for a nonuniform particle distribution. The resin density is approximately 1 g/cm3, and generally, powders with densities of 1– 5 g/cm3 have an acceptable powder/ resin density ratio. A few examples of common ceramics used in AM include SiO2 (2.65 g/cm3),
Al2O3 (3.95 g/cm3), and ZrO2 (5.68 g/cm3) [65 – 69]. The green parts (as-built, nonbonded) are then washed in an alcohol bath to remove excess resin prior to debinding in a furnace to drive off the binder. Parts then undergo sintering in a furnace for densification and experience anisotropic shrinkage of approximately 15% in the X and Y dimensions and 27% in Z [70]. The ceramic materials used are also chosen because they can achieve densities on the order of 95 %RD with pressureless sintering. Examples of vendors offering laser vat photopolymerization platforms include 3DCeram and Formlabs [65]. Ceramic parts can now be made with much higher levels of complexity (see Fig. 10.34) than were previously attainable with traditional ceramic part manufacture. Ceramic AM parts can also be produced using direct light projection (DLP) vat photopolymerization, which is very similar to SLA but uses a different energy source to cure the resin [71]. Vendors offering DLP vat photopolymerization platforms include Lithoz, whose platforms have been utilized to print sacrificial ceramic turbine blade cores used in directionally solidified investment casting of single-crystal turbine blades, as shown in Fig. 10.35 [65, 71].
