Material extrusion (ME) or fused filament fabrication (FFF) is similar to the fused deposition modeling (FDM) common with plastic printers. Wax or polymeric binder rods impregnated with metal powder are heated and extruded through a nozzle, after which they are deposited onto a heated surface with a plastic film. One nozzle is for the build material, and the other deposits the support material. The debinding of green parts is done by immersion in a fluid to dissolve the binder. Debinded parts are placed into a furnace and sintered [66]. ME vendors include Markforged and Desktop Metal [75, 76].
10.3.4.5. MATERIAL JETTING
An ink-jet-style print head distributes liquid binder loaded with nanoparticles in suspension onto a substrate. One jet head deposits liquid build material, whereas the other deposits liquid support material. The build plate is heated, which evaporates the liquid on contact, leaving behind the nanoparticles that make highdensity green parts. Considering the two jet heads, use of multiple materials is a possibility, but densification could be difficult because of differences in material sintering temperatures. Very fine geometric features can be built from ceramic materials because of the 10 –15 mm layer thickness and fine voxel size (1500 dpi). Supports are dissolved away in a warm water/alcohol bath. The process is well-suited for small detailed parts but is likely limited because the low deposition rate results in long build times. Custom feedstock formulations are limited because the process is often proprietary, particles with high density can induce sedimentation of the suspension, spent liquid must be discarded, and parts experience isotropic shrinkage of about 16 vol% from a green to a brown part with 99.9 %RD. Anisotropic shrinkage of ceramic AM parts can be up to 30% in the Z direction as a result of burn-out and sintering, again with as-built parts scaled to compensate for the geometric variation. XJet offers material jetting AM platforms [77, 78].
