10.5. IN SITU PROCESS MONITORING
In situ process monitoring refers to any technology that monitors an AM process. The range of technologies is as broad as the range of NDE methods and can also include machine health readouts more closely associated with traditional process control. Voltage, current, and pressure sensors can be used to detect if something abnormal occurs in the regular operations of the machine, including the ancillary systems, laser or arc, wire feed, or powder recoating processes. Many AM machines have some of these sensors built in, and others can be added. If something abnormal occurs, that area of the machine can be investigated visually, by NDE, or by checking the data streams from other process monitoring technologies. Other technologies monitor the material deposition itself. For metal AM systems, melt-pool monitoring and optical or thermal tomography are common. These systems include on-axis and off-axis photodiodes, visible and near-infrared cameras, and video cameras. There are a few potential applications of these data. Thermal modeling of the AM process can be useful for understanding the thermal history of a part and predicting its microstructure, properties, and residual stress distributions. Melt-pool monitoring can contribute real-time information about the melt-pool emission and geometry. Abnormal melt-pool signatures can also be a good indicator of a change in the process that may result in a part anomaly. This can be used to guide focused NDE. Attempts have also been made to correlate melt-pool monitoring data with computed tomography data to understand whether melt-pool monitoring indications can reliably predict material defects. Some other technologies inspect the deposited material between build layers. This can include imaging the recoated layer in L-PBF. Most of these technologies are camera-based. Some use machine vision to automatically detect abnormalities in the deposited material. Others use laser line profilometry to scan the build in
