10.5.1. OPPORTUNITY FOR IN SITU PROCESS MONITORING
One of the major challenges identified in the discussion of NDE (Chapter 5) was the as-built complexity of AM parts. When a traditional subtractive manufacturing process is used, a part can inspected between each machining step. The very complex geometries that make AM a good value proposition also make inspections challenging. This challenge can become an opportunity when considering the layerwise assembly of AM. If a part can be inspected between build layers, this can enable an even better look at its internal structures and any potential defects that may form.
10.5.2. LIMITATIONS OF IN SITU PROCESS MONITORING
The main limitation of in situ monitoring is temporal. Even when NDE technologies are used in situ, the characterization of the already-deposited material cannot take into effect how that material may change as the result of subsequent deposition. Therefore, the finished part could include cracks or other defects that were not captured by in situ monitoring data. Another major limitation is that inspections between layers take time, which can add up to a large delay over the course of the entire build. The most time-efficient in situ monitoring systems monitor the build in-process, but it is not yet readily apparent how well these signals correspond to defects in the finished part.
10.5.3. DISTINCT FUNCTIONS
To differentiate its capabilities, in situ monitoring data can be grouped into different use cases, with different requirements for how the data are processed, stored, interpreted, validated, and used.
