10.5.3.1. PROCESS MONITORING
Using in situ monitoring for process monitoring is the standard approach. The data can be processed to give real-time warning of unusual signatures, which could help inform a decision to stop or repair a build. The data can also be recorded and referenced later as needed. This approach is valuable for understanding the build process better and can help guide targeted NDE, but it does not attempt to use the signals as quantitative indicators of part quality.
10.5.3.2. PART QUALITY
Using in situ monitoring as a quantitative indicator of part quality requires a known causal correlation between the monitoring data and actual defects in a finished part. Such a correlation would need to be established by building a statistically significant number of samples with defects and correlating the data sets. This enables the use of in situ process monitoring data as a supplement to the quantitative part qualification information acquired using NDE.
10.5.3.3. CLOSED-LOOP CONTROL
For many people, the ultimate goal of in situ process monitoring is the ability to monitor a build, detect and interpret anomalies, and automatically implement corrective actions to heal the defect and improve machine parameters. This requires the same known causal correlation of monitoring data to material defects as mentioned in the preceding section, with the added step of understanding how changing machine parameters can heal the defect or improve the process. Some certification frameworks, such as NASA’s, require a locked build process, so certifying an adaptive process would require a new approach.
10.5.4. ROLE IN CERTIFICATION
Different types of in situ monitoring provide insight into different aspects of the build process. These data can be used in many different ways to help support the certification process. Examples given in the following sections use terminology from the NASA certification standard [88], but these same principles could be used in any certification approach, as discussed in Chapter 9.
