10.7. BUILD SIMULATION AND MODELING
As metal AM continues to grow in popularity, the complexity and scale of components also increases, providing more build challenges. The complexity can cause build failures and result in extra cost or machine time that was unanticipated. As the scale of components increases, the build durations can exceed many months, so failures late in the process are especially detrimental. Modeling and simulation are emerging technology areas that can provide insights into laserbased metal AM processes such as L-PBF and LP-DED. Although simulation and modeling tools have been evaluated since the early days of AM, the efficiency and accuracy of these build simulations have advanced and will continue to become more aligned with the process. Many current metal AM build operations may require many developmental builds to dial in parameters and approach for the component to be successful and meet intended requirements. The main objective with these tools is to help reduce overall process costs and allow for components to be fabricated on the first attempt. Both L-PBF and LP-DED AM result in repeated heating and cooling of the layers during printing. Thermal cycling results in a buildup of residual stress that may lead to distortion and cracking in the final parts. Residual stresses are one of the most significant challenges for metal AM. Additionally, cutting the supports in L-PBF or excess stock in LP-DED may also result in adding more stress. Residual stresses can be mitigated through choices in design, process parameters, and heat treatment. Selection of the proper process parameters is an iterative process that is constrained to selecting an energy density range that will provide the maximum build density with the minimum residual stress. Predictive simulation models with acceptable accuracy can reduce the number of tests required to mitigate stress with minimal trial-and-error iterations to obtain an optimized build process. Predictive simulation and modeling software for AM has matured rapidly in the past few years. The U.S. Department of Energy has several laboratories developing predictive software suitable for AM. Tools include Idaho National Laboratory’s MOOSE, Los Alamos National Laboratory’s Tusas [107] for microstructural modeling and Truchas [108] for multiphysics, Lawrence Livermore National
