The workflow steps for part-level residual stress consist of defining the material properties (such as thermal dependent curves, melting points, mechanical moduli) and the geometry of the part (and supports) to be analyzed; discretizing the geometry into a mesh; assigning the material domains; and if available, defining the activation elements. The material domain assignments may vary with the step of the additive processing if material is added per layer. The “Activation Elements” box represents having “birth/death” elements or the ability to turn elements on or off depending on the additive layer. This is also critical as metal AM evolves and unique parameters are used locally to optimize component features during the build and subsequent properties. The activation of elements may vary substantially between simulation codes because of variations in implementation. Once these definitions have been made, the physics equations to be solved are introduced. In most cases, the equations represent the thermal physics of the layer heating and the structural mechanics of the materials. The physics consists of defining the boundary conditions (e.g., base-plate heating); setting up the initial conditions (e.g., initial temperature); and defining sources (e.g., laser spot heating), sinks (convection), and material responses. The material responses include phase changes associated with melting and evaporation, as well as elasticity and plasticity in the solidifying metal. The mechanical and thermophysical material properties are one area requiring further maturity, as some of the physical properties of the melt pool will reduce uncertainty in models. Once the equations have been established, the solution method is specified. The thermal physics can be solved with a time-domain solver to replicate the laser-scan heating of the part. Once the thermal solution has been obtained, the structural mechanics can be solved statically using the thermal history. For some codes, it is better to solve the coupled system, in which case the thermal and structural mechanics are solved together. Finally, the last step is to output the desired solution space, such as the distortion in the final part and stress tensors. Not listed in the modeling process diagram is the design process such as planning support structures and generating printable code to avoid errant recoater blade strikes. An optimization process could encompass this physics model process. Single-track L-PBF experiments using Inconel 625 were performed in which the melt-pool dimensions were measured for various process parameters [107]. NASA developed COMSOL multiphysics models of the thermal-fluid physics for L-PBF and compared the predictions [107]. The results of this thermal-fluid model are depicted in Fig. 10.52. The resultant melt-pool shapes are shown in the last row. These melt-pool shapes can then be used to determine equivalent heat-source models for a thermal-structural model. Typically, a double-ellipsoidal Goldak model can be fitted to these shapes so that thermal-structural analyses can be conducted without the computational expense of fluid dynamic simulations [112].
