The types of possible inquiries and associated physics vary with scale. The workflow illustrated in Fig. 10.51 is for whole-part residual stress analysis. As shown in Table 10.2, different modeling methods are used for different scales. When a new alloy with a unique ratio of elements is considered, the material properties necessary for successful simulation may not be known. Different scales of study may require different material knowledge. For example, melt-pool physics requires knowledge of how the specific heat, thermal conductivity, density, recoil pressure, viscosity, and surface tension vary with temperature. If heating is occurring as a result of laser application, it is important to also know the optical properties of the powder and liquid metal, such as the spectral absorptivity and emissivity at the specific laser wavelength. For whole-part stress analyses, it is necessary to understand the thermal gradients, expansion coefficient, and mechanical moduli of the material. Many of these properties can be estimated from laboratory measurements, and some of these properties can be determined using ab initio simulation methods. Some of these properties are difficult to obtain at the melting temperatures of the alloys and require more maturation. Analyses related to metal grain boundaries and material properties can be performed on the scale of nanometers using ab initio methods, molecular dynamics, density functional theory, and so on. When a new ratio of elements within an alloy are explored, properties such as the temperature dependent viscosity and surface tension, which can have significant impacts on the weld-bead shape, can be determined with nanoscale methods. Simulations at the micrometer scale can be used to address expected melt-pool dimensions and weld penetration depths as a function of process parameters such as laser power and speed. The micrometer-scale physics can model melt-pool fluid flow (such as Marangoni convection),
