10.7.3. MEASUREMENT METHODS
Although models can be used to simulate a build, they require continuous validation to increase accuracy related to component build. This validation primarily focuses on the resultant residual stresses. There are many methods for measuring stresses in as-built metal AM parts. For this example, planar stress measurements made with XRD were used both for surface stress and for depth studies. The depth studies can be accomplished by using electropolishing to etch into the part. It is also possible to perform structured light scanning to assess the shape of the final geometry so that predicted deformation can be compared between the part and the simulation. Neutron diffraction is another method for strain measurement, but it requires highly specialized facilities and methods. Hole drilling is an older method that uses the shapes of drilled holes to determine stress. The disadvantage to hole drilling is that it cannot easily and accurately measure stresses as a function of depth. Electronic speckle-pattern interferometry (ESPI) [113, 114] infers part distortion and strain/stress using a laser to illuminate a random speckle pattern before and after relieving stress (e.g., hole drilling). Displacement resulting from hot-wire cutting can be related to the stress for certain geometries. For example, cutting the supports off one end from a base plate on a twincantilever beam results in warpage.
