10.7.5. SIMULATION SUMMARY
Multiphysics simulations of metal AM, with appropriate approximations, can guide build and design decisions and reduce the amount of time and expense associated with a trial-and-error approach to selecting process parameters. As more efficient tools become available and the sophistication of approximation methods matures, additive simulation tools will allow for complex design trade spaces to be explored in an automated fashion. This advancement, when combined with the exponentially increasing field of artificial intelligence, such as physics-informed neural networks [117], will yield designs of new alloys and parts that are essentially out of reach with current practice. Modeling and simulations will continue to be synonymous with metal AM as processing capabilities are advanced and accuracy is increased.
10.8. CONCLUSION
The AM field is dynamic and in a state of rapid development and maturation. This chapter attempted to cover ongoing developments within the AM industry, including novel designs, modeling and simulation, feedstock, AM methods, AM/non-AM hybrid methods, materials, and process monitoring. It is intended that this chapter provide the reader with an understanding of the broad array of developing aspects of AM with high potential. Due to the accelerating pace and
